\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEATH TOLL RISES IN CHEMICAL TANK DISASTER RESIDENTS FEAR CONTAMINATION FROM TOXIC LIQUID SPILL\b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STRIKES\par \par \uc2 US, IRAN, AIRSTRIKES, GAS, STRAIT, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, STORM, RAIN\par \par \uc2 CHEMICAL, TANK, GOLDSTEIN, MOLDENHAUER\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot happening across the country, Ginger, thank you. Now, the chemical tanker rupture at a Washington state paper plant, the death toll rising. Two people confirmed dead and the nine missing workers presumed dead. Melissa Adan is on the scene for us there in Longview, Washington. Good morning to you, Melissa.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES IN CHEMICAL TANK DISASTER\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ADAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Robin. This is a tight-knit community here where everyone knows someone directly impacted by this tragedy or for people that work in nearby factories, as now this community is gripping with that somber news, that those nine employees that are still missing here are presumed dead.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ADAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the death toll rising as crews work to recover the nine employees still missing after this deadly chemical tank rupture at a Washington state paper mill.\par \par \uc2 FIRE CHIEF SCOTT GOLDSTEIN (COWLITZ 2 FIRE & RESCUE)\par \par \uc2 There are members working the site tirelessly that have lost coworkers, lost friends, and they remain dedicated to focusing on our recovery efforts.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ADAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And now we're getting a closer look at the devastation. Images from inside show a pile of contaminated gear roped off, all of it set to be destroyed. These fire boots covered in so-called white liquor, the toxic substance used in the papermaking process. More than 550 thousand gallons of it spilled out when the tank ruptured early Tuesday. Two people have died. The nine workers still missing are now presumed dead.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ADAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is close to home.\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL MOLDENHAUER (RESIDENT LONGVIEW WASHINGTON)\par \par \uc2 It's my biggest fear. And now my friends are, are living this fear.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ADAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Officials say the damaged tank is still holding thousands of gallons of hazardous material, but remain stable.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ADAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The \uc2 EPA\uc2  is now here testing the water and air quality for any possible contamination. Officials do say, though, that the local drinking water supply has not been impacted. But now there's a federal investigation underway. And George, the community members tell me they want to ensure that something like this never happens again. George?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260610em5s0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD STRIKE ENDS DEAL REACHED TO END RAIL STRIKE IN NEW YORK\b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WAHL, ISLAMIC CENTER, SHANAH, HASSANE, ABDULLAH, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, FORECAST, STORMS, TORNADOES, WILDFIRES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORNADOES TEAR ACROSS THE MIDWEST\par \par \uc2 HEAT, FORECAST, MEMORIAL DAY, FORECAST\par \par \uc2 EBOLA, STAFFORD, GERMANY, PILLAI, SPENCER, RISK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN TESTS POSITIVE FOR EBOLA\par \par \uc2 RATCLIFFE, SLASH FUND, TRUMP, IRAN, WAR, ATTACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUSTICE DEPARTMENT CREATES $1.8 BILLION DOLLAR FUND\par \par \uc2 GAS PRICES, MEMORIAL DAY, TRAVEL, IRAN, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAS PRICES HIGH AHEAD OF MEMORIAL DAY\par \par \uc2 LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD, \uc2 MTA\uc2 , DEAL, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD STRIKE ENDS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to go now to breaking news. Overnight, a deal was reached to end the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  strike that affected hundreds of thousands of commuters in New York. Stephanie Ramos joins us now with the latest on that. A lot of happy people out there this morning, Stephanie. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, absolutely, Michael. Hey there. Yeah, this morning, hundreds of thousands of commuters here in New York City are breathing a sigh of relief. Negotiators have reached a deal to end the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  strike that shut down North America's busiest passenger railway.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEAL REACHED TO END RAIL STRIKE IN NEW YORK\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The agreement between the \uc2 MTA\uc2  and union leaders was announced Monday night and took effect at midnight after years of contract talks and months of intense negotiations over pay and health care costs for about half of the LIRR workforce, including engineers, signalmen and trainmen. Officials say train service will resume at noon with full service expected in time for the evening rush. And, as Governor Hochul pointed out, just in time for the \uc2 Knicks\uc2  game here at the Garden. Guys?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260609em5j00030\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MAJOR RAIL STRIKE CAUSING MASS CHAOS MAJOR DISRUPTION FOR NEW YORK AREA COMMUTERS\b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAIL STRIKE\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, RAIL, CHAOS, HOCHUL, COMMUTERS, SEXTON\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we're going to begin with the travel chaos for hundreds of thousands of people this morning, the Long Island railroad strike is entering its third day, but it's the first of the workweek. Stephanie Ramos is at Penn Station here in New York with the latest for us. Good morning, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR RAIL STRIKE CAUSING MAJOR CHAOS\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Michael, good morning. As you said this morning, the Long Island railroad strike is entering day three. This is an expansive system here that the Long Island railroad has. It operates 24/7, across 126 stations for more than 700 miles. This strike this morning is causing major disruptions for about 300,000 thousand New Yorkers.\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Contract.\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Right now.\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 If we don't get it.\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Shut it down.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the Long Island railroad strike entering day three, upending the start of the workweek for nearly 300,000 commuters.\par \par \uc2 COMMUTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 No one knows what to do.\par \par \uc2 COMMUTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 How am I going to get back to the Bronx from Long Island?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The LIRR has been shut down since early Saturday morning after months of contract talks between the MTA and a coalition of five unions collapsed.\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Union power.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Leading some 3,500 workers to walk off the job.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR DISRUPTION FOR NEW YORKERS COMMUTERS\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 I value your labor, and I believe that you deserve fair wages and benefits. But also New Yorkers deserve better.\par \par \uc2 MTA SPOKESPERSON (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Our last offer literally gave the unions everything they said they had wanted in terms of pay, but they rejected even that.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The two sides remain at odds over pay raises, reportedly between 4.5% and 5%, as well as health care costs.\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We want to be out running the trains.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN SEXTON (UNION LEADER)\par \par \uc2 We're just looking for an agreement that ensures our members standard of living.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Talks between the MTA and union leaders are set to resume this morning. Hopefully they can make a deal, Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5i0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SOURCES: PRESIDENT NEAR DEAL ON FUND TO PAY POLITICAL ALLIES ETHICS QUESTIONS OVER PRESIDENT'S PROPOSED FUND\b0\par\par\uc2 595 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAIL STRIKE\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, RAIL, CHAOS, HOCHUL, COMMUTERS, SEXTON\par \par \uc2 LIRR\uc2 , STRIKE, COMMUTERS, CHAOS\par \par \uc2 GAS, OIL, PRICES, IRAN, HOLIDAY, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 AIR SHOW, JETS, COLLIDE, PARACHUTES, VIALPANDO\par \par \uc2 STORMS, WINDS, TORNADO, DAMAGE, RESCUES, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADOES, THREATS, CELLS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IRAN, CEASEFIRE THREATS, URANIUM\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING SPREE, ARRESTS, INJURED, RANDOM\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , FUND, ALLIES, CONGRESS, DEMS, GOP, TAXPAYERS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Trevor Ault, thanks. The latest now on President Trump's plan to settle his $10 billion lawsuit against the \uc2 IRS\uc2  by creating a nearly $2 billion fund for payments to political allies like the January 6th rioters. The funds will come from taxpayers, and the proposal is raising legal and ethical concerns from Democrats and Republicans. Senior political correspondent Rachel Scott is tracking the story. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: PRESIDENT NEAR DEAL ON FUND TO PAY POLITICAL ALLIES\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning to you. And sources say that this deal is now being finalized, setting the stage for an extraordinary use of taxpayer money to benefit the President's allies.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, sources say the President is one step closer to creating a new $1.7 billion fund of taxpayer money to pay his allies. ABC News learning the President is expected to settle a $10 billion lawsuit he brought against his own government as early as today. Sources say as part of the deal, the President would drop the suit in exchange for a commission to pay Trump allies who claim they were mistreated by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One group that could benefit from that, the rioters who stormed the Capitol on January 6th. The President sued the \uc2 IRS\uc2  earlier this year, claiming damages from the leak of his tax returns in 2019. He has also sued his own justice department for $230 million for investigations he faced during the Biden administration. An American President has never sued his own administration, something the President acknowledged last fall.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's interesting because I'm the one that makes the decision, right. And, you know, that decision would have to go across my desk.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sources say the President would have control over who runs the commission and the authority to remove members without cause, and that the commission would not be required to disclose to the American people how it runs or its decision making process. Most Republicans staying silent, but some moderates shocked.\par \par \uc2 REP \uc2 BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (REP) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 I don't even know how that's allowable to happen.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats calling it an abuse of power.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS ETHICS QUESTIONS OVER PRESIDENT'S PROPOSED FUND\par \par \uc2 REP ALEXANDRIA OCASIO CORTEZ (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 It's outright corruption. What we're seeing here is outright corruption.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMIE RASKIN (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 Only Congress has the power to appropriate money, and Congress never voted on creating this $1.7 billion dollars political slush fund at the Department of Justice. And Congress would never pass that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So sources caution that the details of this plan are not finalized until publicly announced. In a statement, the President's legal team says that the President continues to hold those who wrong America accountable. The Justice Department declined to comment. George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question this will head to the courts. Okay, Rachel, thanks very much. Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5i0005u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAKING AT 8:00\b0\par\par\uc2 1,422 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAIL STRIKE\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, RAIL, CHAOS, HOCHUL, COMMUTERS, SEXTON\par \par \uc2 LIRR\uc2 , STRIKE, COMMUTERS, CHAOS\par \par \uc2 GAS, OIL, PRICES, IRAN, HOLIDAY, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 AIR SHOW, JETS, COLLIDE, PARACHUTES, VIALPANDO\par \par \uc2 STORMS, WINDS, TORNADO, DAMAGE, RESCUES, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADOES, THREATS, CELLS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IRAN, CEASEFIRE THREATS, URANIUM\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING SPREE, ARRESTS, INJURED, RANDOM\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , FUND, ALLIES, CONGRESS, DEMS, GOP, TAXPAYERS\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS, CAVALIERS, \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , SGA, MVP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE WEATHER DANGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 KEPNER, GRADUATION, MURDERED, HEARTBREAK, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 AVALANCHE, MT EVEREST, FORAN, SURVIVE, GREENE\par \par \uc2 SWATCH, COLLAB, LUXURY, FRENZY, POLICE, SHOPPERS\par \par \uc2 HOCKEY, PLAYOFFS, \uc2 SABRES\uc2 , \uc2 CANADIENS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, STORM, DROWNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA: BREAKING AT 8:00\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR COMMUTER RAIL STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORM THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DROWNING PREVENTION\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) First, we go to the top stories breaking at 8:00. We start with the travel trouble for hundreds of thousands of people this morning. Long Island Railroad strike is entering its third day. First of the workweek. Stephanie Ramos is at Penn Station here in New York. Good morning, Stephanie\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR RAIL STRIKE CAUSING MASS CHAOS\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, George. As you said this morning, the Long Island Railroad strike is entering day three. Take a look behind me. There are plenty of workers here at Penn Station picketing. This strike is really upending the start of the workweek for nearly 300,000 New Yorkers that rely on this expansive system. The \uc2 LIRR\uc2  has been shut down since early Saturday morning after months of contract talks between the MTA and a coalition of five unions collapsed, leaving some 3,500 workers to walk off the job. The two sides remain at odds over pay raises, reportedly between 4.5 and 5 percent, as well as health care costs.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Picket lines now stretching from here at Penn Station through Queens and across Long Island as frustration grows on both sides. Talks between the MTA and union leaders are set to resume today, and hopefully they can make a deal, guys.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORM THREAT\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we definitely hoping that happens Stephanie, and help a lot of commuters out there. Thank you so much. We're going to turn now to millions of Americans who are under storm threat this morning, with some parts of the heartland bracing for possible tornadoes and some intense heat is on the way for so many out there. Ginger is tracking the latest.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESTRUCTIVE STORMS LIKELY TODAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAINT LIBORY, NEBRASKA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA: SEVERE WEATHER OUTLOOK, MONDAY\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, Michael, good morning to you. So, Saint Libory, Nebraska is where we saw this violent tornado ripping across the land just last night. In the last 25 hours, 24 hours, we've had at least 25 reported tornadoes. This one did damage not just in Saint Libory, where we can see the homes there shredded and moved from their foundation. But other places in Nebraska also saw this all the way to Iowa. Today, the risk area is pretty broad from all of Michigan to Chicago down to Saint Louis, back to Wichita Falls, Texas, and that Red River Valley. But this area, level four out of five for mostly Eastern Kansas, meaning the second highest level there is. And tornadoes are a major risk.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA: TORNADO RISK AREAS, MONDAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA: SEVERE WEATHER OUTLOOK, TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA: HIGH TEMPERATURES, MONDAY\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So Manhattan, Wichita, Salina, Auburn, Nebraska, you are in that highest level. It's not just tornadoes, though. We could see big hail. We're talking two to three inch hail. I'm headed out to this area today. But tomorrow we've got a whole 'nother region from Abilene and Dallas up to Cleveland, Rochester, New York, and Burlington. Damaging wind by tomorrow is the main hazard. Then we have to talk about heat. One big reason we're having these severe storms in between the middle of the country is because we've got record high lows or, or excuse me, low highs in Rapid City. And then Syracuse could break a record today at 92. Boston had their first 90 yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA: HIGH TEMPERATURES, TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA: HIGH TEMPERATURES, WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We could easily see our first 90 tomorrow and stick around with it because even Portland, Maine will get into the upper 80s as we head through Tuesday. And of course, that cold eventually does make it here. Robin.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DROWNING PREVENTION\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes it does. All right, Ginger, thank you. We have the unofficial start of summer, less than a week away and new guidance this morning from the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  on how to prevent drowning. Hanna Battah is back with that. Good morning again, Hanna.\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, good morning. It's an important reminder.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DROWNING PREVENTION GUIDELINES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "PREVENTION OF DROWNING" RECOMMENDATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEAR COAST GUARD-APPROVED LIFE JACKETS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DO NOT RELY ON "FLOATIES"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEGIN SWIM LESSONS AFTER AGE 1\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO KEEP CHILDREN SAFE IN WATER\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Especially this time of year, as drowning is the leading cause of death among American children ages one to four. And this morning, the American Academy of Pediatrics is announcing revised guidance to prevent drowning. It was last updated in 2019. Since the pandemic, drowning has increased among children and even young adults. The guidance goes beyond just swimming pools, as much of those drowning increases have come in natural bodies of water like ponds, lakes and oceans. Among the recommendations, children and people who cannot swim, should wear Coast Guard approved life jackets when they are around the water or on boats.\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The statement advises parents not to rely on inflatable armbands or neck rings, commonly called floaties, because they can deflate and then offer little protection. It also suggests that they enter their children in swimming lessons after the age of one, saying those infant floating programs haven't been shown to reduce drowning rates. Bottom line, parents need to be vigilant when their children are around the water. Always stay within arm's reach of them. And one more suggestion from the statement, stay off your phone.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Even a moment's distraction could lead to tragedy. And we hear it all too often.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And watch them.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know, and I'm glad you, you mentioned it's not just pools.\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's natural bodies of water as well.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sometimes we forget that.\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, more and more each year.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Hanna, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:11 MICROPLASTICS IN OUR BODIES\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up on our "GMA Morning Menu," microplastics in our bodies. New studies on how much can be in our systems even before we're born. And what you can do to help reduce exposure to them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:15 POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also ahead new first for Tom Brady. That's in "Pop News."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:45 "RIVALS" CAST LIVE!\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the cast of the award winning series "Rivals" is here to tell us about season two. And Lara's with a special guest. Hey Lara.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:30 BROOKE SHIELDS LIVE!\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So special, Brooke Shields, everybody, is in the house and she'll tell us all about solving mysteries on her new show and how she handles the gen xers, gen z divide, on the show and in real life. We'll talk about that coming up on "GMA" when we come back. So happy you're here.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE SHIELDS (ACTRESS "YOU'RE KILLING ME")\par \par \uc2 Happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Would you do us the honor?\par \par \uc2 BROOKE SHIELDS (ACTRESS "YOU'RE KILLING ME")\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC GOOD MORNING AMERICA, MONDAY, MAY 18\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5i00064\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SOURCES: PRES. TRUMP WORKING ON FUND TO PAY POLITICAL ALLIES TRUMP EXPECTED TO DROP LAWSUIT AGAINST IRS & CREATE A "COMMISSION"\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIRR STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFF THE RAILS\par \par \uc2 LIRR, STRIKE, HOCHUL, TRUMP, MTA, LIEBER, SEXTON\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, FORECAST, HAIL, TORNADOES, STORMS\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY, PRIMARY ELECTIONS, TRUMP, MASSIE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN. CASSIDY LOSES BID FOR THIRD TERM IN LOUISIANA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TAIWAN, XI, ARMS DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAIWAN PRESSES FOR $14 BILLION U.S. ARMS DEAL\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ARRESTS, IMMIGRATION, PALESTINE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , SLUSH FUND, \uc2 IRS\uc2 , FITZPATRICK, RASKIN, CORTEZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: PRES. TRUMP WORKING ON FUND TO PAY POLITICAL ALLIES\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now we turn to a legal move that could put cash in the hands of President Trump's political supporters. ABC's Katherine Faulders is in Washington with more on this story. Good morning, Katherine.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE FAULDERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Rhiannon. Despite the backlash from Democrats and even some Republicans, I'm told by sources, that President Trump is expected to make this arrangement public as soon as tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE FAULDERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, sources tell ABC News President Trump is in the final stages of negotiating a deal with his own government over a $10 billion dollars lawsuit he brought against the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT'S $10 BILLION LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE FAULDERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President is expected to drop the suit as soon as Monday in exchange for the creation of a so-called the Truth & Justice Commission, which would oversee a fund of more than $1.7 billion dollars to pay the President's allies. That means those who stormed the US Capitol on January 6th could benefit, along with others who claim they were treated unfairly by what Trump calls a weaponized Biden Justice Department. Trump sued the \uc2 IRS\uc2  earlier this year after a tax contractor leaked some of his tax records years ago. Trump also filed $230 million in claims against the Department of Justice for investigating him. A President suing their own government is without precedent, something Trump himself has acknowledged.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's interesting because I'm the one who makes the decision, right? And, you know, that decision would have to go across my desk. And it's awfully strange to make a decision where I'm paying myself.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE FAULDERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The extraordinary use of taxpayer money would be shrouded in secrecy. Sources tell ABC News that, under the terms of the potential agreement, which sources caution are not final until publicly announced, Trump would have the authority to remove members of the commission running the fund without cause, and the commission would be and the commission would be under no obligation to disclose its procedures or decision-making process. The move even alarmed some Republicans.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (REP) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 I don't even know how that's allowable to happen.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE FAULDERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats saying Trump is creating a political slush fund that is utterly fraudulent.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's outright corruption. What we're seeing here is outright corruption.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is setting up a $1.7 billion political slush fund for the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers and his other political lieutenants and hangers on.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE FAULDERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, in response to our reporting, a spokesperson for the Trump legal team said that the President continues to hold those who wrong America and Americans accountable, but still no word from the President himself or the Justice Department. And, guys, we really are an unprecedented situation here where President Trump is suing the government he runs. This is something the judge overseeing the case has raised serious concerns about, questioning how the parties involved here are actually different. So, this will certainly face legal challenges in some form. But the question is, can the judge really do anything here to stop this from moving forward? Many legal experts say probably not, at least not immediately. But we'll have to see. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that is the question. We haven't really seen anything like this before. Katherine Faulders for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260520em5h0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LONG ISLAND RAIL\uc2  ROAD STRIKE ENTERS DAY TWO MAJOR COMMUTER RAIL STRIKE THREATENS START OF WORK WEEK\b0\par\par\uc2 705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIRR STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFF THE RAILS\par \par \uc2 LIRR\uc2 , STRIKE, HOCHUL, TRUMP, \uc2 MTA\uc2 , LIEBER, SEXTON\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we do begin here with the strike on the nation's busiest commuter rail.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRIKE IN EFFECT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The walkout now entering its second day. And the clock is ticking ahead of the busy Monday morning commute. ABC's Morgan Norwood is at New York's Penn Station with the latest. Morgan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD STRIKE ENTERS DAY TWO\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, Whit. And you can see these workers back on the picket lines for day two of this strike. And this morning, there is growing concern for that Monday morning commute, which could turn out to be costly and chaotic for the nearly 250,000 riders who depend on the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  to get in and out of New York City.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR COMMUTER RAIL STRIKE THREATENS START OF WORK WEEK\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, day two of a massive rail strike now threatening to upend the start of the workweek for hundreds of thousands of New York commuters. The \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , the busiest commuter rail system in North America, now shut down, leaving some 250,000 daily riders scrambling for a way into New York City.\par \par \uc2 COMMUTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Give them what they want and let them get back to work.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The walkout, starting just after midnight Saturday after months of negotiations between the \uc2 MTA\uc2  and unions representing some 3,500 rail workers collapsed.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN SEXTON (NATL VP BROTHERHOOD OF LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 We're just looking for an agreement that ensures our members standard of living. That's all this is about.\par \par \uc2 JANNO LIEBER\uc2  (CHAIR & CEO METROPOLITAN TRANSPORTATION AUTHORITY)\par \par \uc2 Our last offer literally gave the unions everything they said they had wanted in terms of pay, but they rejected even that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEGOTIATIONS\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The main sticking points pay raises between 4.5% and 5%, along with health care costs.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But you guys are prepared to hold out for not just days, but months.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes. If we have to, yes. We have no choice. We have no choice. It's, for us now, it's about survival. We waited four years. Our backs are against the wall. It's about survival.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, with both sides digging in, the political finger pointing, escalating, too.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP, NY GOVERNOR TRADE BLAME AS LIRR WORKERS STRIKE\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  blaming the Trump administration who had delegated an emergency board last fall for cutting mediation short. Trump firing back online saying, "I have nothing to do with it." Adding, "No, Kathy, it's your fault. And now, looking over the facts, you should have not allowed this to happen." And overnight, confusion over whether talks are even happening. Governor Hochul posting, she's received an update from the \uc2 MTA\uc2  on the status of negotiations and plans for alternate service. But just moments later, the \uc2 LIRR\uc2  union is calling it an open-ended strike, telling ABC News, despite Hochul's claims, \uc2 MTA\uc2  officials had not reached out to them about negotiations. For many commuters, the railroad is the most affordable way into the city, helping them avoid tolls, parking, expensive ride shares and hours of gridlock traffic.\par \par \uc2 COMMUTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It really does add a stressful component. I can't imagine what people are going to be doing on Monday.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this morning, the unions are also pushing back against \uc2 MTA\uc2  claims that higher wages could lead to higher fares for passengers. They point to a Presidential Emergency Board study that they say shows otherwise. Still, this morning, both sides very far apart and no timetable on when they could return to the negotiation table, Rhiannon.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and this could affect a lot of people. Morgan, thank you so much for that update.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260520em5h0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMERICA STRONG GRADUATION CEREMONY 60 YEARS LATER\b0\par\par\uc2 351 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, RAIL SERVICE, COMMUTERS, STRANDED, HOCHUL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION STRIKE FUELS TRAVEL CHAOS\par \par \uc2 MIDAIR, CRASH, PILOTS, EJECT, AIR SHOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIGHTER PILOTS EJECT AFTER MIDAIR CRASH\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADOES, WINDS, HEAT, TEMPS, FIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOARING TEMPS AMID SEVERE WEATHER OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 SHOOTINGS, RANDOM, SUSPECTS, ARREST, INJURIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECTS ARRESTED AFTER SEVERAL RANDOM SHOOTINGS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IRAN, STRAIT, CEASEFIRE, RUSSIA, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP TO IRAN: "CLOCK IS TICKING"\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, PASSENGER, SHIP, EBOLA, OUTBREAK, WHO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW PASSENGER TESTS POSITIVE FOR HANTAVIRUS\par \par \uc2 CAR RAMMING, BYSTANDERS, INJURED, KNIFE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BYSTANDERS TACKLE CAR RAMMING DRIVER\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY, PRIMARY, TRUMP, REVENGE, MASSIE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS OUST TRUMP OPPONENT IN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 CROWDS, MAYHEM, SWATCH, STORES, PEPPER SPRAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PEPPER SPRAY DEPLOYED DURING WATCH RELEASE\par \par \uc2 DIVERS, SEARCH, TRAIN, COLLISON, TRUCK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP WORLD DIVERS JOIN SEARCH IN MALDIVES\par \par \uc2 HOCKEY, POPE LEO, SIX/SEVEN, O'NEAL\par \par \uc2 GRUNDBORG, ARMY, KOREA, VIETNAM, GRADUATE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally, "America Strong." For years he wore the uniform, but now he's traded it for a cap and gown.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Graduation day at \uc2 Georgia Institute of Technology\uc2  was not simply a ceremony for 88 year old Colonel Ken Grundborg.\par \par \uc2 COMMENCEMENT SPEAKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Today, we are honored to have him here to cross our commencement stage.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In 1960, just as he was set to receive his bachelor's degree in civil engineering, the army called him to serve in Korea. Six years later, as he prepared to complete his master's degree, another call Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 COMMENCEMENT SPEAKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You, Sir, are 60 years late to your graduation ceremony.\par \par \uc2 COL KEN GRUNDBORG (GRADUATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 COL KEN GRUNDBORG (GRADUATE)\par \par \uc2 I understood that when they had an opening or a need that that took precedence over me walking.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For decades, his service became the story. But on this day, the unfinished moment finally caught up with him.\par \par \uc2 COL KEN GRUNDBORG (GRADUATE)\par \par \uc2 The gratitude is beyond words that finally I got to do something I had missed.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 About time. Thanks for watching. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020260520em5h0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UNION STRIKE FUELS TRAVEL CHAOS LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD SERVICE SUSPENDED AFTER TALKS STALL\b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, RAIL SERVICE, COMMUTERS, STRANDED, HOCHUL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION STRIKE FUELS TRAVEL CHAOS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening, everyone. Thanks so much for joining us on this Sunday. I'm Linsey Davis. We begin with day two of the strike at the country's largest commuter rail system and fears for the Monday morning commute. Saturday morning more than 3,000 workers walked off the job. After months of negotiations, the unions and the \uc2 MTA\uc2  unable to reach a deal.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, frustrated commuters are scrambling to find alternative transportation. The \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  serves hundreds of thousands of riders each day. This weekend train stations stood like ghost towns, platforms eerily still, with signs warning no passengers, with the strike bringing the rhythm of the rails to a halt.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) New York's Governor is now urging unions to resume negotiations, saying a long strike hurts workers and commuters. A New York official estimates the strike could cost the region up to $61 million a day in lost revenue. ABC's Morgan Norwood leads us off tonight from New York's Penn Station.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD\uc2  SERVICE SUSPENDED AFTER TALKS STALL\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 When we fight.\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We win.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Chaos and confusion spreading tonight after a massive rail strike on the busiest commuter rail in the country leaves more than a quarter million riders without a way into New York City to start the workweek. Frustrated passengers arriving to empty terminals.\par \par \uc2 MARIA OBANDO (COMMUTER)\par \par \uc2 We have to do something to get to our house.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 35,000 WORKERS OFF THE JOB, LEAVING 27,000+ DAILY RIDERS STRANDED\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The walkout starting just after Midnight Saturday after months of negotiations between the \uc2 MTA\uc2  and unions representing some 3,500 rail workers collapsed. In a statement, the \uc2 MTA\uc2  saying, "Our last offer literally gave them everything they said they wanted in terms of pay, but they rejected even that." And the unions representing the LIRR saying, "This strike would not have happened if the \uc2 MTA\uc2  and \uc2 LIRR\uc2  offered our members the reasonable terms the government recommended multiple times."\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 What do we want?\par \par \uc2 STRIKING WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Contract.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Negotiations stalling over a pay increase between four and a half and 5%, along with health care costs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOV HOCHUL: RAIL ROAD IS THE LIFEBLOOD OF LONG ISLAND\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 The railroad is the lifeblood of Long Island. Without it, life as we know it is simply not possible.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, Governor Hochul urging both sides to get back to the table. But the unions claim they haven't heard from the \uc2 MTA\uc2  on when those talks could resume.\par \par \uc2 COMMUTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 How am I going to get back to the Bronx from Long Island?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The expansive commuter rail system operates 24/7, and also serves 126 stations across more than 300 route miles on 700 miles of track. The state's comptroller estimating the strike could cost the region up to $61 million per day in lost revenue.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Blacked out screens, tracks closed down. And now, Monday morning, just hours away, a growing number of questions about whether the \uc2 MTA\uc2 's limited shuttle bus service will be able to handle the influx of thousands of stranded passengers.\par \par \uc2 COMMUTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I pay $394 a month. It better be a seamless transaction for me to receive a refund.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Other riders turning to costly rideshares.\par \par \uc2 COMMUTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 And I Uber for $112.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And with Memorial Day weekend just ahead, the strike also threatens a critical artery out of New York City.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Linsey, tonight, we are seeing more workers join the picket lines, this is Penn Station. And, Linsey, just into ABC News just moments ago, the national mediation board calling both sides to negotiation table as soon as this evening in order to hammer out a deal ahead of tomorrow's busy Monday morning commute. Linsey.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020260520em5h00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 U.S. ISSUES STERN WARNING TO CUBA CUBA IN MIDST OF ECONOMIC CRISIS\b0\par\par\uc2 757 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS\par \par \uc2 RAIL, WORKERS, STRIKE, LONG ISLAND\par \par \uc2 MAINE, LUMBER, MILL, FIRE\par \par \uc2 IRAQI, TERROR, CHARGES, IRAN, WAR\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CHINA, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, DENIES, VA, VOTING, MAP\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 MALDIVES, SEARCH, SUSPENDED\par \par \uc2 CUBA, RATCLIFFE, WARNING, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, the US is ramping up the pressure on Cuba. CIA director John Ratcliffe visiting the island nation amid a worsening economic crisis. ABC's Alex Presha joins us now with details. Alex, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. ISSUES STERN WARNING TO CUBA\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. Yeah, this was an unplanned visit to Cuba by the CIA director to deliver a strong message from President Trump in person, as millions on the island face a crippling energy shortage.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the US with a stern warning to Cuba, imploring the island nation to stop being a safe haven for countries like Russia and China. CIA director John Radcliffe delivering President Trump's message in person, while leading an unannounced trip to Havana Thursday. The CIA saying "the US is prepared to engage with Cuba on economic and security issues," but "only if Cuba makes fundamental changes."\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Cuban government insists it poses no threat to US national security, nor will it allow actions against any other nation to be carried out from Cuba, a sentiment echoed by their foreign minister in an exclusive interview with our \uc2 Whit Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Trump administration alleges that Cuba is rolling out the red carpet for foreign adversaries like Russia and China.\par \par \uc2 BRUNO RODRIGUEZ PARRILLA\uc2  (MINISTER OF FOREIGN AFFAIRS FOR CUBA)\par \par \uc2 Cuba is not a threat to the, to the US national security, or foreign policy, or economy, or the American way of life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CUBA IN MIDST OF ECONOMIC CRISIS\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ratcliffe's visit is happening in the midst of an economic crisis. The US oil blockade and sanctions have caused a crippling energy shortage. Extensive blackouts across the country sparking protests. Cubans banging pots and shouting turn on the lights. The frustrations growing over the country's energy grid, now on the verge of collapse. Many Cubans are without power up to 22 hours a day, getting by in the dark with car headlights and flashlights.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This week, Cubans' energy minister declaring the country has no fuel and no diesel. President Trump has called Cuba a declining country in need of help. The \uc2 US State Department\uc2  has made a public offer of $100 million in humanitarian aid, something Cuban officials are now mulling over. US officials telling ABC News there are disagreements about how that money would be distributed.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, the US has offered that humanitarian aid multiple times this month. Meanwhile, sources say the US could announce an indictment of former Cuban President Raul Castro as soon as next week, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it could come. All right, Alex, thank you. And Whit, we just saw there in that piece, you were just in Cuba last week speaking to the foreign minister. I mean, what were your takeaways from being on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAKEAWAYS FROM BEING ON THE GROUND IN CUBA\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, the big takeaway for me is the looming question, how much longer can this really last? I mean, when I was there last week, it was desperation. I mean, people, we saw people actually sifting through the trash to try to find food. 20 hour rolling blackouts. The gas stations were all closed. People were getting their gas on the black market. And now it's even worse a week later.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're starting to see people pour out into the streets. They are demanding a change of some kind. When I spoke with the foreign minister, he insisted that internal affairs are off the table in these discussions with the US. He said that there's no progress in those discussions, but it seems like the administration is determined to force change, whether the Cuban government likes it or not.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, those US sanctions crippling that economy.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Whit. We appreciate your reporting on that. Great interview.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5g00032\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STACKING SAVINGS ON GROCERIES\b0\par\par\uc2 927 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS\par \par \uc2 RAIL, WORKERS, STRIKE, LONG ISLAND\par \par \uc2 MAINE, LUMBER, MILL, FIRE\par \par \uc2 IRAQI, TERROR, CHARGES, IRAN, WAR\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CHINA, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, DENIES, VA, VOTING, MAP\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 MALDIVES, SEARCH, SUSPENDED\par \par \uc2 CUBA, RATCLIFFE, WARNING, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP OF "THE MANDALORIAN AND GROGU"\par \par \uc2 E-BIKES, E-MOTORCYCLES, MOTHER, CHARGED\par \par \uc2 GROCERIES, SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, back here on "GMA." If you are looking for ways to save at the grocery store, well, I got just the person for you, because ABC's Morgan Norwood has done all the research, looking into this with ways to save. Good morning again, Morgan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STACKING SAVINGS ON GROCERIES\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're like, who, me?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Who, me?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, of course I got you. And, of course, all of us are feeling this at the grocery aisle. In fact, government data out this week really backs this up. So, Americans grocery bills spiked at the fastest monthly pace in nearly four years. I want to take a look. These are the staples that a lot of us are buying and reaching for each week.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ground beef is at an all-time high, up 15 percent in the past year. A pound of fresh tomatoes, guys, up 50 percent in a year.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Iceberg lettuce up about 8 percent. Cheddar cheese up about 5 percent. If you're thinking about a barbecue, maybe not the time. This is a combination of higher diesel prices raising costs for grocers, plus tariffs, some weather issues as well. So, it's really a ripple effect that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. What are some of the kind of apps that people can use out there to save?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. It used to be that we coupon clipped. I know that my, my dad really loves doing that. But everything is digital now. So, your phone will actually do this for you. So, apps like Grocery Pal and \uc2 Checkout 51\uc2 . So we're going to focus on one called Flipp. This week, we actually visited a local grocery store in New Jersey to buy these items that have gone up in price.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That total $53.18. You see it there. Then we shop that same list in the Flipp app. So, you enter your zip code, and then it'll pull up all the deals near you at multiple stores, and that includes your wholesale retailers like \uc2 Costco\uc2 , Sam's, budget grocery stores like \uc2 Aldi\uc2 , and your dollar stores. And it really paid off. So, for that exact same list, our bill was $40.60.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. Okay.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Huh.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's about $12.58 cheaper, more than 23 percent off.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So it's really worth it to try to put the effort forward, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is worth it, yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What about stacking up the savings so people can save you, saving more?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. It takes a little bit of commitment. It takes a little bit of effort. It takes a little bit of time. So, these deals actually do require you to drive to different locations. So, think about it. That is extra time, extra money on gas. But it is worth checking your list and picking at least one or two deals to save, and then to stack up on the deals even more. I don't want you to miss out on this. Like the grocery store's loyalty programs, I love those they are free to join.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Your points can quickly add up to cash back. It's always a nice surprise when you get to the counter and you're like, oh, I have $2 cash back. It makes a difference. Also, think about your delivery apps like Uber or \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 . They also promote deals at your local grocery stores, sometimes with free delivery. So, again, it does take a little bit of effort, some commitment to check prices frequently. Some of the best deals are flash sales that only last about a day or two.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) My grandmother still cuts coupons and mails them to me.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) My wife still does too. She's got, like, a little folder. Yeah. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, she does?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) My dad is still really into it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm into it. I love it.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now you can have the Flipps and the stacks and there's a whole thing. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We love a good deal in the Norwood household.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Morgan. All right. Coming up on "Good Morning America," \uc2 Disney\uc2  unveils its star-studded lineup from TV to movies, and the return of some fan favorites.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STAR-STUDDED LINEUP\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5g00038\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD\uc2  WORKERS BEGIN STRIKE LIRR SHUTS DOWN BUSIEST U.S. COMMUTER RAIL SERVICE\b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS\par \par \uc2 RAIL, WORKERS, STRIKE, LONG ISLAND\par \par \uc2 MAINE, LUMBER, MILL, FIRE\par \par \uc2 IRAQI, TERROR, CHARGES, IRAN, WAR\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, CHINA, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, DENIES, VA, VOTING, MAP\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 MALDIVES, SEARCH, SUSPENDED\par \par \uc2 CUBA, RATCLIFFE, WARNING, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP OF "THE MANDALORIAN AND GROGU"\par \par \uc2 E-BIKES, E-MOTORCYCLES, MOTHER, CHARGED\par \par \uc2 GROCERIES, SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 DISNEY\uc2 , UPFRONT\par \par \uc2 OZUNA, RAMIREZ, CATCH, CONE\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 O'CONNOR, LIEBER, SEXTON, LIRR, \uc2 MTA\uc2 , PENN STATION, 7,000 WORKERS, LONG ISLAND\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we'll have more coming up soon. Okay, Leslie, thank you. We turn now to the other breaking news. Overnight, the busiest commuter rail system in North America has been crippled by a strike. 7,000 Long Island railroad workers walking off the job after a deal was not reached at midnight. ABC's Ike Ejiochi has the latest. Good morning again, Ike.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD\uc2  WORKERS BEGIN STRIKE\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Rachel. We are right outside Penn Station. We're just a few hours ago, it was a mad dash to make the last train, and now it's a picket line. Workers for the LIRR walked off the job at 12:01 am this morning after their unions and the \uc2 MTA\uc2  failed to reach an agreement over health care and pay.\par \par \uc2 LIRR WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 What do we want?\par \par \uc2 LIRR WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 LIRR WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 When do we want it?\par \par \uc2 LIRR WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Now!\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the nation's largest commuter rail system now completely shut down as its workers hit the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 DUANE O'CONNOR (REP FOR BROTHERHOOD OF LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 I feel terrible. I'm terrible. This is gonna hurt. This is gonna hurt the island. This is gonna hurt the city.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The union for the Long Island Railroad and the \uc2 MTA\uc2  failing to reach a new contract deal overnight, negotiating potential salary bumps for the LIRR's 3,500 workers after four years without raises. The coalition of LIRR union says it was trying to negotiate raises between 4.5 and 5% for its employees due to inflation and high costs of living.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: METROPOLITAN TRANSPORTATION AUTHORITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIRR STRIKE\par \par \uc2 JANNO LIEBER\uc2  (CEO METROPOLITAN TRANSPORTATION AUTHORITY)\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this is not the result we were looking for. Our last offer literally gave the unions everything they said they had wanted in terms of pay, but they rejected even that.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But union officials say the \uc2 MTA\uc2  added last-minute health care takeaways that negatively swayed the final decision.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN SEXTON (NATIONAL VP BROTHERHOOD OF LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 Late in the eleventh hour, the railroad decided to throw healthcare contributions for new employees.\par \par \uc2 JANNO LIEBER\uc2  (CEO METROPOLITAN TRANSPORTATION AUTHORITY)\par \par \uc2 You know what we proposed? Half of what an average state worker pays for their healthcare costs. We, that's what we proposed.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both parties at the negotiating table until the clock struck midnight as New York residents rush to make their final trains.\par \par \uc2 PASSENGER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm able to get on the train, and I can get back to Ron Cochran from Penn Station in about an hour and twenty minutes, whereas if I tried to drive home, it would be three hours easy.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, the big question is how long will this last? Well, the biggest impact will be weekend travel. People coming into the city, airport trips, big events, but the real stress test will be Monday morning. If no deal is reached, because that could mean a very difficult commute for hundreds of thousands of New Yorkers across Long Island and New York City. Whit?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIRR SHUTS DOWN BUSIEST U.S. COMMUTER RAIL SERVICE\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5g0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HIGH COURT DECLINES TO REINSTATE VA DEMS NEW VOTING MAP\b0\par\par\uc2 144 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS\par \par \uc2 RAIL, WORKERS, STRIKE, LONG ISLAND\par \par \uc2 MAINE, LUMBER, MILL, FIRE\par \par \uc2 IRAQI, TERROR, CHARGES, IRAN, WAR\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, CHINA, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, DENIES, VA, VOTING, MAP\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Supreme Court denied an emergency request from Democrats in Virginia to use a new congressional map ahead of the midterm elections in November. State officials had asked the justices to step in to allow the state to use a congressional map that was drawn by Democrats and recently approved by voters. The justices declined. The plan would have given Democrats a chance at gaining four more seats in Congress. The appeal was considered a long shot bid, since the justices typically deferred to state courts when it comes to state laws, Whit.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT DECLINES TO REINSTATE VA DEMS NEW VOTING MAP\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5g0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 9:00 HALF HOUR CATCHING A HOMERUN WITH A CONE\b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS\par \par \uc2 RAIL, WORKERS, STRIKE, LONG ISLAND\par \par \uc2 MAINE, LUMBER, MILL, FIRE\par \par \uc2 IRAQI, TERROR, CHARGES, IRAN, WAR\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CHINA, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, DENIES, VA, VOTING, MAP\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 MALDIVES, SEARCH, SUSPENDED\par \par \uc2 CUBA, RATCLIFFE, WARNING, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP OF "THE MANDALORIAN AND GROGU"\par \par \uc2 E-BIKES, E-MOTORCYCLES, MOTHER, CHARGED\par \par \uc2 GROCERIES, SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 DISNEY\uc2 , UPFRONT\par \par \uc2 OZUNA, RAMIREZ, CATCH, CONE\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's our second hour. Breaking news on strike.\par \par \uc2 LIRR WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 What do we want?\par \par \uc2 LIRR WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 LIRR WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 When do we want it?\par \par \uc2 LIRR WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Now.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Long Island railroad workers hit the picket line, shutting down the nation's busiest commuter rail. 250,000 commuters forced to find other ways to get to work. The major sticking points and what both sides are saying this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 With summer just around the corner, our expert has tips for safer swimming, with all the best practices for poolside parents, from pool noodles to the color of your child's swimsuit.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POOL SAFETY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIFESAVING TIPS FOR PARENTS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Drake's big surprise, the Grammy winner dropping not one, not two, but three new albums this weekend. We're breaking down the beats and the beefs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DRAKE'S BIG SURPRISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAKE THEM KNOW\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And we are feeling the love this morning. Rock and Roll Hall of Famer Darlene Love is here, reminiscing about the past and opening up about a new pet project as she singing.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE LOVE (MUSIC AMBASSADOR LOVE WITHOUT LABELS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: R & B LEGEND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DARLENE LOVE\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 It's Saturday morning. This is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. How about that?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I know.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Darlene Love, we were listening. We're like, sing, sing some more, sing some more, please. Gio off this morning, but so great to have Rachel Scott back at the desk. We got a lot of news to get to...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ...including the busiest passenger rail service in the nation, brought to a halt as workers go on strike overnight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, the major name change in women's health care for the condition formerly known as PCOS. The reason behind that change and what doctors say you need to know.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5g0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD\uc2  WORKERS BEGIN STRIKE LIRR SHUTS DOWN BUSIEST U.S. COMMUTER RAIL SERVICE\b0\par\par\uc2 621 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS\par \par \uc2 RAIL, WORKERS, STRIKE, LONG ISLAND\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the other breaking news overnight. The busiest commuter rail system in North America hit by a strike. 7,000 Long Island railroad workers walking off the job after a deal was not reached at midnight. ABC's Ike Ejiochi has the latest. Good morning, Ike.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 LONG ISLAND RAIL ROAD\uc2  WORKERS BEGIN STRIKE\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Rachel. We're here at Penn Station, where just hours ago it was a mad dash to get home, and now it's a picket line. \uc2 LIRR\uc2  workers walked off the job at 12:01 am this morning after the union and the \uc2 MTA\uc2  failed to reach an agreement over healthcare and pay.\par \par \uc2 RAILROAD WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 What do we want?\par \par \uc2 RAILROAD WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 RAILROAD WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 When do we want it?\par \par \uc2 RAILROAD WORKERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Now.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the nation's largest commuter rail system now completely shut down as its workers hit the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 DUANE O'CONNOR (REP FOR BROTHERHOOD OF LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 I feel terrible, terrible. This is going to hurt. This is going to hurt the island. This is going to hurt the city.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The union for the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  and the \uc2 MTA\uc2  failing to reach a new contract deal overnight, negotiating potential salary bumps for the \uc2 LIRR\uc2 's 3,500 workers. After four years without raises, the coalition of \uc2 LIRR\uc2  union says it was trying to negotiate raises between 4.5 and 5 percent for its employees due to inflation and high costs of living.\par \par \uc2 JANNO LIEBER\uc2  (CEO METROPOLITAN TRANSPORTATION AUTHORITY)\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this is not the result we were looking for. Our last offer literally gave the unions everything they said they had wanted in terms of pay, but they rejected even that.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But union officials say the \uc2 MTA\uc2  added last minute health care takeaways that negatively swayed the final decision.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN SEXTON (NATIONAL VP BROTHERHOOD OF LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 Late in the eleventh hour, the railroad decided to throw health care contributions for new employees.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIRR SHUTS DOWN BUSIEST U.S. COMMUTER RAIL SERVICE\par \par \uc2 JANNO LIEBER\uc2  (CEO METROPOLITAN TRANSPORTATION AUTHORITY)\par \par \uc2 You know what we proposed? half of what an average state worker pays for their health care costs. We, that's what we proposed.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both parties at the negotiating table until the clock struck midnight as New York residents rush to make their final trains.\par \par \uc2 NEW YORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm able to get on the train, and I can get back to Ronkonkoma from Penn Station in about an hour and 20 minutes, Whereas if I tried to drive home, it would be three hours easy.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, hundreds of thousands of New Yorkers forced to find alternate methods of transportation.\par \par \uc2 NEW YORKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I have family that comes out on the train almost every weekend to visit, and this will be difficult because they will have to drive, which is also challenging. Three grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the big question here is how long will this last? The immediate impact is weekend travel. People coming into the city, big events, airport. But the real stress test will be Monday morning if no deal is reached. If that happens, this will be a very difficult commute for hundreds of thousands of New Yorkers across Long Island and New York City. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Ike Ejiochi for us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5g0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BUSIEST RAIL SERVICE IN U.S. SHUTS DOWN 270,000+ DAILY RIDERS RELY ON ROUTES FROM LONG ISLAND TO NYC\b0\par\par\uc2 852 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LIRR, \uc2 MTA\uc2 , SEXTON, LIEBER, STRIKE, TRUMP, HOCHUL\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thanks for joining us on this Saturday. I'm \uc2 Whit Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We begin tonight with breaking news, a crippling blow to the country's busiest rail system. Union workers walking off the job early this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUSIEST RAIL SERVICE IN U.S. SHUTS DOWN\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Hundreds of New York's \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  workers hitting the picket lines, paralyzing the system. Talks breaking down over proposed pay increases and health care costs. The strike leaves hundreds of thousands of commuters in limbo. And now, the blame game. New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and President Trump pointing fingers at each other for the potential commuting nightmare.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is the first strike on this rail system in more than 30 years, and it could get much worse by Monday morning. ABC's Morgan Norwood leads us off from Queens, New York.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 270,000+ DAILY RIDERS RELY ON ROUTES FROM LONG ISLAND TO NYC\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, hundreds of rail workers on the nation's largest commuter rail system, now walking off the job and heading the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 If we don't get it.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Shut it down.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , the main source for a quarter million daily riders in and out of New York City, brought to a halt.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry that the \uc2 MTA\uc2  has decided to put you guys in the middle of our dispute.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE UNIONS REPRESENTING 3,500 WORKERS GO ON STRIKE OVER PAY\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The unions representing Long Island rail workers and the \uc2 MTA\uc2  negotiating for months. But just before midnight, the talks collapsing.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN SEXTON (NATL VP BROTHERHOOD OF LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 After two days of round-the-clock negotiations, the parties were unable to reach a deal.\par \par \uc2 JANNO LIEBER\uc2  (CEO METROPOLITAN TRANSPORTATION AUTHORITY)\par \par \uc2 You know what we proposed? Half of what an average state worker pays for their health care costs. We, that's what we proposed.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The talks stalling on negotiations over a pay increase between 4.5% and 5%, and health care costs for some 3,500 workers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP AND GOV. HOCHUL TRADE ATTACKS OVER TRAIN STRIKE\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Trump administration attempted to broker a deal through an emergency board last fall. And today, New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  blaming the President for the failed talks writing, "The disruption that Long Islanders face starting tonight is the direct result of reckless actions by the Trump administration to cut mediation short and push these negotiations toward a strike." Trump immediately firing back on social media, writing, "I have nothing to do with it. Never even heard about it until this morning. She just blurted out, 'It's President Trump's fault.' No, Kathy, it's your fault. And now, looking over the facts, you should not have allowed this to happen."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIDERS FORCED TO SHUTTLE OR DRIVE AMID SOARING GAS PRICES\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And while politicians trade blame, passengers left scrambling to find any way into the city, potentially creating major headaches for fans heading to games. The \uc2 Yankees\uc2 , Mets, and \uc2 Knicks\uc2 , warning fans to plan ahead. Passengers trying to get to JFK Airport staring down a travel nightmare, too. JFK urging travelers to budget extra time. Anthony Carlo from our New York station WABC on Long Island.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY CARLO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The New Hyde Park LIRR station in Nassau County is a ghost town on a beautiful Saturday. This is obviously disrupting weekend travel plans for so many people who tell us they are hoping and praying it's all resolved before the workweek begins.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For many commuters, that means several hours away on Long Island. This railroad, the most affordable way into the city, helping them avoid tolls, costly rideshares, parking, and surging gas prices.\par \par \uc2 COMMUTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm able to get on the train and I can get back to Ronkonkoma from Penn Station in about an hour and 20 minutes. Whereas if I tried to drive home, it would be three hours easy.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Whit, we are just getting word from New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who says she just got an update from the \uc2 MTA\uc2  on the status of negotiations and also plans for alternate service. But she did not elaborate on what those plans, what those solutions would look like. In the meantime, this terminal here, sitting nearly empty, very few passengers, the barricades are up, both sides still far apart, and service at a standstill tonight, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And tens of thousands eager for a resolution. All right, Morgan Norwood for us.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020260520em5g00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ONE-ON-ONE WITH GM CEO MARY BARRA GM CEO TALKS ECONOMY, AFFORDABILITY, JOBS & MORE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,142 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRES RAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEARTLAND ON ALERT\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, HUMIDITY, STORMS, WIND, HAIL, LIGHTNING\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , XI, HIGH STAKES, IRAN, TRADE\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, QUARANTINE, MONITORED, HEWLETT, KORNFELD\par \par \uc2 RAIL, STRIKE, COUNTDOWN, COMMUTER, UNIONS, FIRE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, RADCLIFFE, BLACKOUTS, EMBARGO, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , \uc2 IRS\uc2 , FUND, TREASURY, ALLIES, TAXPAYERS, COMEY\par \par \uc2 MIFEPRISTONE, SUPREME COURT, RULING, MAIL\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS, \uc2 PISTONS\uc2 , CAVALIERS, WEMBY, SPURS, \uc2 TIMBERWOLVES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PRANK, WATER WARS, GUN, OFFICER, VIDEO, RENDON, RYDER\par \par \uc2 ROLLING STONES, AI, VIDEO, "IN THE STARS", CARTER\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , SCHEDULE, TEAMS, 2026, EXCITEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARMING TREND\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, WILDFIRES\par \par \uc2 PLANE, CRASH, OHIO, HOME\par \par \uc2 PATEL, SNORKEL, USS ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 K-BEAUTY\par \par \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 , BARRA\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're gonna go now to a "GMA" exclusive, my one-on-one with GM CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 . The top-selling automaker in the US is investing $9 billion here in this country this year, and even partnering with the Girl Scouts to help train and encourage the next generation workforce. We got into all of it inside the Kentucky factory where GM produces one of its most iconic cars.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH GM CEO \uc2 MARY BARRA\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) GM's CEO, \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 , exclusively took us behind the scenes of the only Corvette factory in the world here in Bowling Green, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 It's been the home of the Corvette for 45 years.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Opening up about the economy, affordability, jobs, and the future.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Consumers are currently facing a wave of economic anxiety. What are you seeing inside of \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ? Are people pulling back on purchases? Are they falling behind on their auto loans?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 We are really not seeing that as of yet. It's something that we watch carefully, but we are really proud of the fact that we have six models at, at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  that start under $30,000.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) People are still buying cars?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 People are still buying cars.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they're paying their bills on time?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And they're, that, again, that's what we are seeing.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When gas prices start climbing, how long is it usually before you actually see an impact on the business and what customers buy?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 It depends on what people think, so it's not just where the price is today, it's more how long do they think it's going to persist, and I think that is why we maybe aren't seeing a really strong reaction yet. When people need a new car, they need a new car.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is there a tipping point in your mind, if this goes through the summer, through Memorial Day, potentially as far as Labor Day, and prices get near $5 or even surpass $5 a gallon, does that concern you?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Of course it concerns me, because I know affordability is an issue for, for every American, and so we want to make sure that people have the opportunity to be able to afford what they need. That's why we focus on having affordable vehicles. But it is something that we're watching because I think it speaks to the overall health of the economy, which is important for the country, important for jobs, and we hope that, obviously, the conflict in Iran ends, ends as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GM CEO TALKS ECONOMY, AFFORDABILITY, JOBS & MORE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Top of mind for Barra is artificial intelligence and using it across all facets of her business.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GM CEO \uc2 MARY BARRA\uc2  TALKS AI & JOBS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is this tremendous excitement around AI, but there's also a lot of fear, especially when it comes to jobs. How many jobs could AI replace at GM?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Well, I think sometimes we, people spend a lot of time figuring out what AI can replace, but they don't think about what's gonna come and what it's gonna enable in the new jobs.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you think AI will create more jobs than it does away with?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 It, it's hard to predict right now what that balance is going to be, but I think there are whole new careers that are going to be coming that no one has thought of yet.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One way the company thinks about the future is through new generations and training, including a years long partnership with the \uc2 Girl Scouts of America\uc2 , and a $50 million commitment to Steam Education.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GM CEO \uc2 MARY BARRA\uc2 'S ADVICE FOR GIRLS\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 We've had exposure to over a million girl scouts, giving them real-life examples of math and science that I think will get their interest in something that means something to them.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Girl Scouts here today, getting to see firsthand how a Corvette engine is built, and ask Barra questions.\par \par \uc2 GIRL SCOUT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 What advice do you have for girls at my age?\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA (CEO OF GENERAL MOTORS)\par \par \uc2 I wasn't sure what I wanted to do, but, you know, studying math and science really gave me a strong foundation where I could really, you know, when I developed interests, I could really pursue it. And that's my big message to someone your age. Don't take yourself out of the running before you even start.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And here she is. In addition to her role as GM CEO, Barra sits on the boards of The Business Roundtable, Detroit Economic Club, and our parent company, Disney. And I asked her, as someone who leads 160,000 employees, what's the biggest thing she is looking for in new hires right now.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She said it's curiosity, guys, because things are changing so fast, and I can tell you having spent some time with those girl scouts, they have that in spades. Jennifer Putman, who is the master builder there at the Corvette site, incredible teacher. And it's, it made me feel really positive about the future.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a good rule for any job.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Truly.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. Thanks, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5f0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 COUNTDOWN TO POSSIBLE RAIL STRIKE FIRE ON THE TRACKS CAUSES DELAYS FOR COMMUTERS\b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRES RAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEARTLAND ON ALERT\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, HUMIDITY, STORMS, WIND, HAIL, LIGHTNING\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, XI, HIGH STAKES, IRAN, TRADE\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, QUARANTINE, MONITORED, HEWLETT, KORNFELD\par \par \uc2 RAIL, STRIKE, COUNTDOWN, COMMUTER, UNIONS, FIRE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, just to be safe. Victor, thank you so much for that. We're gonna turn now to the countdown to what would be a crippling strike of the largest commuter rail line in the country. Stephanie Ramos is at Penn Station here in New York. Stephanie, there is no deal after 12 hours of talks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO POSSIBLE RAIL STRIKE\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, Michael. A 12-hour negotiating session between the \uc2 MTA\uc2  and Long Island railroad unions ended late last night without a deal, leaving in place a potential strike for commuters tomorrow morning. Saturday morning's compute for hundreds of thousands of Long Islanders now hangs in the balance. The Long Island Railroad and unions representing 3,500 workers are back at the bargaining table trying to avoid what could be the first ILWR strike in more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If no deal is reached by Midnight Saturday morning, train service across the entire railroad could shut down, impacting nearly 300,000 daily riders on North America's busiest commuter rail system. At the center of the dispute, pay increases. The \uc2 MTA\uc2  says it's offering raises of about 3%. Unions want closer to 5%, arguing workers need a contract that keeps pace with inflation.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The \uc2 MTA\uc2  warns larger rages without changes to work rules could mean fare hikes, service cuts or layoffs, and that is a claim unions are calling fearmongering. Meanwhile, contingency plans are already under way on Long Island, including shuttle bus service if those trains stop running, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, which would be a big inconvenience for so many people. This morning commute is already being impacted by fire.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE ON THE TRACKS CAUSES DELAYS FOR COMMUTERS\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah, adding more trouble to the travel chaos, a fire in one of the East River tunnels is causing major disruptions this morning. So Long Island railroad commuters will have to divert once again, because those trains are being diverted from Penn Station to Grand Central terminal for the morning rush.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Stephanie, hopefully you're walking where you're going next. You don't have to worry about the trains. Alright, thank you. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5f00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LIRR\uc2  WORKERS THREATENING TO STRIKE WAGE INCREASE WOULD LEAD TO FARE HIKES FOR COMMUTERS\b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CHINA, TRUMP, XI, IRAN, RUBIO, STRAIT, CONSUMERS\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, QUARANTINE, SHIP, KORNFELD, ROSMARIN\par \par \uc2 WAR, UKRAINE, ZELENSKYY, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, SEVERE, HEARTLAND, WIND\par \par \uc2 SEXUAL, MISCONDUCT, CONGRESS, JEFFRIES, JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 PLANE, CRASH, OCEAN, RESCUE, EDDY, PIOWATY, WHIPPLE\par \par \uc2 LIRR\uc2 , WORKERS, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure is. Okay, Matt, thanks very much. Now to a major rail strike looming, a shutdown that could impact hundreds of thousands of commuters. Sam, you've got the details.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIRR WORKERS THREATENING TO STRIKE\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, it could be potentially a big one. The \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  is the busiest commuter rail system in North America, running 24 hours 24/7 between New York City and Long Island. But it could come to a screeching halt this weekend. Some 3,500 workers are threatening to walk off the job Saturday morning if an agreement is not reached. So, the sticking point is money. The unions are demanding cost of living raises, but the Metropolitan Transit Authority says that would lead to huge fare hikes for riders.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The LA (PH) RR carries nearly 300 thousand riders per day, guys, and if a strike does happen, the \uc2 MTA\uc2  is already urging people to work from home if possible. Otherwise, they could take shuttle busses into Queens, connect with the subways, or they could drive, braving the notorious traffic in the region. We all know it is also notorious. It's like the world's largest parking lot, the Long Island Expressway. The last LIRR strike was in 1994. It lasted two days until the Governor stepped in to resolve it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hopefully this one doesn't get to that point.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260527em5e00030\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PLAY OF THE DAY REMOTE WORK PARAGLIDER\b0\par\par\uc2 224 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TORNADO, WILDFIRE, DAMAGE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER\par \par \uc2 IRAN, WAR, PEACE, TALKS\par \par \uc2 ISLAMABAD, LOCKDOWN, PEACE, TALKS\par \par \uc2 US, BOAT, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 SOLDIER, BET, MADURO, POLYMARKET\par \par \uc2 MISSING, STUDENTS, FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 TEEN, CHARGED, MURDER, MALL, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, CAMPS, WARNED, FLOOD\par \par \uc2 MORTGAGE, RATES\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , NHL, PLAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP OF "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA 2"\par \par \uc2 REINER\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS, OGWUMIKE\par \par \uc2 90S, NOSTALGIA\par \par \uc2 MUIRHEAD, PARAGLIDING\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, back now with our Play of the Day, with remote work expanding the possibilities of where we get the job done. Scott Muirhead, a content creator, wanted to test those limits. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MUIRHEAD (VIDEO PRESENTER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Hey, I'm going to run by a couple of clients houses today. Yeah. We got, I have a meeting at 1:00 and then a meeting at 2:00. Yeah. No, I'm commuting there now. Traffic is just horrible.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) By the way, he's paragliding right now. That's off the coast of San Diego. He is just paragliding.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And coming up on "GMA" in our second hour, the cast of "The Devil Wears Prada 2" is returning to the runway and making New York City their catwalk as they open up about returning to the franchise after 20 years.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260508em4p0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 'CHEF OF THE YEAR' CHARGED WITH MURDER OFFICIALS: SUSPECT ACCUSED OF KILLING WIFE IN A HOTEL\b0\par\par\uc2 618 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MALL, SHOOTING, DISPUTE, MORSE, ARRESTS\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, CASON, EVACUATION, STRAIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HUNDREDS FLEE RAGING WILDFIRES\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, WILDFIRES, STORMS, HAIL, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, IRAN, WAR, NUCLEAR WEAPON, CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP VOWS TO "SHOOT AND KILL" MINELAYERS\par \par \uc2 MADURO, VAN DYKE, INSIDER TRADING, POLYMARKET\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOLDIER ARRESTED FOR ALLEGED MADURO BET\par \par \uc2 HIKER, VOLCANO ERUPTION, SANTAGUIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO ESCAPE ERUPTING VOLCANO\par \par \uc2 MACDONALD, MURDER, ARREST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 'CHEF OF THE YEAR' CHARGED WITH MURDER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, an award-winning chef at the \uc2 University of Massachusetts Amherst\uc2  is arrested and charged with murdering his own wife. Her body found inside a campus hotel room. Here's Stephanie Ramos.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIALS: SUSPECT ACCUSED OF KILLING WIFE IN A HOTEL\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, an award-winning chef at \uc2 UMass Amherst\uc2  now behind bars, accused of killing his wife in a hotel room on campus. Jeffrey MacDonald in court this morning, his lawyer entering a not guilty plea on his behalf.\par \par \uc2 POLICE OFFICER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 UMPD is yelling about having a body down and needing additional personnel.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just before 8:00 PM Wednesday, police responding to a 911 call about an emergency at Hotel UMass, about 90 miles from Boston. According to the criminal complaint, a violent struggle erupted between MacDonald and officers. MacDonald allegedly striking one officer repeatedly in the face. Police then finding MacDonald's wife dead inside a hotel room. MacDonald's wife suffering injuries resulting from a violent assault, according to the complaint. MacDonald allegedly telling investigators he'd intentionally beaten his wife to death using hands, feet, as well as a variety of other blunt objects.\par \par \uc2 CONTENT CREATOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Congratulations, Chef Jeff MacDonald...\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Less than a year ago, MacDonald, seen here in a TikTok video posted by UMass in 2024, was named Chef of the Year by the \uc2 American Culinary Federation\uc2 , one of the most prestigious honors in the industry.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, that husband is being held without bail. In a statement, UMass called the news heartbreaking and deeply unsettling. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Stephanie Ramos reporting. Stephanie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 10% OF WORKFORCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: META LAYING OFF 8,000 WORKERS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , cutting 10% of its workforce.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Meta\uc2  saying it will lay off about 8,000 employees beginning next month. The company also canceling plans to fill 6,000 open roles. The company says it plans to invest more on developing artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEDICAL MARIJUANA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EFFORT TO EASE RESTRICTIONS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, an historic policy shift on medical marijuana by the Department of Justice. Acting Attorney General Todd Blanche signing an order reclassifying state-licensed medical marijuana from a schedule 1 drug to a less dangerous schedule 3 drug. The move does not legalize marijuana for recreational use.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back here tonight, what's being called a groundbreaking treatment to restore hearing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also, look at this. The close call for a truck driver and a school bus. The truck blaring the horn, a school bus appearing to run a red light. Also, the terrifying moment a hiker comes face-to-face with the bear. The moment the bear starts to charge, and what that hiker does right there to save his own life. And, of course, live on ABC tonight, the \uc2 NFL\uc2  draft. Why the presumed number one pick tonight is watching from home. Pretty good reason. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020260427em4n00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STRIKE AVERTED FOR LOS ANGELES SCHOOLS TENTATIVE DEALS REACHED WITH TEACHERS UNION & SERVICE EMPLOYEES UNION\b0\par\par\uc2 359 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADO, HAIL, FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE STORMS SLAM THE HEARTLAND\par \par \uc2 IRAN, WAR, PEACE TALKS, VANCE, LEBANON, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLOCKADE OF IRANIAN PORTS UNDER PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO XIV, TRUMP, VANCE, BARRON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT DELETES JESUS-LIKE ONLINE POST\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL, GONZALES, RESIGNATION, SEXUAL MISCONDUCT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ERIC SWALWELL TO RESIGN FROM CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 LOS ANGELES SCHOOLS, STRIKE, NEGOTIATIONS, UNION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRIKE AVERTED FOR LOS ANGELES SCHOOLS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to turn now to breaking news overnight about the second largest school district in the country. And Trevor Ault is in Los Angeles with the update on the strikes. Good morning, Trevor.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR AULT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Rebecca. And we were inches away from a strike here in the school district in Los Angeles. But just in the past few hours, the district has announced they do have a tentative deal in place. As you mentioned, this is the second largest school district in the country and 400,000 students and their families went to sleep not knowing if they would have school today.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR AULT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The district has been negotiating with three separate unions. On Sunday, they reached tentative deals with the teachers union and the administrative union. But the sticking point has been the service employees union. That's workers like custodians, bus drivers, cafeteria workers. That was the final piece. Because if one union did not have a deal with the school district, all three of them were going to go on strike and school would have had to have been canceled.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR AULT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It wasn't until about 2:00 AM that the district announced they have this tentative deal in place. Now, all three of the deals are going to have to be approved by their unions. But as of now, within just a few hours, school will be in session here in Los Angeles. Rebecca?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is a relief for all those families. All right, Trevor, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260507em4e0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NO RESOLUTION IN SIGHT TO DHS FINDING IMPASSE LAWMAKERS GO ON RECESS AS DHS EMPLOYEES REMAIN UNPAID\b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 March 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHUTDOWN STALEMATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "PLANE" MISERY\par \par \uc2 TSA, SECURITY LINES, SHUTDOWN, FUNDING, DEJANOVICH, KEATON\par \par \uc2 DHS, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, FUNDING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NO RESOLUTION IN SIGHT TO DHS FINDING IMPASSE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to where things stand this morning in the battle over homeland security funding. ABC News senior White House correspondent Selina Wang is in Washington. Good morning, Selina.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Janai, good morning. We're now in day 44 of this partial government shutdown, and lawmakers have left town with no deal in sight.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, here is the problem. The House and Senate have each passed their own plans to reopen the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , but they can't agree on the same one. House Republicans rejected the Senate's bill because it stripped out funding for ICE and border patrol, even though both of those agencies are already fully funded under President Trump's mega bill passed last year. So, the House went ahead and passed its own version. But minority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  says that plan is dead on arrival in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And let's not forget, guys, why Democrats thought this fight was worth it in the first place. They've been pushing for real reforms to immigration reforms to immigration enforcement after federal officers killed Americans Renee Good and Alex Pretti in Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Their demands include ICE agents wearing body cameras and obtaining judicial warrants before entering private homes. But so far, they've gotten none of their demands.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And while TSA workers are expected to start getting paid on Monday, as you just heard, tens of thousands of other DHS employees from FEMA to the Coast Guard are not, Gio.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as you said, members of Congress are now going on recess. Selina Wang, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260407em3t00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 50 STATES IN 50 WEEKS CARRYING ON THE TRADITIONS OF HAND-BLOWN GLASS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 March 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EMERGENCY LANDING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN ALLIES PUSH BACK\par \par \uc2 WAR, STEALTH, ATTACKS, ENERGY, TRUMP, NETANYAHU, ALLIES\par \par \uc2 WAR, HEGSETH, CONGRESS, BILLIONS, TRUMP, JAPAN\par \par \uc2 WAR, OIL, GAS, PRICES, SPIKE, STRAIT, FARMERS\par \par \uc2 DRONES, BARKSDALE, THREATS, SECURITY, MULROY\par \par \uc2 AIRCRAFTS, NEWARK, ATC, CLOSE CALL, AVERTED, RUNWAYS\par \par \uc2 WEGOVY, FDA, DOSE, \uc2 NOVO NORDISK\uc2 , ZEBOUND\par \par \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , TOURNAMENT, UPSETS, DUKE, BRACKETS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY IS IT SO HOT?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL TIME MARCH HEAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "SPIDER-MAN: BRAND NEW DAY"\par \par \uc2 PAUL, "BACHELORETTE", PULLED, DOMESTIC VIOLENCE, VIDEO, MORTENSEN\par \par \uc2 IRELAND, LAWSUIT, PARTNERS, FRAUD, BASINGER, BRAND\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, FIFTH GRADER, BUZZER BEATER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ATMOSPHERIC RIVER\par \par \uc2 HAWAII, DOCTOR, COURT, MURDER, KONIG\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN, STUDENT, DEAD, BARCELONA\par \par \uc2 SURFER, BITTEN, SHARK\par \par \uc2 AVALANCHE, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 WEST VIRGINIA, BLENKO\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right now we're going to go 50 States In 50 Weeks. The country's 250th birthday coming up in July. We're celebrating by visiting every single state in the nation. Number 35 on the list is West Virginia. Rhiannon Ally is there in the town of Milton. Good morning, Rhiannon.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning Lara. You and I were just singing the John Denver song yesterday. You know the line. Almost heaven, west Virginia. And one thing that West Virginians are so proud of is the \uc2 Blenko Glass Company\uc2 . It is over a century old. It is still family-owned, and they make the most beautiful creations just like this. And we got a behind the scenes look. Let's show you how it's done.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nestled along the Mud River in rural West Virginia, color and heat come alive.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 50 STATES IN 50 WEEKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CARRYING ON THE TRADITIONS OF HAND-BLOWN GLASS\par \par \uc2 BLENKO GLASS COMPANY\uc2  (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Here goes.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Generations of glassworkers have been molding and blowing glass at Blenko Glass Factory in Milton.\par \par \uc2 BLENKO GLASS COMPANY\uc2  (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Seeing the glass get a little bit darker with this color.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Carrying on the traditions of hand-blown glass for over 100 years. The company still family-owned and run by the great grandson of the founder.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BLENKO (\uc2 BLENKO GLASS COMPANY\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Lisa is using a file to put the lips on the water bottle.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Blenko came to Milton in 1921 and has become the heart beat of this region. But its reach has gone much further. Its glass can be found all over the world in institutions from the White House, to the stained glass windows of cathedrals, along with the pro football hall of fame, and the Air Force Academy, to the hands of musicians at the CMA awards, and into the homes of millions. Hundreds of glass factories used to dot the state, but now there are less than five.\par \par \uc2 JIMBO ADKINS (APPRENTICE GLASSWORKER AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 We're trying to keep the Appalachian glass culture alive.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Their signature piece is their famous 384 water bottles, originally made for Frigidaire back in 1938, and still endures as their best-selling item. From the practical, to the whimsical, to the curious. They are still using traditional sand to hand methods.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BLENKO (\uc2 BLENKO GLASS COMPANY\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 These are the same implements and same tools and same techniques that have been used for over 2,000 years to make glass.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Starting with their own special recipe mixed in-house.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BLENKO (\uc2 BLENKO GLASS COMPANY\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Everything that we use is made in America.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Through their apprenticeship program, veteran glassblowers are teaching a new generation of glassworkers the ancient craft.\par \par \uc2 JIMBO ADKINS (APPRENTICE GLASSWORKER AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 We take melted sand that is at 2,000 degrees, which is a quarter temperature of the surface of the sun, and we make these beautiful vessels out of them.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And once you start...\par \par \uc2 RAINE ROGERS (APPRENTICE GLASSWORKER AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 Something addictive about it, just getting to play with glass every day.\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE ESTEP (APPRENTICE GLASSWORKER AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 This is what I want to do for the rest of my life.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Blenko, known for their unique and vibrant colors.\par \par \uc2 BLENKO GLASS COMPANY\uc2  (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Our tagline is the most colorful name in glass.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Each piece handcrafted one at a time, making every one just a little unique.\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE ESTEP (APPRENTICE GLASSWORKER AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 I'm really doing something that I love doing, and it's being sent off to somebody who loves it. So, that's an incredible feeling.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you can just hear the pride that these workers take in their work. I am here now with the VP of manufacturing. This is David Wertz. David, you guys have made a special vase just for "GMA" that we're going to share with our viewers in just a moment. But first, walk us through how it's done.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 Sure, absolutely. So, what we're seeing here is Mr. Perry Mayes blocking or cooling the outside of our glass. Here comes Mr. Jim Atkins with a preheated piece, and we're going to go ahead and blow it in that mold (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And all of this, this is cool because all of this is hand-blown.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There are no machines.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 None.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So there is room for error here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 There's tons of room for error. In fact, you get six humans working together, you're gonna have lots of room for that error. So, what we see right here is Mr. Perry just hit his foot and told our mold holder here the exact moment to open it up.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is, you saw it. It still looks a little bit like Jell-O, so you got to let it cool down, right?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 That's right. That's right. Everything's temperature...\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is the third and final step we have here. We see the vase, and what is he doing to the top?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 So, we're adding a lip wrap here. Mr. Isaiah's brought a cobalt lip wrap over to Mr. Chris who is our finisher on this team, and he is going to apply that and reheat it here in just a moment to build it into the piece.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And these are all different colors even though it all comes out looking the same initially?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 Exactly right. So, on the pipe, it's a totally different color than by the time it actually cools and anneals. This actually has three different colors all together.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look at this. This is the final product. This is what it looks like when it's all done. Look how beautiful this is. What is the name of this?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 This is the Good Morning West Virginia piece.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good Morning West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 Yep.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You used to actually work here on the floor, but you said this is a really tough job.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 I wasn't tough enough to do it, no, ma'am. So, you had to have a lot of grit. You have to have passion, otherwise you don't show up at 6:00 am when it's 0 degrees or when it's 120 degrees.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WERTZ (VP OF MANUFACTURING AT BLENKO GLASS)\par \par \uc2 You have to be touch and passionate.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tough and passionate, and this is the beautiful products that come out of this. So incredible. We sent one back to the studio for you guys. We are not done yet. I am actually gonna try my hand at stamping some glass. We're going to make a special sun catcher coming up. And I have to shout out this wonderful crowd behind us, guys, because this is all part of the Blenko family. You can see here, just very proud to be from West Virginia, guys.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is cool.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm so glad they're keeping that alive.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What a beautiful tradition, and they're really gorgeous. Thank you so much, Rhiannon.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260403em3k0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 IRAN WAR IMPACT ON GAS DISMAL JOBS REPORT RATTLES MARKET\b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, SEVERE\par \par \uc2 WAR, IRAN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, SOLDIERS, KILLED, SERVICE\par \par \uc2 IRAN, WAR, GAS, PRICES\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the war is already having an impact here at home. The war is threatening the global energy markets, sending gas prices soaring and rattling Wall Street. ABC's Andrew Dymburt joins us now with the latest on that. Andrew, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IRAN WAR IMPACT ON GAS\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. The combination of rising oil prices tied to tensions in the Middle East and a weak labor market could have wider implications for the US economy, casting fresh uncertainty in the weeks and months ahead.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, growing concerns about the US economy, with oil prices surging, and the war in Iran crippling exports.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DE HAAN\uc2  (HEAD OF PETROLEUM ANALYSIS AT GASBUDDY)\par \par \uc2 Oil prices have jumped their most amount in a week since 1983.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Crude prices soaring 35 percent this week alone, the biggest gain ever in futures trading history for the commodity.\par \par \uc2 GAS STATION CUSTOMER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's crazy. It's not needed, especially at a time when people are already struggling.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The impact already showing up at the pump. The national average for a gallon of gas, around $3.40 a gallon, jumping 40 cents in just one week. It's this narrow waterway, the Strait of Hormuz, off Iran's southern coast, turning the oil market upside down. One-fifth of the entire world's oil supply flows through here. Pressure building elsewhere in the US economy too, after a weaker than expected jobs report shows the US lost 92,000 jobs in February. Losses hitting health care hard, where nurse strikes weighed on hiring. Government and information sectors also impacted. Unemployment ticking up to 4.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK (CHIEF ECONOMIST & MANAGING DIRECTOR AT KPMG)\par \par \uc2 Right now, workers are dealing with is an insecurity in their jobs. At the same time that the effects of inflation over the course of five years have compounded.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DISMAL JOBS REPORT RATTLES MARKET\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Meanwhile, the Federal Reserve meets in two weeks and faces the critical decision on whether or not to cut interest rates, with the backdrop of weaker hiring and unstable oil prices renewing fears of inflation.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's been a roller coaster on Wall Street, the Dow suffering its worst week in nearly a year, Janai.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. All right, Andrew, thank you for those updates.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260327em370005q\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 IRAN WAR IMPACT ON GAS DISMAL JOBS REPORT RATTLES MARKET\b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, SEVERE\par \par \uc2 WAR, IRAN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, SOLDIERS, KILLED, SERVICE\par \par \uc2 IRAN, WAR, GAS, PRICES\par \par \uc2 UTAH, MAN, CHARGED, KILLING, MILLER\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN, COACH, MOORE, PLEA, DEAL\par \par \uc2 BANGOR, PLANE, CRASH, REPORT\par \par \uc2 DAYLIGHT SAVING TIME, SPRING, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 WORLD BASEBALL CLASSIC\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP OF "HOPPERS"\par \par \uc2 JACKSON, HOMEGOING, SERVICE\par \par \uc2 ICE, FROZEN, LAKE, POND, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 "HOPPERS", STARS\par \par \uc2 ROCKY, DOG, SUMMIT LOST PET RESCUE\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER, PIPER, TORNADO, HEARTLAND, OKLAHOMA, MICHIGAN, DETROIT, UNION LAKE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, IRAN, ISRAEL, HEGSETH, RUSSIA, USS GERALD R. FORD, PENTAGON, 82ND AIRBORNE\par \par \uc2 WALL STREET, $3.40 A GALLON, STRAIT OF HORMUZ, US ECONOMY, 4.4% UNEMPLOYMENT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, not clear on that. Matt Rivers for us, we appreciate it. And the war is already having an impact here at home, the conflict threatening the global energy markets, and in gas prices soaring and rattling Wall Street. ABC's Andrew Dymburt joins us now with the latest. Andrew, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IRAN WAR IMPACT ON GAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ECONOMIC WOES\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. Growing concerns about the US economy, crude prices soaring 35% this week alone.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The biggest gain ever in futures trading history for the commodity. The impact already being felt at the pump. The national average for a gallon of gas around $3.40 a gallon, jumping forty cents in just one week. This narrow waterway off the southern coast of Iran is causing all this commodity chaos. The Strait of Hormuz, it's - one-fifth of the world's entire oil supply flows through here, and elsewhere in the US economy, there's also pause.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAS PRICES RISING\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DYMBURT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) After a weaker-than-expected jobs report shows the US lost 92,000 jobs in February, losses hitting health care hard, where nurse strikes weighed on hiring, government and information sectors also impacted, unemployment ticking up to 4.4%. It was also a rough week on Wall Street too. The Dow losing almost 1,300 points, it's all this economic uncertainty. Janai?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DISMAL JOBS REPORT RATTLES MARKET\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEBRUARY JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. UNEMPLOYMENT RATE\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260327em3700067\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GAS PRICES SURGE AMID WAR WITH IRAN HOW WILL WAR IMPACT PRICES AT HOME?\b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRIKES INTENSIFY\par \par \uc2 STRIKES, KHORK, AMOR, TIETJENS, COADY, DEATHS\par \par \uc2 WAR, STRANDED, AMERICANS, FLIGHTS, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 WAR, GAS, PRICES, INFLATION, OIL\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, putting a lot of people in a scary situation there. James, thank you. We're going to turn now to the economic impact of the war. It's a wild ride for stocks yesterday, the DOW plunging more than 1,000 points before recouping some of its losses. Elizabeth Schulze is here in the studio with us. And, Elizabeth, we've seen another big move in gas prices this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAS PRICES SURGE AMID WAR WITH IRAN\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A big move. Good morning, Michael. This is something that families will notice when they go to fill up. Gas Buddy says the national average price for a gallon of gas is up about 25 cents, just in the past couple of days. We're now looking at the average price per gallon between $3.17 and $3.19. And these are just some of the biggest price hikes on record that we're seeing right now. They do expect prices to keep going up in the next couple of days and weeks if the war does not deescalate another 25e cents, as much as that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But what does this mean for overall inflation?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. So gas prices have really been a bright spot in the economy, one of the places where prices have been falling over the past year. But now we have wiped out the drop in gas prices since President Trump returned in office. The President acknowledged that Americans could see higher oil prices. But this is not just about gas. When you think about rising fuel costs that means that companies are paying more to transport goods.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW WILL WAR IMPACT PRICES AT HOME?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Economists that we've been talking to say that we could start to see higher food costs in the next days or weeks if companies pass down those transport costs. Another place we could see prices go up pretty quickly is airfares, both international and domestic flights.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah you know, the White House they're promising naval escorts and insurance to oil and gas tankers going through the through the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, the White House wants to get that oil moving again and that critical oil passageway. So right now the President is saying that the Navy will provide those escorts if necessary. They are also offering to give insurance from a fund that is backed by the \uc2 US Government\uc2  to shipping companies to try to incentivize them to get that oil moving. But shipping companies still might not want to take that risk. They do not want to put their cargo and their crew in safety's harm if they think that it's a risk. And it's not clear.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we're not seeing the movement yet.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. Those ships are still solid, it's basically right now a parking lot in the Strait of Hormuz. That oil is just not moving. The longer that goes on, the higher prices could go.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I could imagine if your crew on that ship and they're saying, well go. I don't feel safe. It's very hard to get people to put their lives at risk for oil.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260325em340002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE IMPORTANCE OF BUILDING GENERATIONAL WEALTH BLACK HISTORY MONTH\b0\par\par\uc2 1,438 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 IRAN, RETALIATORY, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN, LAWMAKER, REACTION\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 CLINTON, DEPOSED, EPSTEIN\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, U.S., IRAN, ISRAEL, WAR, NETANYAHU, KHAMENEI, EPIC FURY,\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IRAN, ISRAEL, WAR, MAR-A-LAGO, WHITE HOUSE, AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, AL UDEID, AL-ASAD, SOLEIMANI, AIRBUS, MIDDLE EAST, BAHRAIN, KUWAIT, QATAR\par \par \uc2 GANYARD, KARBLER, RUBIO, TRUMP, \uc2 IAEA\uc2 , US, ISRAEL, IRAN, GULF, RUSSIA, CHINA\par \par \uc2 IRAN, DOJ, TRUMP, US, ISIS ALQAEDA, CYBER-ATTACKS, SYNAGOGUES, CHURCHES\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, IRAN, AL UDEID AIR BASE, QATAR, EMBASSY, JORDAN, BAHRAIN, KUWAIT\par \par \uc2 IRAN, ISRAEL, TRUMP, MEASLES, CDC, \uc2 WEBER\uc2 , GRILL, CPSC, WARNER BROS., PARAMOUNT\par \par \uc2 DUNLAP, LIFE INSURANCE, GENERATIONAL WEALTH, GI BILL, INVESTMENT\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, now to our ongoing celebration of Black History Month. And this morning, we are looking at the importance of creating generational wealth, financial and investment expert Ian Dunlap. You might know him as the master investor on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . He joins us. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE IMPORTANCE OF BUILDING GENERATIONAL WEALTH\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's talk about the importance of building generational wealth and some of the historical or systemic barriers that have made that more challenging for Black families.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 I mean, of course, you can start with slavery. The GI Bill, of course, second. If you look at the housing market, redlining has affected that. And now, more recently, DEI erasure. So, if we look systemically, those are some of the challenges that we've had with being able to build wealth at a large scale.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what are some of the key first steps for people to get started?\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Number 1, financial literacy is important. So, take 10 minutes every day to learn about the market. Number 2, invest in index funds. So, a broad basket of companies to invest in, so you don't have to guess which company to invest in. And then third, pick two technology companies that you love, even though \uc2 Warner Bros.\uc2  and Paramount are now having a marriage. Netflix is still a great stock. Ironically enough, the stock went up on the news.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 So, invest in two technology companies to index funds, and you'll be fine.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GETTING STARTED WITH INVESTING\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's really good advice. And as we were talking about this in high school.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When I was 17 years old. I mean, they were just drilling this into me, these mentors, they were telling me how important this...\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...financial literacy was. What is it that families need to do to, to give their kids that financial literacy?\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 I tell every parent what I did with my - son, I started with seven minutes every Saturday. I would just go through five companies. Hey, this is \uc2 Apple\uc2 , this is \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , this is what it does. Number 2, when you're doing your bills and doing your shopping, have them actually pay and tell them what things cost, so they understand the value. So, like if my son asks me for VC for 2K or Skins on Fortnite, I'm like, this is eighty dollars. We can either buy this for this game, or we can put it here somewhere else in the future, and it gives them a deeper relationship with money.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do they choose?\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Oh, always a Skins.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Always. But third, I think it's important to have the conversation early, because too often we wait until they're 15 or 20 to have the money conversations, and they're left behind. If you start as early as three, they'll get it by the time they're seven or eight years old.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That makes a big difference...\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...because then you've got adults who are trying to figure this out too.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, for anyone watching at home, at any point in their career, how can they get started?\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Number 1, most important thing is to have your investments automated, because too often we - worry about a crash or pullback. Should we invest? Invest every month, you could automate that. Number 2, there's so many amazing technology companies that you can invest in. You only need two. And then thirdly, you don't need millions of dollars. You can start with a hundred bucks. And then the thing I found very interesting, once it works one time, you find money from other areas to then allocate into the market, and then you can see...\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because you're encouraged.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so important because many people just feel like they're priced out.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Like, this isn't for me, I can't afford it. But what are some of the other big mistakes that people make that prevent them from accomplishing this generational wealth?\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Number 1, I have a life insurance, that's something so small of not having a life insurance policy. No conversations in the family about financial literacy, because if you had down, let's say, $100,000, if your family member doesn't know what to do with it, it's gonna get wasted. And then third, no estate planning, like you have to have a plan for how the money is passed down. You need a trust. You need a will. So just having these conversations more often on a day like today is really important. So, you can have a plan to pass it down...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BARRIERS TO CREATING GENERATIONAL WEALTH\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then those, sorry to interrupt, but just on that point, those are very difficult conversations, right?\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're talking about, you know, the end of your life, and what to do with your assets, and stuff like that.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Perhaps, how do you approach those conversations in a meaningful way?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a good question.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 I think it's important to start early. You may not wanna bring up the death conversation, but to equip them if anything happens. This is where you can find the life insurance information.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Here's the estate planner. You have to have them. If not, sometimes properties end up in probate. You don't want that, and then you may have family members who may say, "Well, this was left to me,"...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 ...and they may take them from your child. You don't want that theft from your investment portfolio, that's happened to you.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it is facing those...\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...tough conversations head-on. We appreciate you being with us, Ian.\par \par \uc2 IAN DUNLAP (INVESTMENT & FINANCIAL ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you. And still coming up here on "GMA," a new report warns of rising heart disease risk for women of all ages. Dr. Leah Croll is here to break down what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260323em2s0006a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 U.S. & ISRAEL STRIKE IRAN IRAN HAS LAUNCHED RETALIATORY STRIKES IN THE REGION\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 IRAN, RETALIATORY, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN, LAWMAKER, REACTION\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 US, ISRAEL, STRIKE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 CLINTON, DEPOSED, EPSTEIN\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, U.S., IRAN, ISRAEL, WAR, NETANYAHU, KHAMENEI, EPIC FURY,\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, IRAN, ISRAEL, WAR, MAR-A-LAGO, WHITE HOUSE, AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, AL UDEID, AL-ASAD, SOLEIMANI, AIRBUS, MIDDLE EAST, BAHRAIN, KUWAIT, QATAR\par \par \uc2 GANYARD, KARBLER, RUBIO, TRUMP, \uc2 IAEA\uc2 , US, ISRAEL, IRAN, GULF, RUSSIA, CHINA\par \par \uc2 IRAN, DOJ, TRUMP, US, ISIS ALQAEDA, CYBER-ATTACKS, SYNAGOGUES, CHURCHES\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, IRAN, AL UDEID AIR BASE, QATAR, EMBASSY, JORDAN, BAHRAIN, KUWAIT\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Pierre Thomas, our appreciation to you for being with us this morning. We are going to turn now. As we mentioned, Iran has launched retaliatory strikes in the region. ABC's Marcus Moore joins us from Doha this morning, where those retaliatory strikes in places like Jordan, Bahrain, Kuwait, and in Qatar, Marcus.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S & ISRAEL STRIKE IRAN\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Janai. These are retaliatory strikes that were expected. We are here in Qatar, where in Doha, Qatar, where we have heard sporadic loud booms. Those air defense interceptors going out into the air. And this is part of the retaliation from Iran to intercept those, those missiles.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But many of them, officials believe, headed to the \u8211\'20\'13 Al Udeid Air Base here in Qatar, the largest American base in the Middle East, where there are thousands of US personnel there. That place expected to be a target for the Iranians. And it certainly is adding to the tension here in the region. There have been emergency alert messages that have been sent out to cell phones, Janai, urging people to stay away from military sites and also to shelter in place.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Those messages going out to the US embassy as well. And we also understand that the airspace is being closed in a number of countries, giving you a sense of how tense the situation is here. And there's real concern of this situation, and you may have just heard, just behind, just behind me, with the sound of an explosion. That's what we've been hearing all day today, along with the residents here who are waiting to see what happens next, hoping this doesn't escalate.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As we're seeing, the entire region get pulled into this conflict.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Marcus Moore, for us, we appreciate it. We are watching all of the developments on this major breaking news event. We'll bring you the very latest coming up in just a moment. For now, though, we do wanna turn and get another check of the weather. Meteorologist Cheryl Scott from our Chicago station, WLS, is here for the weather picture. And we've had this had the storms, but some changes on the way.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260323em2s00067\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMAZON ANNOUNCES NEW JOB CUTS AMAZON JOINS OTHER CORPORATE GIANTS SLASHING JOBS\b0\par\par\uc2 471 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, PRETTI, ICE, CARLSON, VIDEO, REPORT, DHS\par \par \uc2 OMAR, TOWN HALL, ATTACK, LIQUID, SUSPECT, ARREST\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ICE, ENFORCEMENT, SHOOTING, FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 STORM, TEMPERATURES, WIND CHILL, SNOW, NOR'EASTER\par \par \uc2 CARBON MONOXIDE, POISONINGS, TIPS, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN AIRLINES, CANCELATIONS, STORM, ATTENDANTS, PILOTS\par \par \uc2 GAUFF, AUSTRALIAN OPEN, LOSS, RACQUET, WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "DIRTY DANCING"\par \par \uc2 COLLISION, HELICOPTER, PLANE, \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , HOMENDY,\par \par \uc2 MURDERS, TEPE, MCKEE, ARREST, THREATS, MENASHE\par \par \uc2 SCAM, GHOST STUDENTS, WILLIAMS, MACKLIN, SIMPKINS\par \par \uc2 PENGUINS, MYSTIC, CHICKS, ENDANGERED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FREEZING FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 PRETTI, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, JOB, CUTS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to breaking news this morning. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  announcing a new round of layoffs. The latest big company to cut its workforce. Our chief business correspondent Rebecca Jarvis joins us with those details. Good morning to you, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON ANNOUNCES NEW JOB CUTS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, Robin, and this is despite a booming business. This morning, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  announcing they will be cutting 16,000 jobs. This is the second round of layoffs in just three months. The retail giant let 14,000 corporate employees go in October. They sent a message to workers today, and the company says they have been "working to strengthen the organization by reducing layers, increasing ownership and removing bureaucracy with organizational changes that started in October and they are now complete."\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Amazon\uc2  ensured employees their plan is not to make broad cuts every few months, but that every team will continue to evaluate and make adjustments as needed, adding that it's "never been more important than it is today in a world that is changing faster than ever."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON JOINS OTHER CORPORATE GIANTS SLASHING JOBS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Last summer, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  CEO \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2  said he expects \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's corporate workforce to change because of artificial intelligence, and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  isn't alone here. We are seeing a number of different types of companies recently announcing cuts as they focus more heavily on AI. From \uc2 Pinterest\uc2 , which just announced it would lay off less than 15 percent of its workforce and reduce office space, to \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  parent \uc2 Meta\uc2 , to \uc2 Nike\uc2  and UPS, which plans to reduce its workforce by 30,000 this year. And Michael, I just want to say, if you are a job seeker in this market, we will continue to help you and hold your hand through all of this. We know it's tough out there, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it sure is. AI is changing the world and not always in the best ways for most people.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260213em1s0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LARGEST-EVER NYC NURSES' STRIKE NEARLY 15,000 WALK OFF THE JOB AT MAJOR HOSPITALS\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GOOD, ROSS, LAWSUIT, FREY, ELLISON, ICE, LEAVITT\par \par \uc2 IRAN, TRUMP, ECONOMY, PROTESTS, TARIFF, U-HAUL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICANS WARNED TO LEAVE IRAN\par \par \uc2 POWELL, THILLIS, TRUMP, INTEREST RATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOJ INVESTIGATION FED CHAIRMAN POWELL\par \par \uc2 SYNAGOGUE, ARSON, ARREST, PITTMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURVEILLANCE VIDEO OF SYNAGOGUE ATTACK\par \par \uc2 TEPE, MURDER, MCKEE, ARREST, KOLARIK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EX-HUSBAND ARRESTED IN COUPLE'S MURDER\par \par \uc2 NURSES' STRIKE, CARR, NEGOTIATION, MAMDANI, STAFFING, WAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LARGEST-EVER NYC NURSES' STRIKE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In New York City tonight, the largest nurses strike in this city's history. Tonight, the new mayor, Zohran Mamdani, speaking out on behalf of the nurses and what they're demanding. Here's ABC's Rhiannon Ally.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY 15,000 WALK OFF THE JOB AT MAJOR HOSPITALS\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the largest nursing strike in New York City's history, nearly 15,000 nurses walking off the job and on to the picket line.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Nurses united.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 NEW YORK STATE NURSES ASSOCIATION\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The union striking three leading hospital systems, Montefiore, Mount Sinai and New York-Presbyterian, demanding higher pay and benefits, and increased staffing and ore security after a string of violent incidents at hospitals.\par \par \uc2 DANIA MUNOZ (NURSE PRACTITIONER)\par \par \uc2 Make sure that we can staff the hospitals appropriately and make sure they're putting patients before profit.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, the city's new mayor, Zohran Mamdani, showing support with a visit to the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ZOHRAN MAMDANI (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 They show up and all they are asking for in return is dignity, respect and a fair pay and treatment that they deserve.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The strike comes in the middle of a severe flu season with New York hospitals reporting a record number of patients. Some hospitals now bringing in outside nurses. \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  already cancelling some elective procedures.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN CARR (CEO OF MOUNT SINAI HEALTH SYSTEM)\par \par \uc2 We will be able to run safely at our hospitals, our outpatient practices, but there will be changes.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, David, tonight, negotiations are ongoing. Meanwhile, those affected hospitals are urging people not to delay treatment if they have a medical emergency. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rhiannon Ally here in New York tonight. Rhiannon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CENSURE FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN. KELLY SUES SECY. HEGSETH\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, Arizona Senator Mark Kelly is suing Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  for attempting to punish him and reduce Kelly's military retirement rank.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kelly now suing Hegseth, the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , the Secretary of the Navy, and the Navy itself. He claims Hegseth's attempt to punish him for a video warning troops not to follow, quote, "illegal orders" violates Kelly's First Amendment rights. Senator Kelly says his job is to hold the administration accountable.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back here tonight, the terrifying fall for a paraglider off the US tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The images right here this evening, the dramatic 500-foot plunge. In New York City, the runaway carriage horse dashing into traffic in Manhattan and then the accident. Also, the images getting a lot of attention tonight. The wild face-off between the rhino and the deer and how this ends here. And of course, tonight's big game, the Texans, the Steelers. Joe Buck standing by from Pittsburgh. The game here on ABC. He's got the stakes in a moment here.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020260114em1c00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 16,000 NURSES SET TO STRIKE MONDAY IN NEW YORK CITY UNION AND MAJOR HOSPITALS REACHES TENTATIVE AGREEMENT\b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ICE PROTESTS ERUPT\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, GOOD, ROSS, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 IRAN, TRUMP, ANTI- PROTESTS, SHEINBAUM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DAY 14 OF ANTI-GOVERNMENT PROTESTS IN IRAN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, IRAN, BOMB, NUCLEAR SITES, PROTESTS, ICE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP'S IRAN THREAT\par \par \uc2 TEPE, MCKEE, MURDER, ARREST\par \par \uc2 NURSES' STRIKE, CONTRACT NEGOTIATIONS, HOCHUL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 16,000 NURSES SET TO STRIKE MONDAY IN NEW YORK CITY\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, this morning, we are hours away from what could be a historic strike by 16,000 nurses here in New York City. ABC's Ike Ejiochi has the latest. Good morning, Ike. A lot of people watching this.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, Janai. Good morning. New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has issued an executive order declaring the disaster as nearly 16,000 nurses at some of New York City's largest private hospitals gear up to go on strike tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSSIBLE NYC NURSE STRIKE\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A source telling ABC News that the nurses are expected to walk out around 6:00 AM Monday at several city hospitals, including New York Presbyterian, Mount Sinai Hospital, Mount Sinai West, Morningside, and Montefiore Einstein. Now, the union's three-year contract with 12 New York City hospitals expired at the end of last year. Now, the nurses' union is pushing for a better staffing ratios, protection of benefits, pay increases and stronger workplace security guarantees, pointing to recent shootings at city hospitals. At the same time, the union says it reached tentative agreements with several New York City safety net hospitals last week with some facilities already rescinding strike notices after reaching deals. Officials and hospital leaders stress patients should not avoid or delay seeking help for medical emergencies. Hospitals will remain open and prepared to offer care even if the strike goes forward.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, Governor Hochul is urging both sides to stay at the table until the issues are resolved. Gio?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Ike, let's hope they resolve that very quickly.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260116em1b0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NYC HOSPITALS BRACE FOR POTENTIAL NURSE STRIKE THOUSANDS COULD WALK OFF THE JOB MONDAY MORNING\b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 IRAN, PROTESTS, VIOLENCE, CRACKDOWN, ACTIVISTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTESTERS DEFY VIOLENT GOVT CRACKDOWN\par \par \uc2 IRANIANS, PROTESTS, TRUMP, KAINE, PAUL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP WEIGHING POSSIBLE MILITARY ACTION\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, PROTESTS, ICE, GOOD, NOEM, SMITH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HUNDREDS MORE AGENTS TO DEPLOY TO MINNEAPOLIS\par \par \uc2 ARSON, SUSPECT, SYNAGOGUE, HISTORIC, ARREST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECT FACING CHARGES IN ARSON ATTACK\par \par \uc2 ARREST, MURDERS, TEPE, MCKEE, CHARGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS IN DENTIST COUPLE KILLINGS\par \par \uc2 NURSES, STRIKE, NYC, HOCHUL, FLU, BROWNSTEIN, HOSPITALIZATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NYC HOSPITALS BRACE FOR POTENTIAL NURSE STRIKE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Olivia, thank you. Here in New York, thousands of nurses are set to walk off the job in just a matter of hours. The Governor declared a state of emergency ahead of the possible strike, and she is pleading with both sides to keep negotiating. The latest now from ABC's Ike Ejiochi.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS COULD WALK OFF THE JOB MONDAY MORNING\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, New York City is bracing for impact, 16,000 nurses are set to strike tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 NURSE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We need respect. We want it now. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The union saying the walkout will happen unless there is progress in negotiations with several city hospitals in the next few hours. Other hospitals settled with the union last week. It would be the largest nurses strike in city history. New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has declared a state of emergency ahead of the potential strike, pleading with both sides to stay at the table.\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 This could jeopardize the lives of thousands of New Yorkers and patients.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) All of this comes amid a spike in respiratory illnesses nationwide, the CDC estimates there have been 15 million flu-related illnesses, 180,000 hospitalizations, and 7,400 deaths this season so far. 42 states, including New York, are seeing high or very high levels of flu activity.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHIC: LOOMING PROTESTS COMES AMID RECORD BREAKING FLU SEASON\par \par \uc2 JOHN BROWNSTEIN PHD (EPIDEMIOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 We are seeing record-breaking hospitalizations and cases in New York City and many other parts of this country.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The nurses want higher pay and safer working conditions. Mount Sinai, one the hospitals, is promising any potential walkout will not affect the care of patients.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Linsey, should a strike happen, state officials are urging everyone not to avoid or delay seeking medical attention in an emergency. Linsey.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020260113em1b00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE FIX IN '26: GETTING A NEW JOB KEYS TO A GOOD JOB SEARCH\b0\par\par\uc2 1,031 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ICE, PROTESTS, GOOD\par \par \uc2 OIL, TANKER, SEIZED\par \par \uc2 IRAN, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SWITZERLAND, BAR, FIRE\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE, DEFENSE, DEATH, PENALTY\par \par \uc2 PARENTS, ARRESTED, ABUSE, DAVIS\par \par \uc2 TEPE, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 POLICE, CARS, FRONT LOADER\par \par \uc2 INDIANA, PEACH BOWL, MIAMI\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF BRUNO MARS MUSIC VIDEO\par \par \uc2 ICE, SHOOTING, PROTESTS, GOOD\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, EDWARDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDCARD WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DISNEYWORLD, MARATHON, WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GOOD, FREY, ICE, ROSS, VANCE, PROTEST, SHOOTING, MINNEAPOLIS, KILLING\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , FIFTH OIL TANKER, VENEZUELA, NAVY, MARINES, MADURO, US MILITARY\par \par \uc2 CDC, SUBCLADE K, FLU VACCINE, MEASLES OUTBREAK, SOUTH CAROLINA,\par \par \uc2 INDIANA HOOSIERS, OREGON DUCKS, PEACH BOWL, NATIONAL CHAMPIONSHIP, MIAMI\par \par \uc2 DURHAM, JOB SEARCH, EVENTBRITE, LINKEDIN, NETWORKING, 10% RULE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back now with our new series, "The Fix in '26." Data released yesterday showed a slowdown in job growth last year amid a slew of layoffs, so here with what you need to know about scoring a new job or keeping the one you have is Emily Durham, a former job recruiter, with insider tips on what works and what doesn't. Emily, thank you for being here. This...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FIX IN '26: GETTING A NEW JOB\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...must be on the mind of so many Americans. So, what are some key strategies for job seekers in the current market?\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 So, I have three.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 The first is we need to be networking, but with the right people. Stop reaching out to recruiters. They're inundated with messages. Instead, let's go to decision makers like leadership and managers. Let's also go to niche networking events. No more career fairs, okay?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEYS TO A GOOD JOB SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Let's get in-house. Niche industry events. You can \uc2 Google\uc2  those, lots on \uc2 Eventbrite\uc2  to check those out, and I'm a big believer in being a LinkedIn influencer. Don't be scared...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oooohh.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 ...to share and repost. Make sure people know your name because you're active online.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, that's so smart. And, and part of that is really getting rid of all of these, like, old strategies that we used to have...\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...bringing new things in. So, what are your lists of don'ts?\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 I am personally over customizing a resume.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 It is happening because people are able to do it at a high volume with AI.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 So, it no longer means anything.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 I no longer am advising you reach out to recruiters. They're not responding, my friends. And I'm also just a big believer, again, in that strategic networking piece as a, a really, really big one. And I also think this is just the time to ditch the high-volume applications, because again...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LESS EFFECTIVE IN 2026\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 ...they're not getting opened at the same rate. Let's focus on networking.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, so, so less is more almost...\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...in putting that energy into it. So, moving on to keeping a job.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) With so many layoffs in the news, what's your advice here?\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Oh, first of all, be gentle with yourself.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right? Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Because this is a really difficult time. I'm a big believer in the 10% rule. You need to ask yourself, what does my employer lose if they lose me as a person? I think 10% of your workweek needs to be spent on highly visible work, so the stuff your boss is gonna hear about...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEEPING YOUR JOB\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 ...or the stuff that's slightly outside of the scope of your job, that gives you...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oooohh.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 ...a little razzle-dazzle. Maybe you lean into an employee resource group, maybe a mentor someone. That's the stuff that helps you stick around. And also, be an office celebrity. Be the person people want to work with, they admire.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Have that personality and small talk with the right people when you're in person, especially.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What, what I hear you say is that your biggest tip is connection.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Human connection, right?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Yes, in the age of AI, the only thing that's setting us apart is the ability to have relationships. That's really it.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You narrowed that down so perfectly and poignantly. In a world of AI, human connection is what we've got. Emily, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was really great.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DURHAM (HOST "CLOCK IN" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really great. Thank you so much. All right, time now for the weather and meteorologist Dani Beckstrom, our own office celebrity.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260130em1a0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW LAWS FOR 2026 INCREASE IN MINIMUM WAGE AND DECREASE IN EPIPEN COST TAKES EFFECT\b0\par\par\uc2 645 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAPPY NEW YEAR!\par \par \uc2 RAIN, COLD, SNOW, PARADE\par \par \uc2 SWISS, FIRE, SKI, RESORT\par \par \uc2 BOAT, STRIKES, VIDEO\par \par \uc2 ACA\uc2 , HEALTH, PREMIUMS, TRUMP, SCOTUS, GUARD\par \par \uc2 MAMDANI, NEW YORK, MAYOR, QURAN\par \par \uc2 "ROCKIN' EVE", SEACREST, ROSS, CAREY, MALONE, CHANCE, KPOP, PITBULL, GRONK\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, FOOTBALL, PLAYOFFS, MIAMI, OHIO, OREGON, TEXAS, ALABAMA, INDIANA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORM ALERTS\par \par \uc2 MOVIE CLIP FROM "THE MANDALORIAN AND GROGU"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "THE MANDALORIAN AND GROGU" IS PRODUCED BY A DIVISION OF \uc2 DISNEY\uc2 , ABC'S PARENT COMPANY\par \par \uc2 2026, CASES, MANGIONE, REINER, KIRK, \uc2 NBA\uc2 , BILLUPS, COMBS\par \par \uc2 NOLA, TERROR, ATTACK, QUIGLEY, MOWRER, MEMORIAL\par \par \uc2 2026, ENTERTAINMENT, MOVIES, MUSIC, TV\par \par \uc2 "NEW YEARS ROCKIN' EVE", BALL, DROP, "AMERICA250"\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM JACKSON, WYOMING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEXT WEEK'S PRECIP OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING FEARS FOR MILLIONS IN THE WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEEP FREEZE\par \par \uc2 FLASH FLOODING, RAIN, SNOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DRENCHING RAIN SPARKS FLASH FLOODING IN SOUTHERN CALIFORNIA\par \par \uc2 MORTGAGE, HOUSING PRICES, INTEREST RATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORTGAGE RATES AT 15-MONTH LOW\par \par \uc2 NEW LAWS, MINIMUM WAGE, EPIPEN, PHONE BAN, FAMILY MEDICAL LEAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW LAWS FOR 2026\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now to the new laws that take effect this morning from minimum wage to family medical leave. Olivia Rubin is here with the few that are making headlines right now. Olivia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary, good morning. Happy new year. Many waking up with their own personal resolutions this year. But these are some rules that can't just be broken in a few weeks. New state laws going into effect around the country this year. And this one, a welcome impact on your wallet.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MINIMUM WAGE INCREASES\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Minimum wage increases taking effect coast-to-coast in nearly 20 states that includes Nebraska and Missouri, you see it right there, jumping up to $15 an hour. And Washington state, which now has the highest statewide minimum wage in the country at $17.30.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And with many concerns about the rising health care cost, New York now implementing a new law in the lowering of the cost of EpiPens. Starting today, some health insurance companies are required by law to cover them with out-of-pocket costs now capped at $100 per year. And changes coming to some schools, too.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A new cell phone ban taking effect in Georgia by the end of the school year, which means no devices for students in kindergarten through 8th grade during school hours. Kids might not like that one. And, meanwhile, the new paid family medical leave programs going into effect in Minnesota, Maine, and Delaware, too.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In Delaware, eligible employees can now take up to 12 weeks per year. Some welcome news there for many.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Lots of changes coming up. Olivia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:14, WALLET WATCH\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And coming up in our "GMA Morning Menu," how to hang out with friends without breaking the bank and still have fun together.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:16, SHAKIRA TALKS "ZOOTOPIA 2"\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That's very important. Also ahead, Grammy-winning superstar Shakira talks "Zootopia 2."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:21, "ALADDIN" ACROSS GENERATIONS\par \par \uc2 MALIKA ANDREWS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, how the cast of "Aladdin" helped open up a whole new world and make a dream come true for some super fans.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:11, AMERICA 250\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And it is 2026 and that means it's the 250th birthday of America. How the year-long celebration kicked off overnight. We have all of the details that's all coming up right here on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260120em110000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MAJOR SKI RESORT CLOSED DUE TO STRIKE SKI PATROLS WALKS OFF THE JOB AS THEY DEMAND PAY INCREASE\b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 December 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WICKED WHITEOUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WINTER BLAST\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, SNOW, WIND, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WICKED WINTER STORM\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , VENEZUELA, DRUG BOAT, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP ANNOUNCES LAND STRIKE IN VENEZUELA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , WAR, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, PEACE PLAN, SECURITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF PLAY IN UKRAINE PEACE TALKS\par \par \uc2 OLMOS, SEARCH, SALAZAR, VIDEO, FLORES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEARCH FOR MISSING TEXAS TEENAGER\par \par \uc2 SKI RESORT, STRIKE, WAGE INCREASE, SKI PATROLS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR SKI RESORT CLOSED DUE TO STRIKE\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now to the strike shutting down a popular ski resort during peak season. Telluride, Colorado, a hot spot for winter sports. Hanna Battah is here with the story. Hey, Hanna, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Mary. Most certainly is with the New Year right around the corner, this is no doubt one of the busiest times of the year at ski resorts all around the country.\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the resort in Telluride, Colorado is closed for business as the ski patrol there has walked off the job. The union says it wants Telluride to raise pay for workers to be on par with other resorts in the region. It's demanding a median wage increase of $8 an hour over three years. Videos posted online show some of the strikers marching with those picket signs. The workers attribute the need for increased to the rising cost of living in Telluride, a town where the median price of a home is nearly $4 million. Workers staged a similar strike in Park City, Utah last year. It's unclear how long this strike will go on.\par \par \uc2 HANNA BATTAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The ski resort is already experienced challenges this season with the state's unusually warm weather this fall and winter. Malika?\par \par \uc2 MALIKA ANDREWS (ESPN NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260123elcu00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DELAYED SEPTEMBER JOBS REPORT SET TO BE RELEASED MAJOR COMPANIES OFFER INDICATION OF OVERALL ECONOMY\b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 November 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT SIGNS EPSTEIN FILES BILL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , EPSTEIN, FILES, BILL, SCHUMER, MASSIE, BONDI, GUIFFRE\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, JOBS, REPORT, STOCKS, RETAIL, AI\par \par \uc2 COMEY, CASE, HALLIGAN\par \par \uc2 CHERFILUS, STEALING, FEMA, BONDI, MILLS\par \par \uc2 WAR, UKRAINE, PEACE, PLAN, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 TSA, AIRPORT, ID, FEE\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , SANDERS, BREAK-IN, BILLS, TEXANS\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM CMA AWARDS\par \par \uc2 JACKSON, ESTATE, GLASS, BIOPIC, "MICHAEL"\par \par \uc2 CMAS, WILSON, GREEN, TOP, JACKSON, FRANKLIN\par \par \uc2 LARSSON, CONCERT, COSTER, VIRAL, DANCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF THE ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, JOBS REPORT, STOCKS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right now, we have a look at the top stories "Breaking at 8."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We start with the new jobs report this morning after a six-week delay due to government shutdown. I want to go back to Rebecca Jarvis on Wall Street. Good morning, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DELAYED SEPTEMBER JOBS REPORT SET TO BE RELEASED\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, good morning again to you, George. And we are seeing stocks pointing higher ahead of that long-awaited report. It was delayed six weeks due to the government shutdown, and it's really the most important piece of data we've received since then on the jobs picture.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Analysts are anticipating the economy added 50,000 jobs in the month of September. That's a little weak and softer than you'd like to see, but we also have to combine that with data just out this morning from \uc2 Walmart\uc2  which reported stronger-than-expected earnings and saying that customers across income spectrums are shopping their stores. The parent company of T.J. Maxx and Marshall's also said that their holiday season sales are off to a strong start.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR COMPANIES OFFER INDICATION OF OVERALL ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) At the same time, we've heard some weakness from retailers like \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , Lowe's and Target who have all reported weaker expectations for the time going forward. And that's because they say of the uncertainty facing the consumer. There is one thing that is clearly booming here on Wall Street and that is artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The company \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  reporting stronger-than-anticipated earnings overnight and that company's stock is booming this morning here. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the trillions. In the trillions, as you told us earlier.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Rebecca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251215elbk0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DELAYED SEPTEMBER JOBS REPORT SET TO BE RELEASED MAJOR COMPANIES OFFER INDICATION OF OVERALL ECONOMY\b0\par\par\uc2 297 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 November 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT SIGNS EPSTEIN FILES BILL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, EPSTEIN, FILES, BILL, SCHUMER, MASSIE, BONDI, GUIFFRE\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, JOBS, REPORT, STOCKS, RETAIL, AI\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Means so much to those families. All right, Mary, thank you. Now to the economy and the long-awaited September jobs support after the government shutdown delayed it. Chief Business Correspondent Rebecca Jarvis is on Wall Street with more for us. Good morning, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DELAYED SEPTEMBER JOBS REPORT SET TO BE RELEASED\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, Robin. And stocks here on Wall Street are pointing higher ahead of that long-awaited jobs report delayed six weeks due to the government shutdown. It's really the first piece of major data investors will see about the economy since the government shutdown, and they are anticipating a softer report on jobs, 50 thousand jobs added in the month. We also have \uc2 Walmart\uc2  data out this morning. The mega retailer reporting sales and earnings that beat expectations and they say that customers across the income spectrum are turning to them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR COMPANIES OFFER INDICATION OF OVERALL ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We've also heard from a number of other retailers that have shared a slightly different story. \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , Lowe's and Target all seeing their customer, their core consumer facing that economic uncertainty and seeing some weakness there. But one part of the economy here is clearly booming. That is artificial intelligence. The mega company there, the tech trade \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , that stock pointing dramatically higher this morning after it beat expectations. It is nearly a five trillion-dollar company now, the biggest in the world. Michael?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251215elbk00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 7:30 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN ENDS\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, TRUMP, BILL, ACA, JAYAPAL, DAVIS, PRICE, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN, EMAILS, GRIJALVA, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 JACKSON, PSP, PARKINSON'S, KING, RAINBOW, PUSH\par \par \uc2 MLB, PITCHER, CLASE, ORTIZ, GAMBLING\par \par \uc2 SNACKS, FRITO-LAY, NKD, NAKED, DAYGLO\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , GRONK, PATRIOTS, JETS, MAYE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JETS & PATRIOTS SET TO FACE OFF\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MOVIE CLIP FROM "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA 2"\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here on "GMA," whoo, your first look...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...at "The Devil Wears Prada Two."\par \par \uc2 ANNE HATHAWAY (ACTOR/"THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA 2")\par \par \uc2 Miranda.\par \par \uc2 MERYL STREEP (ACTOR/"THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA 2")\par \par \uc2 Took you long enough.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh. Oh.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, I like (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is a teaser trailer, aw, the teaser trailer for, Meryl Streep and Anne Hathaway are back. It's been nearly 20 years since the first movie. And this sequel, we'll see the return of Emily Blunt and Stanley Tucci, along with some new faces.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So excited.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "The Devil Wears Prada Two" officially hits theaters on May first. That is the Friday right before the first Monday in May.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which is?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Gala, the Met Gala.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, the Met Gala.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Coincidence, I think not.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think not. I'm really excited.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2  WORKERS ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No way. We are looking forward to that. First, New at 7:30, unionized \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas went on strike this morning at locations around the country, pushing for higher pay and the elimination of what the union calls unfair labor practices. The union calling it the Red Cup Rebellion as the strike coincides with the chain's popular red cup promotion. Earlier, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  expressed disappointment that the union authorized the strike and pointed out that it represents only a small percentage of the company's workers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BASE JUMPERS' CLIFFSIDE RESCUE\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also, take a look at this daring rescue to save a base jumper dangling from the side of a cliff in Utah. His parachute was tangled in the rocky terrain. Rescuers were able to reach him and safely bring him down.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And take a look at this, familiar music right there to pump up the crowd. "Rocky" theme song gave us all hope for that Russian humanoid robot powered by AI. It's doing great so far.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not really. He looks like he's drunk.\par \par \uc2 "GMA" CO-HOST (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh!\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh!\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is that real? Did that really happen?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, it did. It was in the - archival (PH) center in Russia.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Moscow.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Was there not a dress rehearsal? Oh, no, the black...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We saw it, buddy. We saw it.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (inaudible). But you got a, there's a cleanup on aisle four after that too.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know the money they spent on that sucker? And look what happens.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Down goes (inaudible). Down goes Frazier.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Down he goes.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, they're not, so they're not taking over the world just yet.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No. We're safe for a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251124elbd0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMAZON CUTTING AT LEAST 14,000 JOBS POINTS TO AI, SAYING "WORLD IS CHANGING QUICKLY"\b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE MELISSA, JAMAICA\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE MELISSA, TRACK, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 BOAT, STRIKES, TRUMP, PAUL, KELLY, VENEZUELA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 14 KILLED IN MULTIPLE BOAT STRIKES\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, LAWSUIT, SCHUMER, SNAP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOOD AID TO RUN OUT FOR 42M+ PEOPLE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , JOHNSON, JAPAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP HITS AT MILITARY IN U.S. CITIES\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, AI, LAYOFFS, UPS, HUANG, ULLRICH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON CUTTING AT LEAST 14,000 JOBS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn now to the American economy tonight. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  cutting 14,000 employees, pointing to artificial intelligence. And UPS says it will now lay off 48,000 workers. Here's Elizabeth Schulze.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POINTS TO AI, SAYING "WORLD IS CHANGING QUICKLY"\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, massive layoffs at the world's largest online retailer. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  slashing 14,000 corporate jobs, signaling more cuts to come as artificial intelligence dramatically reshapes the American workforce. In a note to employees, the retail giant writing, "The world is changing quickly. This generation of AI is the most transformative technology we've seen since the internet. We're convinced that we need to be organized more leanly." And just ahead of the busy holiday shopping season, UPS announcing 48,000 job cuts today, too. \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , and Intel also announcing cuts earlier this year, pointing to AI's potential to perform tasks instead of humans.\par \par \uc2 LAURA ULLRICH (DIRECTOR ECONOMIC RESEARCH INDEED)\par \par \uc2 Right now, the skills that AI does have, many of them are impacting white collar workers more than blue collar workers.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, I asked the CEO of \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , the world's most valuable company that makes computer chips powering AI if this is just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Should American workers be bracing for more job cuts from AI? And what can they do to prepare for the future?\par \par \uc2 JENSEN HUANG\uc2  (CEO \uc2 NVIDIA\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 AI is going to change every job, but you're not going to lose your job to an AI. You're going to lose your job to somebody who uses AI. And so, I would advise everyone to engage AI.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, with those layoffs tonight at major American employers like UPS and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , economists say that AI is helping companies cut their costs because the technology allows them to do more work with fewer people. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Potentially sweeping changes ahead. Elizabeth, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020251030elas00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 REPORT: AMAZON COULD REPLACE 60,000 JOBS WITH ROBOTS WORKPLACE AUTOMATION\b0\par\par\uc2 393 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: $230 MILLION PAYOUT PUSH\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, DOJ, $230 MIL, PROSECUTIONS, TAXPAYERS, ETHICS\par \par \uc2 ARREST, PARDON, JAN 6, RIOTER, JEFFRIES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , PUTIN, ABOUT-FACE, UKRAINE, WHITE HOUSE, DEMOLITION\par \par \uc2 ICE, NYC, RAIDS, VENDORS, MIGRANTS, SHOT\par \par \uc2 LOUVRE, HEIST, JEWELS, THIEVES, RECRIMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, JOBS, ROBOTS, REPLACE, AUTOMATION\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Time is certainly of the essence. James, thank you. Now to one of the largest private employers in the country, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is reportedly planning to replace more than half a million jobs with robots. Sam is here with the details. What's the company saying here?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPORT: AMAZON COULD REPLACE 600,000 JOBS WITH ROBOTS\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a lot of big numbers, big, big, big numbers, Linsey. This is a big deal. Could be one of the biggest workplace automations in modern history. Now there are concerns this morning that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , it's the second largest private employer in the country, as Linsey said, is hoping to replace more than half a million new jobs with robots.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now here's how this would work. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " citing internal documents is reporting that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  expects sales to double by 2033. That type of growth would normally require about 600,000 more workers. But the company is reportedly looking to fill those new jobs with robots. And ultimately hopes to automate 75% of its entire operations.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now \uc2 Amazon\uc2  also reportedly estimates that automation could help save it more than 12 billion, with a B, dollars from 2025 to 2027, that's just in two years. In a statement, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said the leaked document reflect the perspective, this is a quote, "Of just one team, and don't represent our overall hiring strategy."\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) They add that the company is actively hiring and plans to fill about 250,000 positions for the holiday season. But it does not kind of address the whole idea of automation.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow, that is a big, big change.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's happening everywhere. Not just \uc2 Amazon\uc2  right now.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't think it would be a surprise to a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251031elam00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HEALTH CARE WORKERS ON STRIKE WORKERS FROM \uc2 KAISER PERMANENTE\uc2  ON STRIKE\b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIRE WARNING\par \par \uc2 ATC, SHUTDOWN, DANIELS, RISKS, AIRPORTS, LEFEVRE\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, FALLOUT, GOP, DEMS, ARGENTINA, VENEZUELA\par \par \uc2 ICE, LA, EMERGENCY, RAIDS, CHICAGO, PROTESTS, CARTELS\par \par \uc2 CEASEFIRE, HOSTAGE, TENSIONS, TRUMP, \uc2 HAMAS\par \par \uc2 VIDEO, SHAPIRO, BALMER, GUILTY, SENTENCED\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, CHP, CRASHES, SNOW\par \par \uc2 OPENAI, \uc2 WALMART\uc2 , CHATGPT, SHOPPING, CUSTOMERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 GREENBERG, SUICIDE, KNIFE, WOUNDS, FIANC\u201\'00\'C9\par \par \uc2 D'ANGELO, R&B, TRIBUTES, LEGACY, CANCER, "HOW DOES IT FEEL"\par \par \uc2 XBOX, BOND, WINDOWS, LEDBETTER, \uc2 SAG\par \par \uc2 "DANCING WITH THE STARS", IRWIN, DEDICATION\par \par \uc2 AIR, TRAFFIC, CONTROL, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 HEALTH, CARE, WORKERS, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to thousands of health care workers back on the picket lines this morning for day two of a planned five day strike. Kayna Whitworth is in Los Angeles with more for us. Good morning, Kayna.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEALTH CARE WORKERS ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Robin, good morning. Some 40,000 health care workers in multiple unions across the west, as you said, preparing for day two of this historic strike. And these workers for \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  tell us it's more than wages. They want to be included in staffing language. They allege that patients are being double and triple booked at times. \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  pushing back on that claim, saying they often exceed the mandated nurse to patient ratio, adding it's been working with unions to provide competitive wages and are prepared to increase those wages 21.5 percent over the next four years.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But that is a far cry from the union's demand of 25 percent, which Kaiser says is out of step with today's economic realities and would force them to raise premiums. Now, the union does tell us they will strike for four more days. Hopefully they'll go back to the negotiating table. They will remain unpaid during that time, and as for the patients, you guys, some of them may have appointments rescheduled, but the hospitals and medical offices will remain open. Guys?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good to know that, and hopefully something be worked out in the end. All right, Kayna, thank you. Coming up in our "GMA" morning menu, a look at the rise of on-demand lab tests. Becky is going to be back. She checked them out to see if they could actually help you and prove your health.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE SCIENCE OF YOU\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also ahead, an emotional night in the ballroom as "Dancing With the Stars" celebrated dedication night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EMOTIONAL NIGHT IN THE BALLROOM\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, the life and legacy of Diane Keaton being honored in a big way, and how you could be included. That's ahead in pop news. And Sam is with Tory. Hey, Sam.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Whit. Tory's pushed us into peak fall foliage, if you will. It's day three of our Deals & Steals Fall Festival. Fall Festival. Tory has seasonal clothing and accessories all starting under $10.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's do it.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll be right back. Peak foliage. Peak fall foliage.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251028elaf00061\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRUMP DIRECTS HEGSETH TO USE "ALL AVAILABLE FUNDS" TO PAY TROOPS ADMINISTRATION MISTAKENLY ISSUES LAYOFF NOTICE TO CDC AGENTS\b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAUGHT ON CAMERA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FRIGHTENING CRASH\par \par \uc2 HELICOPTER, CRASH, FUEL LEAK, \uc2 KUBOTA\uc2 , NERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE INJURED AFTER HELICOPTER PLUMMETS TO THE GROUND\par \par \uc2 NOR'EASTER, FLOODING, WIND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PREPARATIONS UNDERWAY AS NOR'EASTER HEADS UP COAST\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 NOR'EASTER, STORM, WEATHER, TRACK\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , WAR, ANGREST, TRUMP, PEACE DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL AWAITS HOSTAGE RELEASE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , PEACE SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 LAYOFF, PAYCHECK, US TROOPS, CODING ERROR, CDC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP DIRECTS HEGSETH TO USE "ALL AVAILABLE FUNDS" TO PAY TROOPS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And amidst all of that, there's still the federal government shutdown. And now, more mass firings may be on tap. ABC's Alex Presha is in Washington with word of help on the way for those military families caught in the middle of all of this. Alex, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. Yeah, a court filing said that more than 4,000 federal workers would be fired during this shutdown. Well, breaking this morning, a White House official confirms to \uc2 ABC News\uc2  that that figure is just a snapshot in time. Those numbers will rise. And as this government shutdown is now in its 12th day, President Trump says he's directing Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  to use all available funds to pay members of the military on October 15th.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some 2 million military service members are part of that essential federal employee group required to keep working through the shutdown. A spokesperson for the Office of Management and Budget tells \uc2 ABC News\uc2  that funds for these checks are coming from the Department of War's research and development. Meanwhile, the Trump administration has begun firing other federal workers, an unprecedented move. The biggest cuts coming from the Treasury Department and Health and Human Services. Other layoffs happening at Commerce, Education, Energy, HUD, and Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And this morning, \uc2 ABC News\uc2  has learned that the administration mistakenly issued layoff notices to key CDC offices, teams responding to measles outbreaks in the US and Ebola cases in Africa. The administration blames a coding error for the mix-up. The White House says it's making cuts to afford essential services.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But President Trump has called the shutdown an opportunity to make cuts to programs supported by Democrats. Gio?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we will see how long the shutdown continues. Alex Presha, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251023elac0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRUMP DEPORTATION AGENDA IMPACTING LEGAL MIGRANT FARM LABOR OWYHEE PRODUCE IN IDAHO HIGHLIGHTS THE FALLOUT ON THE WORKFORCE\b0\par\par\uc2 965 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2025 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , CEASEFIRE, TRUMP, HOSTAGES, GAZA, HORN, AL-OJANI\par \par \uc2 JAMES, INDICTED, TRUMP, COMEY, HALLIGAN, JOHNSON, KOTEK\par \par \uc2 VANCE, HOSTAGES, NATIONAL, GUARD, CHICAGO, JAMES, \uc2 PROPUBLICA\uc2 , HOMAN\par \par \uc2 PRITZKER\uc2 , TRUMP, TROOPS, CRIME, ACT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, JAMES, NATIONAL, GUARD, ACT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 DEPORTATION, MIGRANT, FARM, LABOR, OWYHEE, SOL, MYERS, O'NEILL\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Trump administration has moved aggressively to deport undocumented immigrants across the country. But that crackdown has also impacted the legal migrant workforce, especially on the nation's farms. Mireya Villareal traveled to Idaho for a firsthand look at the fallout.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP DEPORTATION AGENDA IMPACTING LEGAL MIGRANT FARM LABOR\par \par \uc2 MAURICIO SOL (FARMWORKER)\par \par \uc2 We all come on a H-2A visa program, so we come all here legally. So, basically, 90 percent of the workforce here, it's from Mexico. While we usually get, like, a lot of applications, we're are not getting that many now because people is afraid, like, that even when they're legally here, they're getting, like, arrested for no reason.\par \par \uc2 SHAY MYERS (GENERAL MANAGER/OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 So, we've got, at peak, we'll have about 300 workers. We have about 82 H-2A workers and then it just depends on the timing.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Owyhee Produce has transformed into a big family business passed down through three generations and is now in the hands of Shay Myers, who not only runs the operations, but also has become an advocate for immigration reform because the fight has landed literally in their backyard. Caught in the middle of all of it are the workers, families he's known for decades.\par \par \uc2 EMPLOYEE (OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) .\par \par \uc2 SHAY MYERS (GENERAL MANAGER/OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 (Speaks foreign language) .\par \par \uc2 EMPLOYEE (OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SHAY MYERS (GENERAL MANAGER/OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 These guys are able to come from Mexico. We have some from South Africa that are coming this year. They come from other countries. They're given the visa for eight to nine months and they're allowed to stay the entirety of that time. We, they have to be re-interviewed and qualified. And then when they come, we provide the airfare or the bus travel, their housing when they get here, and everything really when they're here, except for food and clothing.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That seems...\par \par \uc2 SHAY MYERS (GENERAL MANAGER/OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 That's part of the requirements that we have to cover.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That seems like a big cost though, because, I mean, that's a lot to get a group of worker - why not just hire people from here?\par \par \uc2 SHAY MYERS (GENERAL MANAGER/OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so we would love to hire people from here. The reality is, is we can't find the numbers of people here. We're in a rural area, number one. Number two, this is hard work. It is difficult work. And there are lots of people that are not willing to do it.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is a clear directive from the administration that they want mass deportation, right? That is impacting then our labor force. Is that what I'm understanding?\par \par \uc2 JAMES O'NEILL (LEGISLATIVE AFFAIRS DIRECTOR\par \par \uc2 It's absolutely impacting the labor force. And the President said so himself. He said the aggressive raids are hurting our farmers. And I think anybody that understands economics knows that means higher prices for them at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Even the Labor Department points to a potential food crisis stemming from these immigration raids saying, in part, that the combination of low migrant influx and no available legal workforce threatens the availability of domestic food production and prices for US consumers.\par \par \uc2 ELIANA RAMIREZ (QUALITY CONTROL/OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 So, the land is always taking pictures. We have six cameras.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Originally from Pueblo, Mexico, Eliana started in the fields as a seasonal work almost 20 years ago, working her way up while providing for her family.\par \par \uc2 ELIANA RAMIREZ (QUALITY CONTROL/OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 I knew from the beginning that I was in a country that offers you opportunities for whoever wants to take it. You know?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIDEO CLIP FROM \uc2 RURAL & MIGRANT MINISTRY, INC.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Eliana is studying to become a US citizen. But the reality is not everyone is as lucky as Eliana. With the Trump administration's aggressive approach to immigration and mass deportation, raids happening in the fields, visa holders held up at the border, workers are hard to come by. Over the last few months, tariffs and raids have put farms and manufacturing plants on a target list, most fearful of being deported even if they are here legally with a visa.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) At least three bills have been filed by Republicans and Democrats acknowledging the growing problem of a limited domestic workforce and reliance on migrant labor. But after more than a decade of advocating for change, Shay worries this may be another can that gets kicked down the road.\par \par \uc2 SHAY MYERS (GENERAL MANAGER/OWYHEE PRODUCE)\par \par \uc2 To continue to not fix this problem is absolutely, completely wrong. The ethics are wrong. Like, we as Americans try to do the right thing. Let's do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Thanks to Mireya. You can see her full report Monday night on \uc2 ABC News\uc2  Live Prime. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 That is all for us today. Thanks for sharing part of your Sunday with us. Check out "World News Tonight" and I'll see you tomorrow on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020251015elac00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WHITE HOUSE BEGINS MASS LAYOFFS OF FEDERAL WORKERS GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN\b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TENNESSEE, PLANT, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL, WORKER, LAYOFFS, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CHINA, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, GAZA, CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ, VIDEO\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE, CHARGES, EVIDENCE\par \par \uc2 ACES, MERCURY, WNBA, CHAMPIONSHIP\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP OF "CHAD POWERS"\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 CRYPTO, BITCOIN, SCAM\par \par \uc2 "CHAD POWERS", "I LIKE ME", "WOMAN IN CABIN 10", "VICTORIA BECKHAM", "PARENT TRAP"\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TOPIC\par \par \uc2 DAVIS, ROARK, TENNESSEE, PLANT, EXPLOSION, ACCURATE ENERGETIC SYSTEMS\par \par \uc2 WHITE HOUSE, SHUTDOWN, LAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, so many unanswered questions, Mola. Thank you. Now we're gonna turn to the latest on the government shutdown, with no end in sight. Mass layoffs have been announced. So, let's go back to Karen Travers in Washington, tracking the latest. Good morning again, Karen.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE BEGINS MASS LAYOFFS OF FEDERAL WORKERS\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. Firing federal workers during a government shutdown is unprecedented, but the White House is now making good on that threat of mass layoffs.\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The full scope of the firings is unclear, but last night the White House budget office, in a court filing, said more than 4,000 federal workers will be let go. The biggest cuts are coming at the Departments of the Treasury and Health and Human Services. The White House says it has to fire workers to be able to afford essential government services, but the president has called the shutdown an unprecedented opportunity to make cuts.\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, as this government shutdown drags on, federal employees and their families are feeling the pain. Many federal workers received only a partial paycheck on Friday, and that is the last payment they're gonna get until the shutdown ends. And next week, 2 million US service members, they'll get no paycheck.\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senators left town for the weekend, and they are not going to be back until Tuesday to hold votes, and that ensures the shutdown is going to last at least two weeks. Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251029elab0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 URGENT WARNING ABOUT BITCOIN ATM CRYPTO SCAM WOMAN SAVED FROM ALLEGED SCAMMER\b0\par\par\uc2 909 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TENNESSEE, PLANT, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL, WORKER, LAYOFFS, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CHINA, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, GAZA, CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ, VIDEO\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE, CHARGES, EVIDENCE\par \par \uc2 ACES, MERCURY, WNBA, CHAMPIONSHIP\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP OF "CHAD POWERS"\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 CRYPTO, BITCOIN, SCAM\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now with an \uc2 ABC News\uc2  investigation of an increasingly common scam involving Bitcoin ATMs that have popped up in many places across the country. The ordeal of one woman was captured on police body camera video as authorities say an alleged scammer pressured her into giving up her life savings. ABC's Jay O'Brien has more on how she was saved in the nick of time.\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 Hello.\par \par \uc2 MYNDI JORDAN (BYSTANDER WHO CALLED 911)\par \par \uc2 She's at $23,000 already.\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 Where is she at right now?\par \par \uc2 MYNDI JORDAN (BYSTANDER WHO CALLED 911)\par \par \uc2 She's in the corner of there.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) You're watching a crypto currency scam caught in real-time.\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 Can I talk to you for a second?\par \par \uc2 FRAN BATES (SCAM VICTIM)\par \par \uc2 Please, I have the bank on the phone.\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 Okay. Let, let me talk to who you're talking to on the bank.\par \par \uc2 FRAN BATES (SCAM VICTIM)\par \par \uc2 And I'm in danger.\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 Okay, stop putting money in there.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fran Bates is loading tens of thousands of dollars of her life savings into something called a Bitcoin ATM.\par \par \uc2 ALLEGED SCAMMER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 This is the security team (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 No, you're not.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And is one click away from losing it all.\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 Don't click on done. Do not click anything.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Over the course of two days, a scammer convinced the 85-year-old her bank account had been hacked, and she needed to put her money into a machine she'd never seen before for safety.\par \par \uc2 FRAN BATES (SCAM VICTIM)\par \par \uc2 ...true, that's exactly what they're...\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That seems so hard to believe.\par \par \uc2 FRAN BATES (SCAM VICTIM)\par \par \uc2 I know. And you think if you had to listen to it, you would wonder what's going on with that woman? Doesn't she realize what they're doing to her? No, you don't. It is a horrible, horrible experience.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fran is just one of dozens of victims \uc2 ABC News\uc2  and our own television stations have found whose scams ended at a Bitcoin ATM, a machine popular in gas stations, supermarkets and liquor stores that experts tell us scammers are increasingly using as a tool to separate Americans from their hard-earned cash.\par \par \uc2 AMY NOFZIGER (DIRECTOR OF FRAUD VICTIM SUPPORT AT AARP)\par \par \uc2 They look pretty much like a regular ATM. The difference is you don't really get money out of them. You put money in and buy crypto currency.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Data from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  shows Americans collectively lost nearly $250 million to scams using Bitcoin ATMs in 2024, more than double from the previous year.\par \par \uc2 ADAM ZARAZINSKI (CEO OF INCA DIGITAL)\par \par \uc2 These are all transnational criminal groups. Yeah, these are not one-off guys in grandma's basement.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Adam Zarazinski, a crypto forensics expert, tracks the sophisticated criminal networks behind these scams and explained why he believes more isn't being done by the companies operating these machines.\par \par \uc2 ADAM ZARAZINSKI (CEO OF INCA DIGITAL)\par \par \uc2 Money. It's money.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) They have a financial incentive to let people be scammed?\par \par \uc2 ADAM ZARAZINSKI (CEO OF INCA DIGITAL)\par \par \uc2 Yep, because they make, they make a percent on every transaction.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And while the vast majority of these victims never see a dime back...\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 No, you listen to me.\par \par \uc2 ALLEGED SCAMMER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Sir, please allow her to finish.\par \par \uc2 LT. JAMES STEWART (WS POLICE OFFICER)\par \par \uc2 You really want to fight this fight with me?\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) For Fran Bates, a police lieutenant intervened just in time after Mindy Jordan, a quick-thinking bystander at that gas station called 911.\par \par \uc2 MYNDI JORDAN (BYSTANDER WHO CALLED 911)\par \par \uc2 I hate when somebody tries to take advantage of somebody that's not able to protect themselves or defend themselves in that case, you know, and I feel like they were taking advantage of you, and I was like, no.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The pair reunited here for the first time.\par \par \uc2 FRAN BATES (SCAM VICTIM)\par \par \uc2 She was my angel.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Your angel?\par \par \uc2 FRAN BATES (SCAM VICTIM)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, she rescued me.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) For "Good Morning America," Jay O'Brien, \uc2 ABC News\uc2 , Washington.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is quite a story. Our thanks to Jay for that.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Still coming up here on "Good Morning America," what to binge this weekend, including a new documentary about Victoria Beckham on her rise to the Spice Girls and beyond.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BINGE THIS!\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251029elab0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRUMP HITS CHINA WITH ADDITIONAL 100% TARIFFS, STARTING NOV 1 TRUMP: NEW AGREEMENT WITH DRUGMAKER \uc2 ASTRAZENECA\b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TENNESSEE, PLANT, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL, WORKER, LAYOFFS, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CHINA, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) This morning, trade tensions are escalating between the US and China as President Trump threatens an additional 100 percent tariff on goods imported from China, a move that sent Wall Street tumbling. ABC's Alex Presha joins us now from Washington with the latest. Alex, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP HITS CHINA WITH ADDITIONAL 100% TARIFFS, STARTING NOV 1\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. And like so many of these announcements, the news came swift and in the form of a social media post. New 100 percent tariffs on Chinese imports will start November 1st. President Trump also plans to impose export controls on any and all critical software. And Trump says this is a response to China blindsiding him when it announced large scale export controls of its own. The President accused China of trying to hold the world captive using its rare earth minerals.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) He added that he could roll back these sanctions if tensions cool. Now, this comes at a time where there is a trade truce between the US and China. Tariffs on Chinese imports are currently at 30 percent, down from 145 percent earlier this down. Now, Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping were set to meet in South Korea during the APEC Forum later this month. That meeting is now in jeopardy. President Trump telling reporters on Friday, "I don't know we're going to have it," and this news is sparking fears that a trade war could reignite. The market is already reacting. The Dow Jones, the S&P 500, and that tech-heavy NASDAQ down big on Friday, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Alex, President Trump also making an announcement about prescription drugs. What more can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP: NEW AGREEMENT WITH DRUGMAKER \uc2 ASTRAZENECA\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Whit. So, \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 , the largest drug maker in the UK, is striking a deal to offer deep discounts on the company's catalogue of more than three dozen medications. Americans will get something called the most favored nation pricing and \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  now joins \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , who struck a similar deal last month. Patients will be able to buy their prescriptions directly through a new Trump Rx website starting early next year, according to the White House. Rachel?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, still big questions if other drug makers will join. All right, Alex Presha for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251029elab00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WHITE HOUSE BEGINS MASS LAYOFFS OF FEDERAL WORKERS FEDERAL WORKERS FORCED TO WORK WITHOUT PAY\b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TENNESSEE, PLANT, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL, WORKER, LAYOFFS, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the latest on the government shutdown. Still no end in sight, and now mass layoffs are underway. ABC's Karen Travers has more from Washington. Karen, the President making good on his threat to fire federal workers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE BEGINS MASS LAYOFFS OF FEDERAL WORKERS\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, Rachel. And firing government workers during a government shutdown is unprecedented, but the White House has been threatening to cut thousands of jobs, and, Rachel, these layoffs come as many government workers are worried about when they'll see their next paycheck.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of big announcements.\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, the Trump administration making good on its threat, telling some federal workers they're fired.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It'll be a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The full scope of the layoffs is unclear, but the White House budget office in a court filing Friday night saying more than 4,000 federal workers will be fired. The biggest cuts coming at the Departments of the Treasury and Health and Human Services, according to the filing. Layoffs also happening at Commerce, Education, Energy, HUD and Homeland Security. The White House says it has to fire workers to be able to afford essential government services, but President Trump has called the shutdown an unprecedented opportunity to make cuts.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It'll be Democrat oriented because we figure, you know, they started this thing, so they should be Democrat oriented. It'll be a lot, and we'll announce the numbers over the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The largest union representing federal workers slamming the firings, calling it disgraceful. And as the government shutdown drags on, federal employees and their families feeling the pain. Many federal workers receiving only a partial paycheck on Friday, the last payment they'll get until the shutdown ends. And next week, 2 million US service members will get no paycheck. Nakita Fowler's husband has served in the Navy for nearly 19 years.\par \par \uc2 NAKITA FOWLER (WIFE OF NAVAL SERVICE MEMBER)\par \par \uc2 Do we have enough money to fill the tank so that we can drive them to soccer practice, or is this something where we have to save the gas money so that my husband can have enough to drive to work every day, because he's still required to go every day even though he's not being paid.\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Senators left town for the weekend and will not hold votes until Tuesday, which ensures the government shutdown will last at least two weeks. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) At least two weeks. All right, Karen, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251029elab00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN IMPACTS HOLIDAY TRAVEL ADVICE IS TO BE PATIENT\b0\par\par\uc2 256 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TENNESSEE, PLANT, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL, WORKER, LAYOFFS, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the impact of the government shutdown on air travel as TSA agents and air traffic controllers are forced to work without pay. ABC's Melissa Adan joins us from Los Angeles with that part of the story. Melissa, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN IMPACTS HOLIDAY TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ADAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. Exactly. So, millions are expected to travel this holiday weekend, and the ongoing shutdown may impact air travel. Yes, air traffic controllers, TSA employees, they're working without pay until the government opens back up. Now, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is reporting an increase in air traffic controller shortages at facilities across the country, while the \uc2 National Air Traffic Controllers Association\uc2  president put out a statement saying workers are showing up, adding that the ongoing controller shortage predates the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ADAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) While the secretary of transportation warning that controllers who don't show up won't get paid and could even be let go. Just yesterday, ATC staffing shortages led to flight delays at Phoenix and Chicago airports, and come Tuesday, it'll be the last partial paycheck for the more than 13,000 air traffic controllers. So, the advice from a major airline trade group is anyone heading to the airport right now is encouraged to pack their patience. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Melissa, thanks for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251029elab00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SHUTDOWN'S IMPACT ON AIR TRAVEL GROWING PRESIDENT SUGGEST SOME FEDERAL WORKERS MAY NOT GET BACK PAY\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING GRIDLOCK\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, AIRPORTS, ATC, FURLOUGHED, TRUMP, DANIELS, LEE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We begin with the widening followed from the government shutdown. It is day eight, and no sign yet of a deal between Democrats and Republicans to end the standoff and pass a spending bill. Airline delays are emerging as a concern with reports of staffing shortages of air-traffic controllers nationwide. Will Reeve is at Newark Airport with the latest for us. Good morning, Will.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHUTDOWN'S IMPACT ON AIR TRAVEL GROWING\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Michael. Right now eight major air-traffic control locations are facing staffing shortages, and one airport near Los Angeles actually had to shut down for hours because of staffing issues. And this all comes as the President now says that some federal workers might not get back pay whenever the shutdown ends.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, growing gridlock for travelers across the country as the government shutdown hits air travel hard. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  warning of staff shortages and at least eight major locations, threatening delays and cancelations in Las Vegas, Chicago, Dallas, Houston, Boston, Newark, Atlanta and Nashville. Smaller airports like the one in Burbank, California hit hardest. Some control towers forced to shut down for hours on end.\par \par \uc2 PILOT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Is this because of the shutdown that you guys are going?\par \par \uc2 AIR TRAFFIC CONTROL (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's for staffing.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Air-traffic controllers are considered essential workers, and have already faced extreme staffing shortages. Now they won't be paid until the shutdown ends, possibly going weeks without a paycheck. It all has a serious impact on productivity.\par \par \uc2 NICK DANIELS (PRESIDENT NATIONAL AIR TRAFFIC CONTROLLERS ASSOCIATION)\par \par \uc2 The stress it's going to lead to fatigue, and it's an issue they've had to contend with for years.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) President trump has already threatened to fire federal workers, and now the White House is floating a new threat, suggesting that some of the 750,000 federal workers might not get back pay.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 For the most part, we're gonna take care of our people. There are some people that really don't deserve to be taken care of, and we'll take care of them in a different way.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) For people like Anthony Lee, a veteran FDA employee, federal workers are simply caught in the cross fire.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY LEE (FURLOUGHED FDA EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 We have workers across the board who are coming to work and having to do their jobs without pay. And then you have other workers who are furloughed, through no fault of their own. And for the administration to even suggest that they're not going to not pay federal workers due to this shutdown is unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the President didn't say which workers stand to lose that money, and Democrats say what the President is proposing is illegal.\par \par \uc2 REP \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (DEM) (MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 And the law is clear, every single furloughed federal employee is entitled to back pay. Period. Full stop.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT SUGGESTS SOME FEDERAL WORKERS MAY NOT GET BACK PAY\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) It was during President Trump's first term back in 2019 that Congress passed a law guaranteeing back pay for federal workers. A law Trump himself signed. Now six years later, even some Republicans are uneasy with the administration's strategy.\par \par \uc2 SEN THOM TILLIS (REP) (NORTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 I don't think that that is helpful discussion right now. Let's get out of the shutdown. Let's negotiate the premium tax credits, and not threaten or use this as a lever to actually create fear and uncertainty for people who are not making a paycheck.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) The shutdown is dragging on with no end in sight, and we're approaching a busy holiday weekend. And to snarl things up even more, right now in the Newark area and around the northeast, bad weather is expected. So right now, George, a lot of headwinds facing travelers amid this shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251016ela80002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOVT SHUTDOWN COULD IMPACT MILLIONS WH: MASS FIRINGS OF FEDERAL WORKERS "IMMINENT"\b0\par\par\uc2 799 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SCHUMER, JOHNSON, VANCE, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, PRICE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. We begin tonight with this paralyzing government shutdown. The impact felt already.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And tonight, millions of government workers, from some 2 million US troops to the TSA security agents at checkpoints keeping flights safe, all now facing the real possibility of missing their paychecks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVT SHUTDOWN COULD IMPACT MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) At the White House today, Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  in the Briefing Room blaming Democrats and warning mass layoffs could be coming. Sources telling \uc2 ABC News\uc2  tonight that those layoffs could come as early as tomorrow. Tonight, Democrats demanding Medicaid cuts be restored and that health care subsidies be extended to keep millions of Americans from seeing their premiums go up.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And tonight, no signs of this ending any time soon. Rachel Scott leading us off. She's up on The Hill again tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WH: MASS FIRINGS OF FEDERAL WORKERS "IMMINENT"\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, with the federal government now officially shut down and millions of workers facing the prospect of missed paychecks, the White House now saying mass layoffs are imminent.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 How long do you think this will go, Mr. Speaker?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (REP-LA) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 I hope it's short.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sources say House Republicans were told President Trump will start firing federal workers as soon as tomorrow. ABC's Mary Bruce asking Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  why that's necessary.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) In every previous shutdown, workers have been furloughed, not laid off. So, why does the President want to fire some workers and not just furlough them? Why is this shutdown any different?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT JD VANCE (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, we haven't made any final decisions about what we're going to do with certain workers. What we're seeing is that we might have to take extraordinary steps, especially the longer this goes on.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats refused to fund the government unless Republicans agree to reverse Medicaid cuts and extend expiring Obamacare subsidies, so health care premiums don't rise for millions.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (DEM-NY) (SENATE MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 It is not radical to say we want to prevent the average American family from getting these huge increases.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The White House says Democrats are holding the country hostage.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT JD VANCE (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 What you don't do is say, unless you do exactly what we want to do as congressional Democrats, we're taking it hostage.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANY FEDERAL WORKERS COULD GO WITHOUT PAYCHECKS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, millions of federal employees considered essential forced to work without pay, including TSA agents and some 2 million military troops, 750,000 others are furloughed.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JO PRICE (FEDERAL CONTRACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God. It's terrible.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Willie Jo Price has worked at the cafeteria in the \uc2 Library of Congress\uc2  for 44 years.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JO PRICE (FEDERAL CONTRACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Electric bills and the gas bill, all these things went up, so I'm already juggling paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) You may not be able to make rent.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JO PRICE (FEDERAL CONTRACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE FREEZES BILLIONS IN FUNDING ACROSS 16 STATES\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the White House turning up the pressure, targeting projects dear to Democrats. Freezing $18 billion in infrastructure funding for New York City. Home of both Democratic leaders \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , and blocking $8 billion for energy projects in 16 states that voted Democrat in the last Presidential election.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's bring in Rachel Scott tonight. She's live up on The Hill again for us. And, Rachel, I know you're working your sources tonight. Any signs this evening how long this could last?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, the shutdown will last for at least one more day. The Senate will not be back in session until Friday. Meanwhile, Senate Republicans really trying to turn up the heat on Senate Democrats, especially those in tough political races ahead. But tonight, the vast majority of Democrats are holding firm, David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) One more day because there's no vote set for tomorrow. But this could go on well beyond that?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm. Way beyond that, David. Days, if not weeks.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott leading us off here. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020251002ela10002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  SHARES PROVOCATIVE MANIPULATED IMAGE TARGETING CHICAGO WEAK AUGUST JOBS REPORT SUGGESTS CRACKS IN ECONOMY\b0\par\par\uc2 1,023 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2025 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, WAR, CHICAGO, JOBS, RFK, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) On Friday, President Trump rebranded the Department of Defense the Department of War. Saturday, he announced the Department's first target, an American city. The President's words, "I love the smell of deportations in the morning. Chicago about to find out why it's called the Department of War." Of course, criticism followed, including this from Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 . "The President of the United States is threatening to go to war with an American city. This is not a joke. This is not normal."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUOTE FROM PRESIDENT TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUOTE FROM GOVERNOR PRITZKER\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this chilling threat from President Trump comes after a week of challenges. Friday's weak jobs report. On Capitol Hill a bipartisan grilling for Health Secretary RFK Jr., and a demand for transparency from \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 's victims. China's display of diplomatic skill and military force. And Russia's rebuff of another Trump deadline on the war in Ukraine. We're going to cover it all this morning. Senior White House correspondent Selina Wang starts us off.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP SHARES PROVOCATIVE MANIPULATED IMAGE TARGETING CHICAGO\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Protests in Chicago as President Trump escalates his threats against the third-largest city in the nation. Trump resharing a manipulated image on social media that places him in front of the city, the image referencing the iconic 1979 film "Apocalypse Now." The caption reads, "I love the smell of deportations in the morning. Chicago about to find out why it's called the Department of War," a reference to his Friday executive order rebranding the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 , the Department of War.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I think it sends a really a - really a message of strength. We're very strong.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEAK AUGUST JOBS REPORT SUGGESTS CRACKS IN ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) For weeks Trump has been threatening to increase the federal presence in Chicago after sending two thousand National Guard troops to patrol the streets of Washington, and ramping up immigration enforcement in the capital. The ramped-up deportation campaign coming amid warning signs that Trump's immigration crackdown and trade war are causing cracks in the economy. President Trump brushing off a dismal new jobs report out Friday, showing a steep slowdown in hiring.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Employers added just 22 thousand jobs in August and unemployment rose slightly to four-point-three percent, the highest rate in nearly four years. And revised numbers for June show employment actually fell by 13 thousand jobs, the first decline in almost five years. Still, Trump insists his tariffs will revive American manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Wait until these factories start to open up that are being built all over the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATORS SPAR WITH RFK JR. OVER CHANGES TO VACCINE POLICY\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the numbers tell a different story. Seventy-eight thousand losses in manufacturing since the start of the year as tariffs drive up the cost of supplies. Meanwhile, Trump's promise to make America healthy again sparking a showdown on Capitol Hill with \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary RFK Jr. grilled by senators over COVID vaccine policy.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM) (VIRGINIA)\par \par \uc2 Do you think the vaccine did anything to prevent additional deaths?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 Again, I would like to see the data and talk about the data. I'm not...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM) (VIRGINIA)\par \par \uc2 You have had this job for eight months and you don't know the data about whether the vaccine saved lives?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 Oh, and that's the...\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) That hearing devolving into a shouting match.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 That was your promise, Mr. Kennedy, not mine.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 I know I, I never promised that I was going to recommend products with which there is no indication.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 You didn't promise? Oh, which story...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (DEM) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 That they're going to have to go off-label...\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 This is crazy talk. You're just making stuff up.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (DEM) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 ...to, to prescribe, to...\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Two Republican senators also sounding the alarm, Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , a physician whose support was key to Kennedy's final confirmation, and Senator \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2 , a surgeon and the number two Republican in the Senate who rarely strays from the party line.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (REP) (WYOMING)\par \par \uc2 If we're going to make America healthy again, we can't allow public health to be undermined.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 I would say effectively we're denying people vaccine.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 You're wrong.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) I pressed the President on Cassidy's warning.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mr. President, Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  said effectively we're denying people vaccines. Do you have full confidence in what RFK Jr. is doing?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 He means very well and he has got some a little different ideas. I like the fact that he's different.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And George, turning back to Chicago, Mayor \uc2 Brandon Johnson\uc2  is highlighting a decrease in crime in the city as President Trump is threatening a surge in federal enforcement. And on Saturday, Mayor Johnson fired back at President Trump's post saying that Trump, quote, "wants to occupy our city and break our constitution." But, George, the White House is defending the President's post saying that Democratic leaders are more upset about a post from the President than with crime, and saying that that, quote, "tells you everything you need to know about Democrats' twisted priorities." George?\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020250911el9700002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AUGUST JOBS REPORT SHOWS NUMBERS WEAKER THAN EXPECTED ECONOMIC GROWTH IS COMING SOON\b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ICE, RAIDS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, THREAT\par \par \uc2 AUGUST, JOBS, REPORT\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, "THIS WEEK"\par \par \uc2 DEPARTMENT OF DEFENSE, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 DENVER, PLANE, CRASH\par \par \uc2 MARYLAND, MURDER, MENDES\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINATION, ACCESS\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, ADAMS\par \par \uc2 AIRLINE, PASSENGER, RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 MCNEIL\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, COLLEGE, "COLLEGE GAMEDAY"\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO, LGBTQ, MASS\par \par \uc2 POWERBALL, LOTTERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ICE, CHICAGO, RAID, IMMIGRATION, HYUNDAI, \uc2 PRITZKER\uc2 , SCHRANK\par \par \uc2 TOPIC\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  , JOBS REPORT, ECONOMIST, FEDERAL RESERVE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Alex. Thank you so much for that. Meanwhile, we are gonna talk here about the job market slowdown and the Trump administration's response to the latest weak employment report. ABC's senior White House correspondent Selina Wang in Washington with more on this. Selina, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUGUST JOBS REPORT SHOWS NUMBER WEAKER THAN EXPECTED\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Gio, good morning. Yeah, that report showed a dramatic slowdown in hiring, yet the President is brushing off the numbers, saying economic growth is coming soon. But many economists warn that it is his trade war and immigration crackdown that are causing real cracks in the economy. So, let's just break down these numbers.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Employers added just 22,000 jobs in August, that's less than a third of what economists expected. Unemployment rose to 4.3%, that's the highest in nearly four years. And revised numbers for June showed employment actually fell by 13,000 jobs. That is the first loss in almost five years. Now, President Trump is still insisting that his trade policy is going to spark this renaissance in boosted American manufacturing, but the data shows 78,000 losses in manufacturing since the start of the year, as tariffs have been driving up the cost of supplies.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOB GROWTH SLOWING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, some economists are calling this a no-fire, no-hire market. What that means is that workers who have a job are frozen in place, and those who want a job are frozen out. But, guys, what this weak report does mean is that it's almost certain that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates when it meets in a couple of weeks in order to stimulate the economy. That could mean lower borrowing costs, so your car loan or your mortgage rate could go down, so a bit of a silver lining there, Janai.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250915el960000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AUGUST JOBS REPORT NUMBERS WEAKER THAN EXPECTED\b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ICE, RAIDS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, THREAT\par \par \uc2 AUGUST, JOBS, REPORT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the job market slowdown and the Trump administration's response to the latest employment report. \uc2 ABC News\uc2  senior White House correspondent Selina Wang is in Washington with more. Selina, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUGUST JOBS REPORT SHOWS NUMBERS WEAKER THAN EXPECTED\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Whit, good morning. Yeah, that report showed a dramatic slowdown in hiring, and the President is brushing off the numbers saying economic growth is coming soon. But many economists warn it's his trade war and immigration crackdown that are causing real cracks in the economy.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, President Trump downplaying the dismal new jobs report.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're going to have tremendous job growth.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) That's despite employers adding just 22,000 jobs in August, less than a third of what economists expected. Unemployment rising to 4.3 percent, the highest in nearly four years, and revised numbers for June showing employment actually fell by 13,000 jobs, the first loss in almost five years.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK (CHIEF US ECONOMIST & MANAGING DIRECTOR AT KPMG)\par \par \uc2 What we've seen is an unprecedented surge in uncertainty with regard to trade policy. It creates people to hesitate on making big spending decisions. It also creates firms to hesitate on hiring and making big investments.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Still, Trump insists his trade policy will spark a renaissance in American manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Wait till these factories start to open up that are being built all over the country.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the data shows 78,000 losses in manufacturing since the start of the year as tariffs drive up the cost of supplies.\par \par \uc2 MARK HAMRICK\uc2  (SENIOR ECONOMIC ANALYST AT BANKRATE)\par \par \uc2 This is a no fire, no hire job market. If you are thinking about your own job security, if you're thinking about looking for work, this is a situation that doesn't give you any added confidence.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) 31-year-old Kyle Clark, who's worked for years as a technical editor, hasn't been able to find a job since April.\par \par \uc2 KYLE CLARKE (SEARCHING FOR JOB)\par \par \uc2 I applied for 44 jobs and I've had seven responses. It's a roller coaster.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But this weak jobs report, guys, does make it almost certain that the federal reserve will cut interest rates when it meets in a couple of weeks in order to stimulate the economy. Now, that could mean lower borrowing costs, so your car loan or mortgage rate could go down, and this does come as mortgage rates just had its biggest one day drop in over a year on Friday. The 30-year fixed mortgage rate breaking out of the high 6 percent range where it has been stuck for months. But Gio, the question here is this. Is this enough to get people back into the market as home prices have remained stubbornly high?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORTGAGE RATES: BIGGEST ONE-DAY DROP IN OVER A YEAR\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And a lot of people, Selina, are waiting for those mortgage rates to keep dropping. Selina, thank you so much for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250915el9600004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEAK JOBS REPORT PROMPTS WARNINGS 22,000 JOBS ADDED IN AUGUST AS UNEMPLOYMENT RISES\b0\par\par\uc2 685 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORMS\par \par \uc2 UNEMPLOYMENT, JOBS, ECONOMY, TRUMP, SWONK, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEAK JOBS REPORT PROMPTS WARNINGS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) The other major story this Friday night, the labor market slow-down raising concerns about the health of the US economy. President Trump was asked about the disappointing numbers late today.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President downplaying the numbers and promising, quote, "We're going to have tremendous job growth." The US added just 22,000 jobs and the unemployment rate rose to 4.3% in August, a nearly four-year high.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce at the White House tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 22,000 JOBS ADDED IN AUGUST AS UNEMPLOYMENT RISES\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, a dramatic slowdown in the American job market. Employers adding just 22,000 jobs in August, less than a third of what economists expected. Unemployment rising to 4.3%, nearly a four-year high. And revised numbers for June showing employment actually fell by 13,000 jobs. The first loss in almost five years.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK (CHIEF ECONOMIST & MANAGING DIRECTOR \uc2 KPMG\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The labor market is starting to show a real chill. Not only are workers who have a job frozen in place, unable to hop to a higher-paying job elsewhere, but those who want a job are frozen out.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thirty-one-year-old Kyle Clark (PH) has worked for years as a technical editor, but he's been unable to find a job since April.\par \par \uc2 JOB SEEKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, like, despite how good or bad the economy is, I still need a job. I still need to pay rent. I still need to pay my bills. I like, I like to eat. I like to, you know, be able to afford groceries.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President has acknowledged he won the election in large part because he promised to grow the economy and lower prices. Today, he downplayed the latest numbers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP INSISTS TARIFFS WILL SPARK MANUFACTURING RENAISSANCE\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're going to have tremendous job growth.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) He insists the economy is booming and is adamant his tariffs will spark a renaissance in American manufacturing. But the data shows the opposite. Four straight months of manufacturing job losses.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANUFACTURING JOBS\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Wait till these factories start to open up that are being built all over the country. You're going to see things happen in this country that nobody expects.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK (CHIEF ECONOMIST & MANAGING DIRECTOR \uc2 KPMG\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 We've lost 78,000 manufacturing jobs since the start of the year. That's the exact opposite of what the administration was hoping for, but some of those job losses are, in fact, due to the higher input costs that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) While the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has resisted Trump's pressure to lower interest rates, this week's jobs report making it all but certain they will cut rates to try and encourage hiring when the Fed meets in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's bring in Mary Bruce live at the White House tonight. Because, Mary, while the President was taking questions on the economy, the President also signing an executive order late today to rename the Defense Department the Department of War?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And David, tonight, they're already replacing and changing the signs over at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's been the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  since 1949. But today, the President rebranding it the Department of War, saying it sends a message of victory and strength. Now, a formal name change would likely require congressional approval. But today, the President glossing over that saying, quote, "We're going with it. We're going with it she strongly." David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce with us tonight. Mary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250909el9500003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CSC STANDOFF AS CHIEF IS OUSTED WORKERS STAGE WALKOUT; FOUR TOP OFFICIALS RESIGN\b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, THOMPSON, WESTMAN, MERKEL\par \par \uc2 CDC, MONAREZ, HOURY, KENNEDY, VACCINE, PROTEST, O'NEILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CSC STANDOFF AS CHIEF IS OUSTED\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn now to the new fallout tonight after the White House announced the firing of CDC director, Susan Monarez, only weeks into the job. Monarez is refusing to resign, saying only President Trump himself can fire her. Four other top officials at the CDC have now resigned. And tonight, a walkout protest at the health agency. Here's ABC's Mary Bruce.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORKERS STAGE WALKOUT; FOUR TOP OFFICIALS RESIGN\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, at the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 , dozens of workers walking out. Four top officials resigning in protest after Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. ousted CDC director Susan Monarez.\par \par \uc2 DEBRA HOURY MD MPH (CHIEF MED OFFICER / DEP DIR PROGRAM & SCIENCE CDC)\par \par \uc2 We need our leaders above us to believe in CDC and stop saying, question the expert.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) In his resignation letter, the director of the Immunization Office saying, Secretary Kennedy "treats the CDC as a tool to generate policies and materials that do not reflect scientific reality."\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 Raise your right hand.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kennedy swore Monarez in as the new CDC director just last month.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 Susan is an expert in public health.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kennedy is a known vaccine skeptic. In her confirmation hearing, Monarez defending the scientific consensus.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC DIRECTOR CLASHES WITH RFK JR. OVER VACCINES\par \par \uc2 SUSAN MONAREZ (FORMER DIRECTOR CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL)\par \par \uc2 Vaccines absolutely save lives. And if I'm confirmed as the CDC director, I will, I commit to making sure that we continue to prioritize vaccine availability.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) But she clashed with Kennedy, who has moved quickly to overhaul vaccine policy. Just yesterday, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  narrowing the scope of people approved for the COVID vaccine this fall to just people over 65 and adults and children at high risk. Her lawyers say she refused to rubber stamp unscientific, reckless directives and fire dedicated health experts.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD BESSER\uc2  (FORMER CDC ACTING DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 There were two lines. One is that she would never do anything that was illegal, and the other was that she would never do anything that flew in the face of science. And she was being asked to do both of those things.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the CDC in turmoil. Monarez says she hasn't resigned. That the only person that can fire her is President Trump, and that she still hasn't heard from him. But the White House insists the President has fired her because she is, quote, "not aligned with his mission."\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of trouble at the CDC, and it's going to require getting rid of some people over the long-term in order for us to change the institutional culture.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, tonight, the White House naming a replacement to lead the CDC, tapping Kennedy's deputy, Jim O'Neill, to serve as interim director. He's been Kennedy's number two for the past three months and has said previously that he supports vaccines, but not government mandates. Rachel?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce with the sudden shakeup at the CDC. All right, Mary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250902el8s00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GROCERY GIANT KROGER LAYING OFF NEARLY 1,000 EMPLOYEES THIRD ROUND OF CUTS THIS YEAR\b0\par\par\uc2 284 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TARIFFS ON INDIA\par \par \uc2 TARIFFS, INDIA, PRICES, CONSUMERS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , FED, LAWSUIT, COOK, FALLOUT, MADOWITZ\par \par \uc2 IOWA, UPSET, DEMOCRATS, SENATE, GOP\par \par \uc2 MONSOON, DUST STORMS, RAINFALL, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ISRAEL, HOSTAGES, GAZA, ATTACK, JOURNALISTS, AL-KHEETAN\par \par \uc2 KROGER, LAYOFFS, EMPLOYEES, CORPORATE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, George. We turn now to grocery giant \uc2 Kroger\uc2  announcing it is laying off nearly 1,000 employees to cut costs. Rhiannon Ally is here with those details. Good morning, Rhiannon.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROCERY GIANT KROGER LAYING OFF NEARLY 1,000 EMPLOYEES\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Rebecca. According to reports, this is actually the third round of cuts this year for the grocery chain, this time \uc2 Kroger\uc2  is laying off a thousand employees at the corporate level, so this will not affect employees who work in stores, manufacturing facilities or distribution centers.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now according to Reuters, in a memo to employees, interim CEO \uc2 Ron Sargent\uc2  said the cuts will simplify the organization and will free up money to lower grocery prices and to help them add stores, add hours for store employees. Now the company has given no indication that these cuts have anything to do with tariffs.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) They say people are looking to save at the grocery store, which is part of its efforts to lower prices. Now \uc2 Kroger\uc2  previously made back-to-back cuts in February and March, and they've recovering from a failed merger with \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  since last year. We reached out to \uc2 Kroger\uc2  for a response, Deborah, but they have not commented just yet.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250902el8r00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AIR CANADA STRIKE ENDS AFTER TENTATIVE DEAL REACHED AIRLINE SAYS IT CAN TAKE A WEEK TO GET BACK TO NORMAL OPERATIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 351 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE ERIN, RIP CURRENTS, BETSKER, NICHOLSON, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HURRICANE ERIN, WAVES, RIP CURRENTS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ZELENSKYY, PUTIN, WAR, MACRON, MERZ, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP PUSHES FOR ZELENSKYY-PUTIN MEETING\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, WAR, RUSSIA, SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LATEST REACTION TO HIGH-STAKES UKRAINE WAR SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 COLLIER, TEXAS, REDISTRICTING, TRUMP, MAIL-IN VOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS REDISTRICTING BATTLE\par \par \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2 , STRIKE, DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  STRIKE ENDS AFTER TENTATIVE DEAL REACHED\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to breaking news in the major airline strike that has impacted thousands of travelers. Transportation correspondent Gio Benitez is here with more. Good morning, Gio.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Michael, good morning. Yeah, this was a dramatic four days. But now, this morning, the breaking news right here, that strike is officially over, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, let's take you back in time and tell you what happened here, because 10,000 flight attendants had refused to get back to work, despite a federal order. The head of the union even said, they were willing to go to jail or be fined. This, of course, was all over pay. Now, the union says, they have reached a tentative agreement. So, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  was forced to cancel hundreds of flights, and said that half a million customers were affected by the strike both in Canada and right here in the United States. In America, United is \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 's partners, and they told \uc2 ABC News\uc2  that very few United customers were affected, so that's some good news there.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 , as you can imagine, they say that restarting a major airline is a huge effort. It could take about a week to get things fully back to normal. Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. And I'm sure people will be happy when it does get back to normal, Gio. They can get on their way. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250826el8j0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AIRLINE STRIKE TRIGGERS FLIGHT CHAOS \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  OPERATIONS HALTED AFTER UNION DEFIES GOVT. ORDER\b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ZELENSKYY, PUTIN, MACRON, MERZ, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, ATTACKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIA UNLEASHES DEADLY STRIKE\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE ERIN, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE ERIN STATE OF EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, HURRICANE ERIN, RIP CURRENTS, TRACK\par \par \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2 , STRIKE, TRAVEL, HANCOCK, ROUSSEAU, HAJDU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRLINE STRIKE TRIGGERS FLIGHT CHAOS\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the airline strike grounding hundreds of flights across North America. 10,000 \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  flight attendants defying a second order demanding they return to work. The strike now stranding 130,000 passengers a day. ABC's Gio Benitez covers aviation.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  OPERATIONS HALTED AFTER UNION DEFIES GOVT. ORDER\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Unpaid work, won't fly.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, 10,000 flight attendants on strike, grounding a major North American airline. Earlier today, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  saying the \uc2 Canada Industrial Relations Board\uc2  has declared the strike unlawful, ordering their flight attendants to get back on the job, but the \uc2 Canadian Union of Public Employees\uc2 , doubling down on their demands for better pay.\par \par \uc2 MARK HANCOCK\uc2  (CUPE NATIONAL PRESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 If it means folks like me going to jail, then so be it. If it means our union being fined, then so be it. We're looking for a solution here.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  forced to cancel hundreds of flights.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ROUSSEAU\uc2 . \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  CEO\par \par \uc2 We are harming our customers, and over 500,000 to date.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Travelers frustrated.\par \par \uc2 PASSENGER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Said that we have cancelled all our flights.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Canadian officials say the country, already hit by President Trump's tariffs, cannot afford to take risks with the economy.\par \par \uc2 PATTY HAJDU (CANADIAN MINISTER OF JOBS AND FAMILIES)\par \par \uc2 In a year where Canadian businesses and families have faced too much disruption and uncertainty, they shouldn't have to shoulder another blow.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Mary, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  says that it's providing customers with a full refund, travel credit or rebooking them on another airline. All flights will be canceled until at least tomorrow afternoon. Mary?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Gio, we know you'll stay on this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250820el8i00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CANADIAN GOVERNMENT MOVES TO END FLIGHT ATTENDANT STRIKE THE UNION FIGHTING FOR BETTER WAGES\b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE ERIN'S EFFECTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWERFUL STORM\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE ERIN, RIP CURRENTS\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE ERIN, RIP CURRENTS, WEATHER, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRACKING HURRICANE ERIN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ZELENSKYY, PUTIN, SUMMIT, RUSSIA, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT ZELENSKYY SET TO MEET WITH TRUMP MONDAY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PUTIN, SUMMIT, ZELENSKYY, WAR, UKRAINE, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2 , STRIKE, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANADIAN GOVERNMENT MOVES TO END FLIGHT ATTENDANT STRIKE\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to that airline strike that has grounded hundreds of flights and stranded thousands of travelers this weekend. \uc2 Canada's government\uc2  ordering \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  flight attendants back to work and sending that contract dispute to arbitration. ABC's Olivia Rubin is here with the latest on that. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, good morning. Good morning, guys. So, the airline now saying this strike is over. Flights will resume after you said the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  stepped in here, ending this flight attendant strike that had brought the entire airline to a halt, stranding thousands of travelers around the world. Remember, 10,000 flight attendants walked off the job Saturday morning seeking better pay, canceling over 700 flights. That's according to the airline. And that's left up to 130,000 passengers stranded. So, the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  stepping in, intervening. The federal jobs minister saying the strike was rapidly impacting Canada's economy and directing both sides back to work and into binding arbitration.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) A government board overnight ordering the flight attendants back to work by 2:00 PM today, the airline says. And remember, the union was fighting for better wages here, saying their salaries were below minimum wage, below the poverty line, and that sometimes they were not paid at all for hours of work they did when planes are on the ground such as boarding. The airline, though, said they offered a 38% rise in total compensation. That's over the next four years.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  says flights will be coming back online starting today. But still, it might take several days for them fully to get back up to speed, guys. So, we could still see more cancellations.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this isn't over yet. They still have to work this out.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. It does seem like some terms were put in place by that independent board, though nothing there has been released on what exactly they're getting or not getting.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But a complete 180 from where we were yesterday. So, some good news there. Thank you so much, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250821el8h0005q\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 10,000 \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  FLIGHT ATTENDANTS GO ON STRIKE FLIGHTS AND PASSENGERS ARE AFFECTED\b0\par\par\uc2 656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PUTIN, SUMMIT, WAR, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 INTERNATIONAL, REACTION, TRUMP, PUTIN, SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PUTIN, SUMMIT, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE, ERIN\par \par \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2 , FLIGHT, ATTENDANT, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, DC, POLICE\par \par \uc2 CANTRELL, CRIMINAL, CHARGES\par \par \uc2 TEEN, DROWNS, NEW JERSEY\par \par \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , FINES, UNIVERSITY, MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 ROGERS, DEATH\par \par \uc2 DRAKE, RECORD, LABEL, LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 AUTISM, GENDER\par \par \uc2 BOARD, GAME, NIGHT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PUTIN, ZELENSKYY, ALASKA, RUSSIA, UKRAINE, PEACE DEAL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PUTIN, SUMMIT, ZELENSKYY, KYIV, RUSSIA, UKRAINE, MEDVEDEV\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE ERIN, CARRIBEAN, PUERTO RICO, CATEGORY 4, TROPICAL STORM\par \par \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2 , FLIGHT ATTENDANTS, STRIKE, CIRIUM\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. For now, though, we turn to breaking news overnight as a flight attendant strike shuts down \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 . Hundreds of flights and tens of thousands of passengers affected in Canada and the US. ABC's Olivia Rubin is here with those details. Good morning, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 10,000 \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  FLIGHT ATTENDANTS GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, good morning, guys. Good morning, Janai. So, passengers should be double-checking their flights this morning. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  is shutting down after 10,000 flight attendants went on strike, walking off the job just before 1:00 am this morning.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the impacts here are far-reaching. Over 700 \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  and Air Canada Rouge flights have been canceled since yesterday, over 400 just today so far. This is impacting up to 130,000 passengers per day. And while the airline is the largest in Canada, it's going to affect travelers here, too, at home. The airline operates hundreds of flights per week in and out of the United States. That's according to data from Cirium, an aviation analytics company.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIR CANADA STRIKE CANCELLATIONS\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, what really are the sticking points here in the negotiations? Well, it comes down to wages. The flight attendants' - union says salaries are below the rate of inflation and below minimum wage. The union also saying many flight attendants go largely without pay for some of the work that they do while planes are on the ground, not in the air, and that includes boarding time. The airline, though, saying they've offered a 38% raise in total compensation. That's over the next four years, including a 4 to 8% raise for pay on the ground in the first year.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIR CANADA STRIKE\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But the sides are still at a standstill. So, what should you do if your flight is canceled? Well, the airline very clear this morning. Don't go to the airport. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  says they have a flexible rebooking policy right now for customers to change, delay their flight, no extra cost. So, keep an eye on those notifications. Don't go to the airport.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, but...\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, good advice.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...but like you said, it carries the Canadian flag, but so many flights (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you so much, Olivia. And coming up here on "Good Morning America," the Morning Menu. The changes coming to some of your favorite breakfast foods and snacks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY: NATURALLY-DYED SNACKS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also ahead here, the safety issue most people never consider. Are their life jackets counterfeit? One mom found something very scary when she bought a life jacket for her son. So, Gio here, went to the experts.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAFETY ALERT: LIFE JACKETS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And she sashayed her way into our lives on "RuPaul's Drag Race." Jinkx Monsoon is here to talk, "Oh, Mary." We are so excited for Jinkx. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) See, two Jinkx right there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JINKX MONSOON LIVE!\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250826el8g0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  SUSPENDS OPERATIONS AMID STRIKE STOPPAGE STRANDED 10,000+ TRAVELERS DURING PEAK SUMMER TRAVEL\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , PUTIN, SUMMIT, CEASEFIRE, ZELENSKYY, WAR, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , DONESTK, CEASEFIRE, SANCTIONS, PUTIN, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STRIKES AMID NO CEASEFIRE DEAL\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, TRACK, HURRICANE ERIN, WIND, FLASH FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE ERIN POWERFUL CATEGORY 5\par \par \uc2 BOYLE, RIP CURRENTS, HURRICANE ERIN, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS ON ALERT FOR DANGEROUS RIP CURRENTS\par \par \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2 , STRIKE, HAJDU, BLANDINI, SPEEDIE, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  SUSPENDS OPERATIONS AMID STRIKE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the major airline strike impacting more than 100,000 travelers around the world. The \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  has ordered \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 's striking flight attendants back to work and into arbitration. The work stoppage coming during the peak summer travel season. Here's ABC's Marcus Moore.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STOPPAGE STRANDED 10,000+ TRAVELERS DURING PEAK SUMMER TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, \uc2 Canada's government\uc2  ordering \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  flight attendants who walked off the job early this morning back to work.\par \par \uc2 PATTY HAJDU (MINISTER OF JOBS AND FAMILIES CANADA)\par \par \uc2 A work stoppage would cause thousands of Canadians to be stranded abroad and across this country, and this is simply unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The move just 12 hours after the country's largest airline halted operations. Tens of thousands of customers stranded and scrambling to find alternate flights.\par \par \uc2 SARAH BLANDINI (STRANDED \uc2 AIR CANADA\uc2  PASSENGER)\par \par \uc2 I understand. I'm in solidarity with strikers in general. But, I mean, why do we have to pay the consequences of this?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The union representing some 10,000 flight attendants says, despite the order, the strike is still on. Frustration growing tonight. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  suspending nearly all of its 700 daily flights, affecting more than 100,000 customers worldwide. Ticket counters now empty. Flight boards show dozens of canceled flights.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Hey, hey, ho, ho (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Flight attendants on the picket line outside airport terminals in Montreal, Toronto, and Vancouver now calling for higher wages.\par \par \uc2 LILLIAN SPEEDIE (VICE PRESIDENT CUPE LOCAL 4092)\par \par \uc2 We can't use food banks. We can't live 12 to a house. We can't be sleeping in our cars anymore.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, the union says it's seeking a fair deal at the time when the airline is posting record profits. And, Whit, the union calling for this strike to go on.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Marcus Moore for us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020250819el8g00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STOCKS TUMBLE IN WAKE OF NEW TARIFFS, WEAK JOBS REPORT FMR. TREASURY SECRETARY SUMMERS ON LATEST ECONOMIC NEWS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,901 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2025 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MCENTARFER, ECONOMY, JOBS, LUMMIS, SARIN, SUMMERS, FED, TRADE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Suppressing statisticians is a time honored tool for leaders trying to solidify their power and stifle dissent. It's happened throughout history. Most recently in Venezuela and Turkey, where presidents punished officials and economists who do not tow the party line. It has not been part of the American tradition, but late Friday, it happened here. President Trump fired the head of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  after baselessly claiming she rigged July's employment report. Today, we will examine what that means for our economy and our democracy. Chief Washington Correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  starts us off.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STOCKS TUMBLE IN WAKE OF NEW TARIFFS, WEAK JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) It was a double dose of bad economic news for President Trump on Friday, as the market reacted to his latest tariffs and the grim new jobs report showing the unemployment rate up and new jobs down. The stock market tumbled. The Dow losing two point nine percent on the week. Its worst weekly performance since early April when Trump first announced his liberation day trade war.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 My fellow Americans, this is liberation day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP FIRES OFFICIAL WHO OVERSEES JOBS, INFLATION DATA\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President's reaction to the bad economic news, first, shoot the messenger. He fired the person responsible for compiling the data, the Chair of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , Erika McEntarfer.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, why did you fire the head of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Because I think her numbers were wrong. Just like I thought her numbers were wrong before the election. Days before the election.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Without providing any evidence, Trump accused McEntarfer of fudging the numbers to make him look bad. Also insisting, again with no evidence, that she had rigged job data ahead of last year's election to help \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . In fact, the final jobs report before the election didn't help Harris at all. It was even worse than this week's report.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM NEWS COVERAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US MARKETS\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN (\uc2 ALLIANZ\uc2  CHIEF ECONOMIC ADVISER)\par \par \uc2 There isn't much evidence that they are politically driven all this. It's just the nature of the exercise.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  is non-partisan. It compiles economic data that has been used by economists, investors and academics for more than a century. As for the now fired McEntarer, she was confirmed by the Senate in January of 2024 in an overwhelmingly bipartisan vote of 86 to 8. In fact, then Senator JD Vance voted for her. William Beach, who was appointed by Trump to serve as \uc2 BLS\uc2  Commissioner during his first term, said the firing, quote, "Sets a dangerous precedent and undermines the statistical mission of the Bureau," while calling McEntarfer, quote, "a very fine analyst and a good colleague." At least one Republican senator suggested Trump's move was, quote, "impetuous."\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CYNTHIA LUMMIS (REP) (WYOMING)\par \par \uc2 It's not a statisticians fault if the numbers are accurate and that they're not what the President had hoped for.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ECONOMISTS WARN OF PRICE HIKES AS NEW TARIFFS ANNOUNCED\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) In addition to shooting the messenger, the President is pointing fingers, putting the blame for economic turbulence on \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 . He appointed Powell as the Federal Reserve Chairman in 2017. But this week he called him a, quote, "stubborn moron," for failing to cut interest rates. All of this comes as the President announced a new wave of tariffs set to go into effect this Thursday. This new round raises taxes on imported products from nearly 70 countries. Many economists are warning the impact of the earlier tariffs is now just being felt and that consumers are going to see higher prices.\par \par \uc2 NATASHA SARIN (YALE BUDGET LAB PRESIDENT & CO-FOUNDER)\par \par \uc2 Apparel prices are going up by some 35 percent as a result of these tariffs. And computer prices are going to go up by some 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Trump's tariffs are now facing a serious legal challenge, a federal appeals court raising questions about whether the President has the authority to impose across the board tariffs without congressional approval.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Previous trade deals including Nafta and the US/Mexico/Canada agreement that Trump negotiated during his first term didn't go into effect until they were approved by Congress. If Trump loses in court, it could upend most of his new tariffs and also the various trade deals that he has negotiated over the last several weeks. George?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 , thanks. After the President fired the head of the \uc2 BLS\uc2  on Friday, we invited the White House to provide a guest to respond. They declined. But we're are joined now by former Treasure Secretary \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 . Larry, thank you for joining us this morning. I guess this firing of the \uc2 BLS\uc2  commissioner goes in the category of shocking but not surprising?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FMR. TREASURY SECRETARY SUMMERS ON LATEST ECONOMIC NEWS\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2  (FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. I mean, this is way beyond anything that \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  ever did. I'm surprised that other officials have not responded by resigning themselves as took place when \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  fired people lawlessly. This is a preposterous charge. These numbers are put together by teams of literally hundreds of people following detailed procedures that are in manuals. There's no conceivable way that the head of the \uc2 BLS\uc2  could have manipulated this number. The numbers are in line with what we are seeing from all kinds of private sector sources. This is the stuff of democracies giving way to authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP FIRES BLS HEAD FOLLOWING WEAK JOBS REPORT, REVISION\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2  (FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 It, firing statisticians goes with threatening the heads of newspapers. It goes with launching assaults on universities. It goes with launching assaults on law firms that defend clients that the elected boss finds uncongenial. This is really scary stuff. And it can hardly be surprising that when the rule of law is a bit in question that there's a big uncertainty premium in the markets that is operating to both make there be less investment, which slows the economy down, and also means there's less supply so that there's more inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Adding to that uncertainty is the President's campaign against \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , the head of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , saying he has been a moron, I think was the word the President used for not lowering interest rates so far. What's the impact of that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FED LEAVES INTEREST RATES UNCHANGED DESPITE TRUMP PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2  (FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 Look, I think that this kind of political Fed bashing is a fool's game. The Fed doesn't listen. So, short-term interest rates aren't going to be different because of it. The market does listen. So, longer-term interest rates are going to be higher, which is going to make it more expensive to buy a house. This is hurting the economy, not helping. I think the President understands that. And what the President is doing is recognizing that for all kinds of reasons of which his policies are very important ones, the economy is at a lot of risk. And he is looking to set up a scapegoat if the economy performs badly. That's what this attacking Chairman Powell is really about. It's not really about trying to change policy. There's no chance that that's going to happen to any substantial degree.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) The, the, the jobs report on Friday probably does increase the chances that the Fed will cut interest rates in, in September. What's your take on what that report told us?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 73K JOBS ADDED IN JULY; PRIOR MONTH FIGURES REVISED DOWN 258K\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2  (FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 I think it told us that the economy is closer to stall speed than we thought that it was. The July number was weak. The big deal is the downwards revision for the two months before that. And that means there's a real possibility that we're in a stall speed kind of economy, which means we could tip over into recession. That wouldn't be my prediction right now. But the risk is greater certainly than it was before. And it's a risk we don't need to be taking. But it's a risk that's made more serious by these tariffs. What your viewers should understand is that these tariffs are not job creators.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP UNVEILS SWEEPING TARIFFS IN 'NEW SYSTEM OF TRADE'\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2  (FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 When you raise tariffs on steel, for example, yeah, there's some people who work in the steel industry, but there are 50 times as many who work industries like the automobile industry who are now going to be much less competitive when they try to compete all over the world. So, this is an immense gift that we are giving to our country's adversaries by alienating our allies like Canada, like Europe. We are making it much easier for China to grow and flourish in the global economy. And I just don't understand why we would want to do that, especially when what we're getting out of it is an increase of more than two thousand dollars in the bills that typical middle class families are going to have to pay.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, the markets have been pretty complacent about the tariffs so far. Are we seeing their impact in this underlying jobs report?\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2  (FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 I, I think that that is an element in it. I think both the direct effects of the tariffs, but probably more importantly, the sense of uncertainty that anything could happen and who knows what business is going to be attacked next, who knows what the rules are going to be. In an environment like that, what should a business do? It should sit and it should wait. Wait in terms of hiring people. Wait in terms of new factory construction. What's keeping the economy going in significant part is not anything actually that's coming out of the President's policies.\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2  (FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 It is all of the investment that we're having in data centers, which is coming out of the tremendous progress that some companies are making in technology, and that's keeping the economy going. But we're taking needless risks here, needless risks both on the unemployment side and, George, on the inflation side, which makes it harder for us to respond to any incipient recession threat and when inflation is above target and when Americans say that cost of living is the biggest issue that's facing them.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , thanks for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2  (FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020250808el8300002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRUMP FIRES OFFICIAL AFTER WEAK JOBS REPORT CLAIMS WEAKER-THAN-EXPECTED NUMBERS WERE "MANIPULATED")\b0\par\par\uc2 867 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , JOBS, ECONOMY, MCENTARFER, LUMMIS, TARIFFS, KUGLER, RESIGNATION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thanks for joining on this Friday night. I'm \uc2 Whit Johnson\uc2 , in for David. And we begin tonight with President Trump angry over a disappointing jobs report, firing a Labor Department official who releases the data.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP FIRES OFFICIAL AFTER WEAK JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That report coming just hours after the President slapped new tariffs on dozens of countries. The one-two punch triggering a stock market slide.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOW JONES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 43,588.58, DOWN, 542.40, 1.23%\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Dow closing down 542 points. All major indexes showing losses. Twenty-four 24 hours ago, President Trump announced higher tariffs on imports from nearly 70 countries, ranging from at least 10% to as high as 40%, taking effect in six days on August 7th.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JULY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 73,000 NEW JOBS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNEMPLOYMENT: 4.2%\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, that disappointing jobs report, just 73,000 jobs added last month. Unemployment ticking up to 4.2%.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAY & JUNE REVISED: 258,000 FEW JOBS COMBINED\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And the shocker, numbers for May and June revised sharply down, losing 258,000 jobs combined.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, President Trump has fired the commissioner of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . And now news that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , a top official is stepping down. ABC's chief Washington correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  leads us off at the White House.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLAIMS WEAKER-THAN-EXPECTED NUMBERS WERE "MANIPULATED"\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, President Trump abruptly fired the nonpartisan economist responsible for government statistics on the job market, claiming, without providing evidence, that today's weaker-than-expected jobs report was, quote, "manipulated."\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mr. President, why did you fire the head of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Because I think their numbers were wrong, just like I thought her numbers were wrong before the election, days before the election.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The now-fired \uc2 BLS\uc2  Commissioner Erika McEntarfer was confronted by the Senate in 2024 by an overwhelming bipartisan vote, 86-8. Then-Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  voted for her. Her firing coming shortly after today's jobs report showed the unemployment rate has risen to 4.2% with the economy adding just 73,000 jobs in July, less than expected. \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  also revised the job numbers for the last two months as it often does, saying there were over 250,000 fewer jobs created in May and June than previously thought.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I believe the numbers were phony.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President claimed, without providing evidence, that the numbers were rigged to make him look bad. "She will be replaced with someone much more competent and qualified," Trump declared on social media. "Important numbers like this must be fair and accurate, they can't be manipulated for political purposes." But many economists expressed alarm at the firing, worried that it's now President Trump who is trying to manipulate the numbers. And at least one Republican senator, a Trump ally, suggested Trump's move was, quote, "impetuous."\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CYNTHIA LUMMIS (REP) (WYOMING)\par \par \uc2 It's not the statistician's fault if the numbers are accurate and that they're not like the President had hoped for.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP ANNOUNCES NEW TARIFFS AGAINST NEARLY 70 COUNTRIES\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) All this after the President announced a new wave of tariffs set to go into effect next week, raising the tax on imported products from nearly 70 countries. That coupled with the jobs report sent the stock market tumbling today. The Dow losing 2.9% this week, its worst week since early April when Trump's trade war began. But Trump's tariffs are now facing serious legal challenge. A federal appeals court raising questions about whether the President has the authority to impose the across-the-board tariffs without congressional approval.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2  back with us now from the White House. And, Jon, the economic news today coming as President Trump ramps up his criticism against Federal Reserve chairman, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 . And now, a top official is stepping down?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FED BOARD MEMBER RESIGNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KUGLER'S EARLY EXIT ALLOWS FOR TRUMP APPOINTMENT\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, \uc2 Adriana Kugler\uc2 , she's one of seven members of the Fed's Board of Governors and a Biden appointee, announced her resignation today. She didn't give the reason why. But her term, Whit, was scheduled to expire in January and her departure gives President Trump the opportunity to name a new Fed governor, perhaps giving him more influence over the Fed, and also the chance to name somebody, put somebody on the Board who could be a replacement for \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  when his term as chairman expires next year.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Jon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250805el8100002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MASS FEDERAL LAYOFFS MORE THAN 1,300 STATE DEPT. EMPLOYEES LOSE JOBS\b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , FEMA, FLOODING, SEARCH, HANNA\par \par \uc2 HEAT, WILDFIRE, GRAND CANYON, TORNADO, STORMS, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE EVACUATIONS & TORNADO THREATS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS, TORNADO WATCH, HEAT, TRACK\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , EPSTEIN, LIST, BONDI, BONGINO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: FIERY CLASH OVER EPSTEIN FILES\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, LARSON, IMMIGRATION, RAIDS, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: $50,000 REWARD FOR GUN SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 STATE DEPARTMENT, LAYOFFS, RUBIO, KIM, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS FEDERAL LAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the emotional scene at the State Department a short time ago. More than 1,300 workers laid off just days after the Supreme Court cleared the way for the Trump administration to make sweeping cuts. ABC News obtaining an email informing some workers they received layoff notices by mistake. ABC's Rachel Scott reports from the White House tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE THAN 1,300 STATE DEPT. EMPLOYEES LOSE JOBS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, an emotional scene at the State Department. Mass layoffs now underway. Workers walking out carrying boxes. Their colleagues putting signs in the window. "You don't deserve this." "We love you." "Thank you for your service." Notices going out to some 1,100 civil service employees and 246 foreign service officers, axed as part of the Trump administration's move to shrink the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO (SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 We took a very deliberate step to reorganize the State Department to be more efficient and more focused.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God. Did you survive?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Among the areas targeted, programs designed to protect human rights and promote democracy and divisions dealing with refugees and immigration.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ANDY KIM (DEM) (NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 If you are doing this out of love of country...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats warn the effects of these cuts could be far-reaching.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What kind of impact is this going to have?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ANDY KIM (DEM) (NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 The rest of the world is just watching this with their jaw dropped. But it's also probably the happiest day for some of our adversaries and our competitors. I can imagine Vladimir Putin's very happy today seeing what kind of decimation is happening to our State Department.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Linsey, another twist in all of this tonight. We are learning some employees across multiple State Department divisions got layoff notices by mistake. They are now receiving emails letting them know they still have jobs, Linsey.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott reporting from the White House. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250715el7b00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PHILLY STRIKE ENDS TENTATIVE DEAL REACHED\b0\par\par\uc2 692 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DEADLY FLOOD EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 RAIN, FLOODING, BURN SCAR, DEATHS, CRAWFORD, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, DEATHS, MISSING, ABBOTT, ROBERSON, CAMP, JONES, LEITHA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PUTIN, UKRAINE, WEAPONS, PAUSE, ATTACK\par \par \uc2 TSA, SHOES, NOEM, AIRPORTS, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 FTC\uc2 , CANCEL, SETBACK, CONSUMERS, COURT\par \par \uc2 WIMBLEDON, SHELTON, FRITZ, ANISIMOVA, SEMIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA"\par \par \uc2 MURDER, STEWART, PADDLEBOARD, MYSTERY, BETTS\par \par \uc2 BURROW, BREAK-INS, BURGLARS, ATHLETES, "QUARTERBACK"\par \par \uc2 SLOUGH, BAHAMAS, SNORKELING, BOAT, PROPELLER, ICU, ARREST\par \par \uc2 PRIME DAYS, SHOPPING, DEALS, RETAILERS, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 MLB, GIANTS, WALK OFF HOMER, BAILEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS TEXAS RAIN TRACK\par \par \uc2 NEW MEXICO, FLOOD\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, FLOOD, DEAD, MISSING\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA, STRIKE, ENDS\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to turn now to breaking news overnight. A tentative agreement has been reached between workers and the city of Philadelphia. Trash was piling up on the streets for nine days after some 10,000 workers walked off the job. And Matt Rivers is back here in studio with more details. Hello again to you, Matt.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PHILLY STRIKE ENDS AFTER TENTATIVE DEAL REACHED\par \par \uc2 MATT RIVERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Will. Nine days of sanitation workers being off the job means all that trash piles up awfully quickly. But the message from the mayor and the union this morning, the strike is over. The mayor making an announcement overnight saying negotiations with the union, district council 33, finally made it over the line. The sides agreeing to a 14 percent pay raise over her term in office. The union president saying the deal isn't perfect, but thought it was in the members' best interest, saying workers will be back on the job either today or tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MATT RIVERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Some 9,000 workers were off the job, leading to trash out on the streets quickly becoming a problem, especially in this summer heat. You know, we were down in Philly yesterday. We saw it with our own eyes, did cover this. It was what everyone was talking about, so no doubt there will be plenty of people happy in Philadelphia to see this coming to an end, guys.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question about that.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Matt, thanks very much. Coming up in our "GMA" Morning Menu, The Science of You. It's tick season. Becky Worley is gonna find out how well DEET and more natural repellants work.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE SCIENCE OF YOU\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also ahead, the new cast members joining Meryl Streep and the stars of "The Devil Wears Prada 2."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS!\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, Michelin Star chef Danny Grant is here showing us how to make the perfect steak. He's calling it, shot, his shot, he called it fantastic. And Lara and Sam are with Lori B.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUMMER'S BEST BITES\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's ... wheel in those knives, Sal. Massive discounts on major retailers. Lori Bergamotto is here with our first feature deal. Let's hear it.\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. Talk about slashing prices, you guys. This magnetic...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Love the (inaudible) one.\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...magnetic knife bucket, it's magnetic, so cool.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's cool.\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Has a deep price cut on Amazon.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sam, don't you.\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) From $74.99 down to $35.98. Sam, don't touch those knives.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I like how your face in the close-up is, like, a smile with knives. It's like a horror movie.\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gift with purpose.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is a great deal, and they are great knives we hear, and we have even more deals on the way coming up on "GMA." Let's get that close-up one more time.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Scary. Scary.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250717el790000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PHILADELPHIA CITY WORKERS STRIKE ENTERS SECOND WEEK BOTH SIDES ARE TRYING TO GET A DEAL DONE FOR WAGES AND BENEFITS\b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 FLOODING, GROSS, BELLOWS, MERCHANT, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 WARNING SYSTEM, DISASTERS, WILLIAMS, PATRICK, GROSS, CRUZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW QUESTIONS ABOUT WARNING SYSTEM IN FLOOD ZONE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS, HEAT ALERTS, WIND, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE PAIN POSSIBLE FOR HARD HIT PARTS OF TEXAS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, TARIFFS, ECONOMY, TRADE, WEAPONS, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP THREATENS NEW TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 UNION STRIKE, NEGOTIATIONS, WAGES, BENEFITS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PHILADELPHIA CITY WORKERS STRIKE ENTERS SECOND WEEK\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn now to the Philadelphia City workers strike entering its second week. The city set to resume negotiations today with the union representing about 9,000 workers. And Matt Rivers is in Philadelphia with more. Matt, I hear it's getting pretty stinky there, and we see that garbage piling up behind you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Rebecca. That is what happens when 9,000 city workers go out on strike. People leaving their trash there in a designated drop-off spot. Others just leaving it in the street in front of their houses. This strike now eight days and counting.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The good news here is that officials from both sides are expected to meet later today to try and get a deal done. The main sticking points here, wages and benefits. The union saying the city won't budge from initial offers deemed not good enough. The city saying they're negotiating in good faith. But meanwhile, the sanitation workers off the job, those mountains of trash piling up, people even leaving furniture out on the street as the city struggles to keep up. And so, again, we do expect officials from both sides to meet today. The hope of so many here is that they'll get a deal done because dealing with that, George, all summer long, not a very appealing option.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not at all. Okay, Matt, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250808el7800006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PHILADELPHIA UNION WORKERS NEGOTIATIONS DEADLOCKED STARS CANCEL PERFORMANCES AMID PHILLY STRIKE\b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DISASTER ZONE\par \par \uc2 FLASH FLOODING, CAMP MYSTIC, SEARCH, ABBOTT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES IN TEXAS FLOODING\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, TRACK, TROPICAL STORM CHANTAL, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 TSA, HOLIDAY TRAVEL, FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD-BREAKING TRAVEL WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY TRAVEL, TRAFFIC, GAS PRICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY TRAVEL: AAA PROJECTS 61 MILLION ON THE ROADS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, TARIFFS, ECONOMY, SPENDING BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP SET TO SEND TARIFF LETTERS TO SOME COUNTRIES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IMMIGRATION, DEPORTATION, FUNDING, SPENDING BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW BILL BOOSTS FUNDING FOR DEPORTATIONS\par \par \uc2 UNION STRIKE, WAGES, NEGOTIATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PHILADELPHIA UNION WORKERS NEGOTIATIONS DEADLOCKED\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to that ongoing strike in Philadelphia significantly impacting essential services. The city and union workers are back at the negotiating table. ABC's Olivia Rubin joins us with the sticking points for both sides. Good morning, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Stephanie. This strike has now stretched into its sixth day. Day six, that's a long time. And despite these ongoing negotiations, there still doesn't seem to be any progress on a deal.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, trash continuing to pile up on the streets of Philadelphia as negotiations between the city and its largest blue-collar workers union remain deadlocked.\par \par \uc2 RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Everybody is upset and everybody is dumping their trash here.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both sides heading back to the negotiating table this weekend for the first time in days. But still no new agreement to end that strike. Workers taking to the streets once again. That union known as the AFSCME District Council 33 representing over 9,000 workers, including some of the city's most critical, including trash collectors and 911 dispatchers. Now some of those essential employees have been off the job for nearly a week after walking off on Tuesday at 12:01 AM calling for better wages. There's no indication on how close the sides are. In negotiations earlier this week, the union seeking a 5% pay increase each of the next three years.\par \par \uc2 GREG BOULWARE (PRESIDENT DISTRICT COUNCIL 33)\par \par \uc2 Our place right now is 555 over the course of three years.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The city's mayor Cherelle Parker saying she's offered more than a 13% pay increase over her four-year term calling it, quote, "historic" but also warning there is a limit to how far she will go.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CHERELLE PARKER (DEM) (PHILADELPHIA)\par \par \uc2 I will not put the fiscal stability of the City of Philadelphia in jeopardy for no one.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STARS CANCEL PERFORMANCES AMID PHILLY STRIKE\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The pressure on the city mounting. Celebrity performers LL Cool J and Jazmine Sullivan backing out of the city's 4th of July celebration saying they're standing with the workers.\par \par \uc2 LL COOL J (RAPPER AND ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 There's absolutely no way that I could perform across a picket line, and pick up money when I know that people are out there fighting for a living wage.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, there's no indication of what exactly came out of those negotiations yesterday or when they may resume. Tomorrow, for those counting, would be day seven. Gio?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Philly watching that very closely. Olivia, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250709el760005s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 OVER 9,000 PUBLIC WORKERS ON STRIKE IN PHILADELPHIA HEADLINERS PULL OUT OF PERFORMANCES IN PHILADELPHIA\b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TEXAS, FLOODS\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, BIG BEAUTIFUL BILL\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, DRONE, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 PARTEY, CHARGED, RAPE, SEXUAL ASSAULT\par \par \uc2 SHARK, SIGHTINGS, NEW YORK\par \par \uc2 WORKER, STRIKE, PHILADELPHIA\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, from New York to Philadelphia now where, with the worker strike ongoing, garbage piling up as huge crowds pack the city for Independence Day, and headliners, including LL Cool J pulling out of big performances in solidarity. ABC's Olivia Rubin joins us now with more. Good morning, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OVER 9,000 PUBLIC WORKERS ON STRIKE IN PHILADELPHIA\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Janai, good morning. This is day five of this strike that you're talking about, and this is the largest blue collar workers union in the city, and the effects there are intensifying. Over 9,000 of Philadelphia's critical workers, think sanitation workers, 911 dispatchers, and members of the Water Department, walked off the job Tuesday at 12:01, demanding better pay and working conditions. 4th of July celebrations did move forward there, but the largest Independence Day parade in this, in the nation losing some of its headlining performers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEADLINERS PULL OUT OF PERFORMANCES IN PHILADELPHIA\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) LL Cool J, as you said, standing with those workers, backing out of his performance. He said on social media, quote, "there's absolutely no way that I could perform across a picket line and pick up money when I know that people out there are fighting for a living wage." R&B singer and Philly native Jazmine Sullivan soon following suit there. The union known as DC 33 praising those cancellations as, quote, "amazing news," as they try to force this deal here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GARBAGE PILES UP AMID PHILADELPHIA PUBLIC WORKERS' STRIKE\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RUBIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Another big problem though is the trash. Garbage is piling up on the streets as negotiations hit a standstill. There's been no word on when those negotiations might resume since they ended on Wednesday without a deal. The union most recently proposing a 5 percent wage increase per year for the next three years according to our Philadelphia station WPVI. The city countering, proposing essentially 2 percent per year. The city's mayor Cherelle Parker previously said she, quote, "did right by the blue collar men and women of the district council 33 with her offer." Speaking on the 4th of July, she said, while the strike is, quote, "not preferred," it is a right. Gio?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hopefully they get those negotiations going again very, very soon. Olivia Rubin, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250723el750005s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEGOTIATIONS RESUME AFTER UNION STRIKE GARBAGE PILES UP AFTER CITY FAILS TO REACH A DEAL WITH WORKERS\b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 FLOODING, DEATH TOLL, CAMPERS, RICE, GUILLEN, NOEM\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, FLOODING, TROPICAL STORM CHANTAL, STORM, TRACK\par \par \uc2 RIP CURRENTS, FLOODING, FIREWORKS, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS RIP CURRENTS ALONG EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 UNION STRIKE, WAGES, LL COOL J, BOULWARE, PARKER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEGOTIATIONS RESUME AFTER UNION STRIKE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the union strike in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Negotiations resuming today after garbage workers who were among the nearly 10,000 to walk off the job five days ago, seeking better pay and benefits. But trash is now piling up.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And 4th of July celebrations impacted when major stars cancelled the performances. Here's ABC's Perry Russom.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GARBAGE PILES UP AFTER CITY FAILS TO REACH A DEAL WITH WORKERS\par \par \uc2 PERRY RUSSOM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As bags of garbage piled up in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's starting to smell already as you all can see.\par \par \uc2 PERRY RUSSOM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, contract negotiations resume for the thousands of union workers on strike. More than 9,000 members from District Council 33 demanding higher wages. Their jobs include fixing roads, answering 911 calls, and picking up trash in the city. Grammy winners Jazmine Sullivan and LL Cool J, headliners for the city's Independence Day concert cancelling their performances.\par \par \uc2 LL COOL J (RAPPER)\par \par \uc2 There's absolutely no way that I can perform across a picket line.\par \par \uc2 PERRY RUSSOM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Members of DC 33 reportedly make on average $46,000 a year. Union President Greg Boulware says, he wants incremental wage increases.\par \par \uc2 GREG BOULWARE (PRESIDENT AFSCME DISTRICT COUNCIL 33)\par \par \uc2 Our place right now is 555 over the course of three years.\par \par \uc2 PERRY RUSSOM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mayor Cherelle Parker says the city offered a more than 13% wage increase over her four-year term.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CHERELLE PARKER (PHILADELPHIA)\par \par \uc2 I will not put the fiscal stability of the city of Philadelphia in jeopardy for no one.\par \par \uc2 PERRY RUSSOM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The mayor striking a softer tone yesterday.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CHERELLE PARKER (PHILADELPHIA)\par \par \uc2 We are working extremely hard doing everything that we can do, Philadelphia, to reach a fair and fiscally responsible contract.\par \par \uc2 PERRY RUSSOM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A judge ordered workers from the medical examiner's office who were on strike to get back to work. The city says there were delays in picking up dead bodies. A 19-year-old was shot and killed on Thursday, his body lying in the street for hours. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just awful news. Perry Russom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020250708el7500005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW BILL BOOSTS FUNDING FOR DEPORTATIONS TRUMP ADMINISTRATION SENDS EIGHT MEN TO SOUTH SUDAN\b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 FLOODING, DEATH TOLL, CAMPERS, RICE, GUILLEN, NOEM\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, FLOODING, TROPICAL STORM CHANTAL, STORM, TRACK\par \par \uc2 RIP CURRENTS, FLOODING, FIREWORKS, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS RIP CURRENTS ALONG EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 UNION STRIKE, WAGES, LL COOL J, BOULWARE, PARKER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEGOTIATIONS RESUME AFTER UNION STRIKE\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, CEASEFIRE, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , TRUMP, GAZA, AID, GHF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL SENDS CEASEFIRE NEGOTIATORS TO DOHA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IMMIGRATION, SOUTH SUDAN, SPENDING BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW BILL BOOSTS FUNDING FOR DEPORTATIONS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to President Trump's so-called Big Beautiful bill, ramping up the immigration crackdown with $170 billion, a massive and historic infusion of cash for detention and deportation. Here's ABC's senior White House correspondent Selina Wang.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP ADMINISTRATION SENDS EIGHT MEN TO SOUTH SUDAN\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the first image of the eight Trump administration detainees heading to South Sudan after the Supreme Court cleared the way for their removal to the war-torn nation. The undocumented migrants had been held at a military base in Djibouti as the legal battle played out. All of the men have been convicted of serious crimes, but only one is from South Sudan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: $170 BILLION FOR BORDER WALL, DETENTION CENTERS & AGENTS\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This comes as President Trump celebrates a major political victory, signing into law a sweeping domestic bill that makes a historic $170 billion investment in immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 So, you have the biggest tax cut, the biggest spending cut, the largest border security investment in American history. We're going to have strong borders.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE BIG BEAUTIFUL BILL ACT\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The new law includes funding for the border wall, more detention beds and $100 billion for Immigrations and Customs Enforcement. The administration promising more detention centers like this, dubbed Alligator Alcatraz by state and federal officials, opening this week in Florida. Two hundred Marines also sent to the State to help with immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We've got to work with the farmers.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the President acknowledging the hardships for farmers who employ a large percentage of undocumented workers, floating the idea for allowing those migrants to stay in the US.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 If a farmer is willing to vouch for these people, in some way, Kristi, I think we're going to have to say that's going to be good. Right? We don't want to do it where we take all of the workers off the farms.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Despite Trump's repeated promises to protect farmers, immigration raids, like this one on a strawberry field in California, have continued.\par \par \uc2 FARM WORKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 (Speaking foreign language)\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 "We wake up in fear," this farm worker says. Many are concerned that if they go to work, they won't ever come back.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Whit, those immigration raids are putting cities across America on edge, especially in Los Angeles, where some of those July 4th celebrations were cancelled. Fear is keeping many undocumented migrants inside, skipping school, work, even family gatherings, afraid of being swept up and detained. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Selina Wang, our thanks to you tonight.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020250708el7500007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THOUSANDS OF PHILADELPHIA CITY WORKERS GO ON STRIKE WORKERS WANT 8% INCREASE FOR EACH YEAR OF A FOUR-YEAR DEAL\b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 FLOODING, STORMS, WEATHER, RESCUE, TRAVEL, TRACK\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY TRAVEL, STORMS, DELAYS, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JULY 4TH TRAVEL CRUSH GETS UNDERWAY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, SPENDING BILL, THUNE, COLLINS, SCHUMER, BACON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO PASS TRUMP'S MASSIVE TAX & SPENDING BILL\par \par \uc2 KOHBERGER, PLEA DEAL, RESTITUTION, GONCALVES, FRY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECT IN IDAHO COLLEGE MURDERS AGREES TO PLEAD GUILTY\par \par \uc2 AMBUSH, BRUSHFIRE, ROLEY, GREIF, NORRIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE IDENTIFY SUSPECT IN DEADLY IDAHO AMBUSH\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, UNION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS OF PHILADELPHIA CITY WORKERS GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have some breaking news now out of Philadelphia. The city's largest workers union officially on strike. Rhiannon Ally has the details, what it means for the city. Good morning, Rhiannon.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, George. This could be a smelly situation for the city of Philadelphia because, until a deal is made, this strike will impact several services around the city, including trash pickup.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) The city offered a deal last night, but the largest union representing the 9,000 city workers did not accept it. So, the workers want an 8% increase for each year of the four-year deal. The city offered 7% over three years, which averages out to just over 2% per year. So, these two sides are pretty far apart in what they want. Services provided by the union include everything from 911 dispatch to street repairs and that trash pickup I mentioned, and more than that. In the meantime, the city has temporarily added drop-off locations around the city where the residents can bring their trash. And the mayor said they will continue working on a compromise. But the good news is July 4th festivities will continue on as planned, guys.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rhiannon, our thanks to you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250801el710002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WHERE ARE THE JOBS? TOUGH JOB MARKET FOR RECENT COLLEGE GRADS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,097 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TWISTER TERROR\par \par \uc2 TORNADOS, FLORIDA, MINNESOTA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , IRAN, NUCLEAR, STRIKE, BLUMENTHAL\par \par \uc2 MAMDANI, NYC, MAYOR, CUOMO, ADAMS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN AIRLINES, FIRE, EMERICH, VILLASISTA, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 FORTNITE, \uc2 FTC\uc2 , LAWSUIT, REFUND, PLAYERS\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , DRAFT, FLAGG, MAVERICKS, KNUEPPEL, HARPER, CASTLE, WEMBANYAMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COOLER END TO THE WEEK\par \par \uc2 MUSIC VIDEO CLIP FROM WHITNEY HOUSTON/I WANNA DANCE WITH SOMEBODY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 COMBS, TRIAL, ARGUMENTS, JURY\par \par \uc2 STUDY, WILDFIRE, SMOKE, TOXIC, DNA, NADEAU, FIREFIGHTERS, SCOTT, TOXINS\par \par \uc2 BEACH, UMBRELLA, LIFEGUARD, BONGIOVANNI, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 BEZOS, SANCHEZ, VENICE, WEDDING, TRIGGS, PROTESTERS, JOHN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEZOS & SANCHEZ ARRIVE IN VENICE AHEAD OF LAVISH WEDDING PARTY\par \par \uc2 BERMUDEZ, GRADUATION, JOB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORNADOES & EXTREME HEAT\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS, HEAT WAVE, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE STORMS & EXTREME HEAT\par \par \uc2 VACCINE, CDC PANEL, RECOMMENDATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE PANEL MEETING\par \par \uc2 HEART DISEASE, TOOLS, ARRYTHMIA, BLOOD PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW HEART DISEASE STUDY\par \par \uc2 JOB, UNEMPLOYMENT, COLLEGE GRADUATES, AI\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back with our "GMA Cover Story" and a difficult job market for recent college graduates.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHERE ARE THE JOBS?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to the latest unemployment report, many are having no luck landing that first job. Chief economics correspondent Rebecca Jarvis is here with what's going on. And so, Rebecca, why are we seeing this?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, well, Robin, it's a really competitive jobs market right now. And I think a lot of us can relate to this. At some point, we all left school and we all had to find a job. I was leaving college at a time where we had come out of a really strong jobs market, and there were fewer jobs. And that's very similar to what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOUGH JOB MARKET FOR RECENT COLLEGE GRADS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) More people are graduating with college degrees and so that's more competitive if you have a college degree. And that's why we see the unemployment rate higher for graduates at 6.1% versus the national average at 4.2%. Fewer people are also leaving their jobs. Because if you leave a job, you got to get a new one.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And because it's more competitive to get a new one, people are less inclined to do that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how does AI play a part in all of this?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE JOB SEARCH & ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It certainly is playing a role in terms of both how companies are hiring, how companies are thinking about new employees.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AI IMPACT ON JOB MARKET\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Because when they look at those entry-level employees and they think about AI and the future, they are seeing tasks they might be giving over to the machines. They might be handing these over to the machines at some point. So, they need fewer entry level employees, which makes it more difficult for the employees.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And some of the research that's out there suggests that 14% of the jobs right now that exist aren't going to exist in the future, but there will be new jobs. I know it can sound scary, but we do want to talk about ways that people can help themselves as well.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And exactly, you mentioned how competitive it is. So, what can people actually do to stand out?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW RECENT COLLEGE GRADS STAND OUT WHEN SEARCHING FOR JOBS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, obviously, there is learning the new technology. We talked a lot about this here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIPS FOR STANDING OUT\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It can be intimidating but getting into that AI, understanding how it works, how to make it work for you can be very useful. But you have to also tap into your network. The good old fashion who do you know? Who do you know that can help you? How do you put those skills forward?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Have an open mind about jobs. The job of your dreams might not be the job, I don't know any about you. But I left college, I did not have the job of my dreams. I had the job that paid the bills. And that was the important thing for me when I left college. So, it's really important to have an open mind. And then tap into the human characteristics, the things AI and technology cannot do. Write the thank you letters after your interview. Follow-up. Stay on top of those relationships.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good idea. So, let's say I'm a parent and I don't want my kids living on the couch. I feel it's part of my job to help them get a job. How can a parent help?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW PARENTS CAN HELP NEW COLLEGE GRADS GET HIRED\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Okay. So, first of all, you can do mock interviews. You can help them with their network. You can do all of that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW PARENTS CAN HELP\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But I think it starts earlier than when they're looking for a job.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Helping them learn how to hustle earlier in life. Not solving every single problem for them, so that when they do face that challenge coming out of college, they know how to accept the no's and keep rolling with the punches.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I like that.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, very much. We were all talking about our first jobs. We know what your first job was in \uc2 New York Giants\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's why I didn't join the conversation when y'all are talking.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you even fill out an application for that?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I sent resumes. I went to a combine. That's all I did.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know. All right.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Worked out for all of us.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, it did.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It did, it did.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250711el6q0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMAZON CEO ANNOUNCES AI WILL REPLACE SOME JOBS HOW YOU CAN PREPARE FOR AI IN THE WORKPLACE\b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW WARNING OF "ALL-OUT WAR"\par \par \uc2 MIDEAST CRISIS, STRIKES, ESCALATION, TRUMP, GOP, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, IRAN, STRIKES, MISSILES, US\par \par \uc2 MIDEAST, TRUMP, CRISIS, IRAN, ISRAEL NUCLEAR\par \par \uc2 STORMS, RAIN, FLOODING, RESCUE, WINDS\par \par \uc2 LANDER, ICE, ARRESTED, MIGRANT, PADILLA, DEPORTATIONS\par \par \uc2 RIP CURRENT, RESCUE, BOOGIE BOARD, ROMERO, FOSTER\par \par \uc2 OSCARS, HONORARY, THOMAS, ALLEN, PARTON, CRUISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT IN THE WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 READ, TRIAL, JURY, DELIBERATIONS, CANNONE, ESTANISLAO\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, AI, JASSY, EMPLOYEES, JOBS, WORKFORCE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Thank you, Dan, appreciate your insight, as always. We're gonna turn now to the CEO of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  telling the company's employees that some of their jobs will be replaced by AI and that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's work force is expected to shrink in the coming years as AI's role expands. Our chief business correspondent Rebecca Jarvis is here with the story. I don't know how good of news this is for the physical employees. Rebecca, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON CEO ANNOUNCES AI WILL REPLACE SOME JOBS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, I mean, good morning, Michael. Hey, guys. And this is big news. And it's unsettling news for a lot of people listening. In a message to employees, \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , writing that the tech giant will need fewer people doing some of the jobs that are being done today and more people doing other types of jobs, and that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  will reduce its total corporate work force in the next few years.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is already using generative AI to do everything from improving inventory placement to forecasting demand to cutting costs and delivery times. You experience some of this as a customer. But in the future, according to Jassy, those "AI agents will be teammates." The technology is now your teammate in the future.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well it sounds like this is a growing trend though.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is. And we've heard this now from Meta, we've heard it from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . Like I said, it is deeply unsettling as a human to hear it. But learning how these tools actually work and can help you in your work and life is a huge advantage right now. \uc2 Google\uc2  offers a free course called \uc2 Google\uc2  AI Essentials.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW YOU CAN PREPARE FOR AI IN THE WORKPLACE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can download an app like Chat GPT, ask it questions. Once you familiarize yourself, you can even use to its manage things like your calendar and summarize long documents. And, guys, this is something we're going to be talking about into the future. It's important to remember here that it's not necessarily a job replacement, but it makes jobs different.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Being at the higher end, doing the higher level thinking, being helpful and being human, this is key. When you are in a job, put forth the best human aspect of yourself and be kind to humans as well.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's true. All right, all good points there, Rebecca, thank you. Coming up, Lara has sad news about a dear friend of "GMA." Lara.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHEF ANNE BURRELL PASSES AWAY\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, we were all so stunned when we heard that Chef Anne Burrell had passed away. We'll let you know what we know about the 55 year old chef whose bright smile always brought joy when she visited us right here on "GMA." That's next.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250702el6i00034\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LOUVRE MUSEUM\uc2  SHUTS DOWN AS STAFF PROTESTS OVERCROWDING GROWING BACKLASH TO TOURIST INFLUX IN EUROPE\b0\par\par\uc2 765 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU, DEFRIN, TRUMP, IRAN, WAR, ISRAEL, GAZA\par \par \uc2 G7, TRUMP, ISRAEL, IRAN, WAR, NUCLEAR DEAL, FRANCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT LEAVES G7 EARLY AS ISRAEL-IRAN CONFLICTS ESCALATES\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, GAZA, WAR, UKRAINE, DRONE ATTACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIA UNLEASHES DEADLY NEW AIR ATTACK ON KYIV\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TORNADO, STORM, FLOODING, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORNADOES TEAR THROUGH THE MIDWEST\par \par \uc2 RAIN, FLOODING, RESCUES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY FLASH FLOODING DEVASTATES WEST VIRGINIA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP MOBILE, IVES, TRUMP, WARREN, ETHICS, CRYPTOCURRENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TRUMP FAMILY BUSINESSES RAISE ETHICAL QUESTIONS\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, PRIME DAY, JAMES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON EXPANDS PRIME DAY\par \par \uc2 NBA FINALS, ALEXANDER, SIAKAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THUNDER BEAT \uc2 PACERS\uc2  IN PIVOTAL GAME 5\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MALPRACTICE, MILGRAM, PLASENCIA, ESTRADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW BOMBSHELL IN MATTHEW PERRY CASE\par \par \uc2 LOUVRE MUSEUM\uc2 , PARIS, OVERCROWDING, HENDERSON, PROTEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 LOUVRE MUSEUM\uc2  SHUTS DOWN AS STAFF PROTESTS OVERCROWDING\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to turn now to the unexpected shutdown at the most visited museum in the world. Staff at the \uc2 Louvre in Paris\uc2  are protesting overcrowding and understaffing, forcing to remain closed for several hours on Monday. Tom Soufi Burridge is live in Paris. Good morning, Tom.\par \par \uc2 TOM SOUFI BURRIDGE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, George. Yeah, it really is the perfect time to visit Paris. You can feel the summer vibes now. But although it looks stunning behind me, all is not well inside that building, the world's most visited museum, \uc2 The Louvre\uc2 , as tourist numbers continue to soar. You can see the buses right across Europe.\par \par \uc2 TOM SOUFI BURRIDGE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, as millions flock to Europe for their summer getaways, locals are fed up and fighting back against mass tourism. \uc2 The Louvre\uc2  closing for hours Monday, leaving throngs of ticketholders standing in line outside as workers staged a mass walkout to protest overcrowding and understaffing. The largest art museum in the world is home to the Venus de Milo and the most famous painting in the world, "The Mona Lisa," which tours roughly 20,000 visitors a day. The museum welcoming 8.7 million tourists last year, but workers say they're not built for crowds that big.\par \par \uc2 CLINT HENDERSON (MANAGING DIRECTOR OF THE POINTS GUY)\par \par \uc2 If you've been there, it doesn't matter what time of the year you go, it's going to be crowded. But the summertime is absolutely crazy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING BACKLASH TO TOURIST INFLUX IN EUROPE\par \par \uc2 TOM SOUFI BURRIDGE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) All over Europe, frustrations about overcrowding and mass tourism reaching a fever pitch. On Sunday, thousands demonstrating in Lisbon. And in Venice, locals protecting the construction of a new hotel. And in Barcelona, protesters spraying tourists with water guns. Spain, with a population of 48 million, hosted a record 94 million international visitors last year. Many there say the growing market for short-term rentals has made city rents unaffordable for residents.\par \par \uc2 CLINT HENDERSON (MANAGING DIRECTOR OF THE POINTS GUY)\par \par \uc2 More and more housing stock in communities around the world is being given over to short-term renters who are coming in. It not only increases the tourism problem, so there's more people in these cities, but there's also less housing stock now for locals.\par \par \uc2 TOM SOUFI BURRIDGE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, guys, \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  says overtourism is overwhelmingly driven by hotels, which make up about 80% of guests' nights in Europe. France is planning to build a new entrance to \uc2 The Louvre\uc2  and a new system for viewing "The Mona Lisa." And, of course, tourism is a massive economic driver here. So, like the Mona Lisa herself, it's not clear whether people are smiling or not about so much tourism. Guys?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't know. I didn't know if we should laugh or not at that. But very well done. Agreed. All right, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:04, SEAN "DIDDY" COMBS TRIAL\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up, "Breaking at 8," why a second juror is in danger of being removed from the Sean "Diddy" Combs trial?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 7:42, LUCKY TO BE ALIVE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Sam is back with a critical on-field rescue. Hey, Sam.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Hey, Michael. So, this is a really scary thing. Imagine on the soccer field and a perfectly healthy coach collapses. But his community steps in and sprints in into action to save him. We're going to hear from him when we come right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250723el6h0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ECONOMY ADDS 139,00 JOBS IN MAY, UNEMPLOYMENT RATE 4.2% HIRING SLOWED IN MAY BUT REMAINS STURDY\b0\par\par\uc2 333 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MISSILE, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GARCIA, CHARGES, DEPORTATION\par \par \uc2 LOS ANGELES, ICE, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MUSK, DOGE, SUPREME COURT, SOCIAL SECURITY\par \par \uc2 ARRESTS, ALLEGED, HIGH SCHOOL, SHOOTING, PLOT\par \par \uc2 READ, TRIAL, WOLFE, ACCIDENT, RECONSTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 GAUFF, SABALENKA, TENNIS\par \par \uc2 PANTHERS, OILERS, HOCKEY\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP OF BASEBALL GAME\par \par \uc2 COMBS, TRIAL, TESTIMONY, JANE\par \par \uc2 JAIL, ESCAPEE, HUNT, ENDS\par \par \uc2 SEATTLE, BURGLARIES, RILEY\par \par \uc2 FATHER'S DAY, GIFT, IDEAS\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, ONE, DAY\par \par \uc2 WORLDPRIDE, PARADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE STORMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 40 MILLION UNDER WEATHER ALERTS\par \par \uc2 STORM, WINDS, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS, TORNADOES, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GARCIA, MOSHENBERG, BONDI, DEPORTATION, HUMAN SMUGGLING\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, RAIDS, IMMIGRATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: L.A. PROTESTS ERUPT AFTER ICE RAIDS ACROSS THE CITY\par \par \uc2 ATHLETES, ALLOWANCE, \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , SETTLEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COLLEGES ALLOWED TO PAY STUDENT ATHLETES DIRECTLY\par \par \uc2 EMPLOYMENT, ECONOMY, JOBS, STOCKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ECONOMY ADDS 139,00 JOBS IN MAY, UNEMPLOYMENT RATE 4.2%\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The latest numbers on the economy show the jobs market is slowing but remains sturdy.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The US adding 139,000 jobs in May, slightly higher than expectations. The unemployment rate remains 4.2%. Jobs were added in health care, leisure, and hospitality and social assistance. The news sending the Dow up more than 400 points.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 22 MINUTES, KING PRINCESS LIVE!\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Well, still coming up here on our "GMA Morning Menu," we are in the presence of royalty. King Princess is here. We're looking forward to talking with them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 32 MINUTES, HYDRATION HYPE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yeah, absolutely. Also, ahead here, we are breaking down wellness drinks and electrolyte supplements to see if they're worth the hype. What you need to know here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 10 MINUTES, SEALS & STEALS: CLEVER SOLUTIONS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Tory Johnson joins us with "Deals & Steals" on clever solutions, all things around the home. Back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250714el670006a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW TARIFF THREAT AIMED AT EUROPEAN UNION TRUMP: "I'M NOT LOOKING FOR A DEAL" IN EU TRADE WAR\b0\par\par\uc2 589 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ADAMS, WEATHER, TRAVEL, MEMORIAL DAY, BIRD STRIKE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, TRACK, FLOODING, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLASH FLOODING & SEVERE THUNDERSTORMS EXPECTED\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PARSONS, WEST POINT, GRADUATION, HARVARD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP DELIVERS COMMENCEMENT AT WEST POINT\par \par \uc2 TARIFFS, LUTNICK, \uc2 EU\uc2 , ECONOMY, APPLE, TRADE, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TARIFF THREAT AIMED AT EUROPEAN UNION\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) News on the economy tonight and growing pressure on retailers. President Trump's trade war taking yet another turn as he threatens to impose new tariffs on tech giant, \uc2 Apple\uc2 , and the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Some retailers like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and \uc2 Nike\uc2  are already planning to raise prices. Here's ABC's Alex Presha.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP: "I'M NOT LOOKING FOR A DEAL" IN EU TRADE WAR\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As millions of Americans are shopping for Memorial Day savings, there's new fallout tonight as President Trump reignites his trade war with Europe, threatening to send prices higher. As \uc2 EU\uc2 's trade commissioner urging that trade with the US must be guided by mutual respect not threats, and that it stands ready to defend its interests. President Trump announcing a 50% tariff on \uc2 European Union\uc2  products starting June 1st after he said talks between the US and the EU are going nowhere.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's time that we play the game the way I know how to play the game.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President also, for the first time, targeting an American company. Tech giant \uc2 Apple\uc2  could face a 25% tariff by the end of June if it doesn't move all of its iPhone production to the US. Tariffs on other tech companies, like \uc2 Samsung\uc2 , could soon follow. The President has downplayed fears that American companies will shoulder the burden of these tariffs.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 They're not hurting, they're helping because they're creating jobs in America. We're creating tremendous amounts of jobs in America like you've never seen.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick admitted this week that tariffs would be felt at home once they hit 15%.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD LUTNICK (SECRETARY OF COMMERCE)\par \par \uc2 The world has a 10% tariff now. At 10%, you're not going to see it. Above 15%, I agree, you're going to see it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR RETAILERS CONSIDER RAISING PRICES ON GOODS BY NEXT WEEK\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The mixed messaging sent markets sliding Friday as three of the country's biggest retailers say they're bracing for tough times. Earlier this month, retail giant \uc2 Walmart\uc2  announced they will raise prices due to higher costs from Trump's tariffs. The company also preparing to layoff 1,500 employees. Target's hoping it won't have to raise prices, but has already slashed its annual outlook due to the unexpected impact of tariffs and heightened uncertainty. And \uc2 Nike\uc2  is planning on raising prices for a number of its products starting next week. It does not blame tariffs, however, the majority of its footwear is sourced in China and Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  says it plans to keep its prices stable in spite of the tariffs. But on a holiday weekend historically marked by great sales, new data is showing that the tariffs are driving up furniture costs, rising seven times faster than overall prices last month. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alex Presha, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020250528el5o00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NJT\uc2  STRIKE ENTERS 2ND DAY AFFECTS 350,000 DAILY RIDERS\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, THREAT\par \par \uc2 NEW ORLEANS, JAIL, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 NJT\uc2 , STRIKE, TRANSIT\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're gonna turn now to the major transit strike in New Jersey and New York affecting hundreds of thousands of people. Shakira fans going to her concert overnight having to find another way to get there, and it is not over just yet.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NJT STRIKE ENTERS 2ND DAY, AFFECTS 350,000 DAILY RIDERS\par \par \uc2 NJ TRANSIT\uc2  (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Who are we?\par \par \uc2 NJ TRANSIT\uc2  (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Locomotive engineers.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the New Jersey transit strike now entering its second day.\par \par \uc2 PASSENGER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's just disorganized from top to bottom.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Leaving passengers frustrated as they navigated the evening commute trying to find other options.\par \par \uc2 PASSENGER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Basically pay for two ways now. I'm already paying them monthly, so now I'm double paying for my commute, plus parking.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Negotiators failing to hold talks Friday after both sides said that they were willing to come back to the table.\par \par \uc2 GOV PHIL MURPHY (DEM) (NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 Our doors are open. We are ready to restart negotiations literally this second, but we need the BLET to come back to the negotiating table in good faith.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The vice president of the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainman\uc2 , James Lewis, telling me how talks broke down late Thursday, hours before the strike was called.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You were in that negotiation meeting for 15 hours.\par \par \uc2 JAMES P. LOUIS (VICE PRESIDENT OF BLET)\par \par \uc2 Correct.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What happened?\par \par \uc2 JAMES P. LOUIS (VICE PRESIDENT OF BLET)\par \par \uc2 We gave them our last offer. We thought it was gonna be one that they, could be acceptable. After a few minutes, they came back in the room, thanked us for what we did, but said they, they're not talking anymore, and they got up and walked out.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It all comes down to pay. The union saying its members are the lowest paid of any major passenger railroad, despite operating the third largest train system in the country.\par \par \uc2 NJ TRANSIT\uc2  (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 What do we want?\par \par \uc2 NJ TRANSIT\uc2  (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Contracts.\par \par \uc2 NJ TRANSIT\uc2  (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 When do we want it?\par \par \uc2 NJ TRANSIT\uc2  (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Now.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On Friday, Penn station's platforms abandoned, and these trains that typically carry more than 350,000 commuters each day sitting empty in Hoboken. For now, passengers relying on New Jersey transit bus routes with additional capacity, preparing for increased traffic and commute times.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, just a mess, and Beyonce's concert is also at MetLife Stadium in New Jersey on Thursday. So, fans may also need to find another way to get there. The talks aren't expected get going again until tomorrow. We will see if they finally get to a decision.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So many people impacted by this and having to find alternative ways to get to work, to school.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Potential ripple effect too. Gio, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250627el5h0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRANSIT STRIKE ENTERS 2ND DAY\b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, THREAT\par \par \uc2 NEW ORLEANS, JAIL, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 NJT\uc2 , STRIKE, TRANSIT\par \par \uc2 COMBS, COURT, CASE, TESTIMONY, VENTURA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, DRONE, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , NUCLEAR, DEAL, IRAN\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO XIV\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , BASKETBALL\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP OF BASEBALL GAME\par \par \uc2 MEMORIAL DAY, TRAVEL, TIPS\par \par \uc2 FLORIDA, BEAR, ATTACK, 911\par \par \uc2 WNBA, BASKETBALL\par \par \uc2 DOG, ANGEL, MAX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TORNADO, STORM\par \par \uc2 ESCAPE, COMEY, NEW ORLEANS, JAIL\par \par \uc2 NEW JERSEY, TRANSIT, STRIKE, UNION\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just a wild story in so many ways. All right, Mireya, thank you so much. It is also day two now of the transit strike, affecting hundreds of thousands of people in New Jersey and New York.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NJT STRIKE ENTERS 2ND DAY, AFFECTS 350,000 DAILY RIDERS\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Talks breaking down between union leaders and New Jersey Transit Management on Thursday. Better pay for employees seems to be the issue here. The union saying its members are the lowest paid of any major passenger railroad, despite operating the third largest train system in the country. The last New Jersey transit strike, which was some 40 years ago, lasted about three weeks. Janai?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250627el5h0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW JERSEY TRANSIT TRAIN ENGINEERS GO ON STRIKE NEXT MEETING ISN'T UNTIL SUNDAY\b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORNADO OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADOES, DAMAGE, OUTAGES, FIREMEN\par \par \uc2 STORMS, WINDS, RAIN, ALERTS\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, TRANSIT, HAAS, ENGINEERS, COMMUTERS, KOLLURI\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, MIDEAST, UAE, DEALS, JET\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, BIRTHRIGHT, JUSTICES, SAUER\par \par \uc2 COMBS, TRIAL, VENTURA, TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 NBA PLAYOFFS, \uc2 NUGGETS\uc2 , THUNDER, \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , CELTICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BACK TO THE FUTURE"\par \par \uc2 BELICHICK, "THE ART OF WINNING", FOOTBALL, PATRIOTS, UNC, HUDSON\par \par \uc2 PGA CHAMPIONSHIP, THOMAS, SHOTS, ACE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 TRANSIT, ENGINEERS, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the transit strike overnight that has paralyzed one of the largest train systems in the nation, impacting about 350,000 commuters. Let's go back to transportation correspondent Gio Benitez in Newark, New Jersey. Good morning again, Gio.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW JERSEY TRANSIT TRAIN ENGINEERS GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Michael. Good morning again. Yeah. As you just said, this is a train system that hundreds of thousands of people use. And now this morning it's at a standstill.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, no deal. New Jersey train engineers on strike. 450 union members walking off the job at midnight, disrupting the third largest train system in the nation, and the lives of more than 350,000 commuters.\par \par \uc2 ADAM ISMAIL (NEW JERSEY COMMUTER)\par \par \uc2 It's a mess, man. Hopefully it ends soon and we can move forward from this.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) I spoke with the VP of the union just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You were in that negotiation meeting for 15 hours.\par \par \uc2 JAMES LOUIS (VICE PRESIDENT OF BLET)\par \par \uc2 Correct.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What happened?\par \par \uc2 JAMES LOUIS (VICE PRESIDENT OF BLET)\par \par \uc2 We thought we were real, very close to a deal. We were hoping to get this done. We knew they were going to try to do a press conference. It seemed like everybody was pushing. We gave them our last offer. We thought it was going to be one that they could be acceptable. After a few minutes, they came back in the room, thanked us for what we did, but said they, they're not talking anymore, and they got up and walked out.\par \par \uc2 ENGINEERS ON STRIKE (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 It's time to pay the train crew.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It all comes down to pay. The union saying their members are the lowest paid of any major passenger railroad in the country. The strike suspending all New Jersey transit rail service.\par \par \uc2 KRIS KOLLURI (PRESIDENT & CEO NEW JERSEY TRANSIT)\par \par \uc2 We're going to ask for your patience, and if you're able to work from home, we really would ask you to do that.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what happens next? Well, we're told the next meeting doesn't happen until Sunday, so commuters won't be able to use this railroad for days, perhaps even longer. Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, a lot of chaos in the meantime, Gio. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250612el5g00038\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW JERSEY TRANSIT TRAIN ENGINEERS GO ON STRIKE TRAIN ENGINEERS STRIKE IMPACTING ABOUT 350,000 COMMUTERS AROUND NYC\b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORNADO OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADOES, DAMAGE, OUTAGES, FIREMEN\par \par \uc2 STORMS, WINDS, RAIN, ALERTS\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, TRANSIT, HAAS, ENGINEERS, COMMUTERS, KOLLURI\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Okay, Ginger, thanks. We're gonna turn now to the transit strike overnight that has paralyzed one of the largest train systems in the nation, impacting about 350,000 commuters. Transportation correspondent Gio Benitez is in Newark, New Jersey. Good morning, Gio.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW JERSEY TRANSIT TRAIN ENGINEERS GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, George, good morning. Yeah, these engineers have been out here picketing since Midnight. Now this is a major issue because we're talking about this huge train system in and out of New York that now has been brought to a standstill.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning no deal. New Jersey train engineers on strike, 450 union members walking off the job at Midnight. "GMA" talking to the chairman of their union overnight.\par \par \uc2 TOM HAAS (GENERA CHRM BROTHERHOOD LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 I certainly think it could have been avoided had \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2 , you know, put on the table a fair and reasonable wage for their locomotive engineers.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Disrupting the third largest train system in the nation and the lives of more than 350,000 commuters.\par \par \uc2 ADAM ISMAIL (NEW JERSEY COMMUTER)\par \par \uc2 It's a mess. But hopefully it ends soon and we can move forward from this.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It all comes down to pay. The union saying their members are the lowest paid of any major passenger rail road in the country.\par \par \uc2 TOM HAAS (GENERA CHRM BROTHERHOOD LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 Jersey Transit engineers are upwards of 20% behind the locomotive engineers at other rail roads.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRAIN ENGINEERS STRIKE IMPACTING ABOUT 350,000 COMMUTERS AROUND NYC\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The strike suspending all \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  rail service. The Transit Authority adding limited capacity to existing commuter bus routes into New York that are close to rail stations. But they estimate only 20% of rail customers can be accommodated on buses, making bad traffic even worse.\par \par \uc2 KRIS KOLLURI (PRESIDENT & CEO NEW JERSEY TRANSIT)\par \par \uc2 We're going to ask for your patience. And if you are able to work from home, we really would ask you to do that.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But after Thursday's 15 hours of negotiations between \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  and union members from the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , there's still work to be done.\par \par \uc2 TOM HAAS (GENERA CHRM BROTHERHOOD LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS & TRAINMEN)\par \par \uc2 Enough is enough. And \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  needs to come back to the table, offer the locomotive engineers a fair and reasonable wage that addresses the wage gap.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So we told you here that the big issue is about pay. The union says that the annual average salary is $115,000 a year. They want that brought up to $170,000 a year. You're hearing all of the support here, these are buses going by, honking their horns here for these engineers. But the state says that if they do that for this union, they're gonna have to do that for other unions. And that, they say, would bankrupt \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2 . George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250612el5g0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CRIPPLING NORTHEAST TRANSIT STRIKE 350K COMMUTERS AFFECTED NJ TRANSIT ENGINEERS PICKET\b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STORM, TORNADOES, EVACUATIONS, WOOD\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS, TRACK\par \par \uc2 JAIL BREAK, MANHUNT, HUTSON, MALLETT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 10 INMATES ESCAPE IN BRAZEN JAILBREAK\par \par \uc2 NJ TRANSIT\uc2 , STRIKE, MURPHY, LOUIS, KOLLURI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRIPPLING NORTHEAST TRANSIT STRIKE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Here in the northeast, the crippling transit strike affecting hundreds of thousands of commuters in and out of New York City. Hundreds of engineers at the \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  System walking off the job, bringing all trains to a stop. Here's ABC's transportation correspondent Gio Benitez.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 350K COMMUTERS AFFECTED NJ TRANSIT ENGINEERS PICKET\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, hundreds of thousands of commuters now left scrambling.\par \par \uc2 PASSENGER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You know, it was like a total disaster. I have to connect to three different busses to pick up my nephew by 2:30.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Roughly 450 engineers walking off the job after midnight this morning at \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2 , the third largest transit system in the nation.\par \par \uc2 PICKETER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 What do we want?\par \par \uc2 PICKETERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 PICKETER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 When do we want it?\par \par \uc2 PICKETERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Now.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The walkout, the state's first transit strike in more than 40 years, ending \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  Train Service in New Jersey, New York, and Philadelphia. Some Metro-North Trains in Orange and Rockland Counties also impacted.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR PHIL MURPHY (DEM) (NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 It is a slap in the face of every commuter and worker who relies on \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And traffic is a real concern, especially for big events. We're here at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . You have that playoff game tonight. And over at \uc2 MetLife\uc2  Stadium, you have Shakira tonight. Fans may have to find a new way to travel, maybe even for Beyonce next week.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Train stations usually bustling with riders empty. At the center of the labor dispute, wages.\par \par \uc2 JAMES LOUIS (\uc2 BROTHERHOOD OF LOCOMOTIVE ENGINEERS AND TRAINMEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 They are close to 20% behind their peers off the other railroads.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The union says engineers earn an average of $113,000 a year. They want that to increase to 170,000. But \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  says their demands would ruin its budget and force it to put those costs back on consumers.\par \par \uc2 KRIS KOLLURI (CEO GATEWAY DEVELOPMENT COMMISSION)\par \par \uc2 It has to be fair to them, but it has to be fiscally responsible.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Whit, tonight, both sides say they are ready to keep negotiating. But the next scheduled meeting isn't until Sunday, Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gio Benitez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250521el5g00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MAJOR TRANSIT STRIKE LOOMING NJ TRANSIT UNION DEMANDS SALARY INCREASE\b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY FLOODING\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, FLOODING, EAST, NASH, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MIDEAST, MILITARY, QATAR, JET\par \par \uc2 HEALTH, KENNEDY, VACCINES, MURPHY, CASSIDY, CUTS\par \par \uc2 SAID, ISIS, PLOT, ARMY, \uc2 FBI\par \par \uc2 NEW JERSEY, TRANSIT, STRIKE, UNION, SALARY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So alarming. All right. Pierre, thank you. Now a looming transportation strike that would leave much of the nation's third largest transit system at a standstill. \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  engineers are threatening to walk off the job. Transportation Correspondent Gio Benitez is in Newark, New Jersey, with more. Good morning to you, Gio.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR TRANSIT STRIKE LOOMING\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Robin, good morning. Yeah, as you said, time is really running out for \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  here because starting tonight at midnight, workers could walk out, and that would halt all commuter trains. Of course, we're talking about New York City, so these are trains into New York City. Now, this is a very big deal because, as you said, Robin, this is the third largest transit system in the nation. We're talking about 350 thousand commuters, a million weekday trips. The state is already making plans to add more buses.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 NJ TRANSIT\uc2  POSSIBLE STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NJ TRANSIT UNION STRIKE DEMANDS\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as you can imagine, this fight has a lot to do with pay. The Union says that its members earn an average salary, about a hundred thirteen thousand dollars a year. They want an average yearly salary of a hundred seventy thousand. \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  says the average salary is actually a hundred thirty-five thousand a year with highest earners already making over 200 thousand dollars. But if this walkout happens, this would actually be the first transit strike in the state in over 40 years. So we're talking about since the 1980s. The good news though, the potential good news is that our station here in New York, WABC, is reporting that these negotiations could resume later today, Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250612el5f00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 IS WORK-LIFE BALANCE IMPOSSIBLE YOUNG PEOPLE ARE LOOKING FOR MORE PERSONAL TIME\b0\par\par\uc2 821 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLACK SMOKE AT THE CONCLAVE\par \par \uc2 POPE, CARDINALS, CONCLAVE, SISTINE, CHAPEL, SMOKE, DOLAN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TRADE, DEAL, UK, STARMER, POWELL, CHINA, BESSENT, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS, BEATING, PD, ACQUITTED, BEAN, HALEY, SMITH, MULROY\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS, COMMENTS, SCOTUS, IMPEACHMENT, TRUMP, CITIZENSHIP\par \par \uc2 UNITED, NEWARK, KIRBY, \uc2 FAA\uc2 , \uc2 FTC\uc2 , OUTAGE, ID\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS, CELTICS, \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , BRIDGES, BRUNSON, THUNDER, \uc2 NUGGETS\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM HARTFORD, CONNECTICUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOOD ALERTS\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "ABBOTT ELEMENTARY"\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, MURDERS, KOHBERGER, TRIAL, TESTIMONY, AMAZON\par \par \uc2 ANISTON, STALKER, CARWYLE, GATE, CRASHER, COURT\par \par \uc2 YELLOWSTONE, WILDLIFE, GORED, TOURIST, BISON, MAGILL, CLARK\par \par \uc2 CONSUMER, CAR, PRICES, TARIFFS, NISSAN, ADVERTISEMENT, TIPS\par \par \uc2 'JEOPARDY!", ANSWER, ISAAC\par \par \uc2 MUSIC CLIP FROM "JEOPARDY!" THEME\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM TULARE COUNTY, CALIFORNIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEST WARMS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONCLAVE DAY 2 UNDERWAY\par \par \uc2 CONCLAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, TRUMP, COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY\par \par \uc2 MARKWOOD, ACCIDENT, FALL, MLB\par \par \uc2 EMPLOYERS, WORK-LIFE BALANCE, GREDE, SCHAWBEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back with our "GMA Cover Story" and questions about work-life balance after the CEO of the popular brand Skims said, it's your problem and not your employer's. Rhiannon Ally is back with that story and the reaction. Good morning again, Rhiannon.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IS WORK-LIFE BALANCE IMPOSSIBLE\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you Robin. Many of us want to spend more time with our family and friends, but we also want to continue growing at work. Well, one CEO says, there's one place you should never talk about work-life balance, and her comments are getting some pretty strong reactions.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, new comments on work-life balance from the founding partner of Skims and CEO of Good American, \uc2 Emma Grede\uc2  is facing scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM PODCAST, "A DIARY OF A CEO WITH \uc2 STEVEN BARTLETT\uc2 "\par \par \uc2 EMMA GREDE (FOUNDER OF SKIMS AND CEO OF GOOD AMERICAN)\par \par \uc2 Work-life balance is your problem. Like, that's yours to figure out.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Grede appearing on "The Diary of a CEO" podcast Monday, telling the coast \uc2 Steven Bartlett\uc2  she considers this a red flag in a job interview.\par \par \uc2 EMMA GREDE (FOUNDER OF SKIMS AND CEO OF GOOD AMERICAN)\par \par \uc2 So, can you talk to me about work-life balance in this organization? Sorry, I'm leaving. So, when somebody talks to me about their work-life balance in an interview process, I'm like, something is wrong with you.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The 42-year-old mother of four described the expectations she has for her employees.\par \par \uc2 EMMA GREDE (FOUNDER OF SKIMS AND CEO OF GOOD AMERICAN)\par \par \uc2 I don't think the expectation is that anyone is going to have to work seven days a week in order to get, you know, to have an average job. If you want to be one of those people that's like, you know, at the top of the organization, the chances are, you might have to work a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The juggling work ambitions and personal life isn't easy, especially for working moms. According to a \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  survey, nearly 60% of millennial mothers say being a working mom makes it harder for them to get ahead at work, compared to about 20% of working millennial fathers. Grede's remarks sparking backlash with comments like, "A CEO saying stuff like that is a red flag to not buy their items." And "I can't support this type of leadership. It's so outdated." Considering the extremely competitive job market, some experts say it is best to avoid this topic in an interview.\par \par \uc2 DAN SCHAWBEL (MANAGING PARTNER WORKPLACE INTELLIGENCE)\par \par \uc2 Employers have the power, so you need to make sure that you're focused on their needs and less of your own needs. So, once you start talking about work-life balance, then you're putting the onus on them when you need to present yourself as the best candidate as possible trying to solve their problems.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, our expert did say that young people specially are looking for more personal time. That's one of the reasons Grede's comments are so controversial. But he does say too much work can lead to burnout. And that can eventually make workers less productive and want to quit. How are you guys feeling over there?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's how it was presented. You know, how the gentleman said it at the end was the same way how she said it, but how he said it was just more of a way that you can accept it and understand it.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm, yeah.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Understood.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Rhiannon.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250604el580000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UPS TO LAY OFF 20,000 WORKERS UPS CLOSING A NUMBER OF FACILITIES\b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP ONE ON ONE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, 100 DAYS, TARIFFS, CHINA, GARCIA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TARIFFS, AUTOWORKERS, ROLLBACK\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, REPORT, SLOWDOWN, TARIFFS, PORTS\par \par \uc2 STORMS, RAIN, HAIL WINDS, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, TIMES SQUARE, SUSPECT, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, STRIKES, DRONES, INJURIES\par \par \uc2 FAA\uc2 , TRAVEL, AIRLINES, AIRSPACE, ROUTES\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS, \uc2 PACERS\uc2 , BUCKS, \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , \uc2 PISTONS\uc2 , CELTICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOOD EVENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE GOLDEN GIRLS"\par \par \uc2 BELICHICK, INTERVIEW, HUDSON, EMAIL, BOOK, RELATIONSHIP\par \par \uc2 READ, RETRIAL, MURDER, MCCABE, O'KEEFE, TESTIMONY, ROBERTS\par \par \uc2 RUNAWAY, BOAT, SPEED, RESCUE, KING, DIXON\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA ZOO, TORTOISES, NAMES, HATCHLINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 UPS, LAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now we're gonna turn to UPS announcing layoffs for 20,000 workers and the closing of some facilities. Rebecca Jarvis is back with the big picture. Good morning again, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, nice to see you, Michael. And these are big job cuts coming to UPS. The company announcing that they will lay off those 20,000 workers and close a number of facilities as it dramatically reduces shipments of Amazon packages, something that's been in the works for quite some time. Like so many companies recently though, UPS also saying it won't be able to forecast future guidance given the current macroeconomic uncertainty with tariffs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UPS TO LAY OFF 20,000 WORKERS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Railroad companies, trucking, airline, and shipping companies have all felt significant slowdowns recently with US companies effectively sitting on their hands, waiting to see how long and how impactful President Trump's tariffs will be on their businesses. Shippers experienced this surge in traffic early in the year as US businesses raced to get ahead of the tariffs and stockpile merchandise and supplies. And now we have this ongoing uncertainty. The more of it that we see, the longer it lasts, the more concerned economists are that it will lead to further job cuts at all sorts of companies, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, and we're gonna have more from you in just a little bit.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Rebecca. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250509el4u0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STOCKS DIVE AS TARIFF WAR ESCALATES DOW PLUNGES NEARLY 1,700 POINTS IN WORST DAY SINCE 2020\b0\par\par\uc2 850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TARIFFS, STOCKS, ECONOMY, ZANDI, WOODS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. And it's great to have you with us here on a Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We begin tonight with the US stock markets in a free fall after President Trump's announcement of sweeping tariffs. The worst single day losses in years since the beginning of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STOCKS DIVE AS TARIFF WAR ESCALATES\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Americans and their 401 (k) s taking immediate hits. And families now bracing for higher prices, from groceries, to cars, to appliances.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOW JONES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 40,545.93, DOWN, 1,679.39, 3.98%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: S&P 500\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 5,396.52, DOWN, 274.45, 4.84%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NASDAQ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 16,550.61, DOWN, 1,050.44, 5.97%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Dow falling nearly 1,700 points today alone. Nearly 4%. The S&P 500 down nearly 5%. And the tech-heavy NASDAQ index falling nearly 6%. Their worst day in five years. President Trump announcing a base line 10% tariff on goods coming into the US, even steeper tarries for many countries, including China, the EU, Japan, and South Korea. Late today, President Trump saying, quote, "I think it's going very well." In addition, overnight, a 25% tariff on all imported cars and auto parts kicked in. And tonight, the third largest US automaker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the maker of Chrysler, Dodge, and \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , now laying off 900 workers in Indiana and Michigan. \uc2 Nike\uc2  saw its stock fall more than 14% today. \uc2 Apple\uc2  stock falling more than 9%. And analysts say bringing back production to the US, even if just 10% of iPhones were made here, it would more than triple the cost of an iPhone.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, how will this all work? How long will this pain last? And what President Trump said today about the economic pain already. ABC's chief business and economics correspondent Rebecca Jarvis leading us off tonight from Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOW PLUNGES NEARLY 1,700 POINTS IN WORST DAY SINCE 2020\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, US Stocks in free fall. President Trump's sweeping new tariffs sending stocks plummeting. Leading to the market's worst day since the start of the pandemic five years ago. The Dow plunging nearly 1,700 points, or 4%. The S&P 500 down more than 4.8%. The tech-heavy NASDAQ down nearly 6%. Among the hardest hit, car makers, including US companies Ford, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , and \uc2 General Motors\uc2 . And already tonight, the President's sweeping tariffs taking a toll on American jobs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 STELLANTIS\uc2  TEMPORARILY LAYS OFF 900 AUTO WORKERS IN U.S.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The third-largest automaker in the US, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which makes \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , Chrysler, and RAM, temporarily laying off 900 workers in Indiana and Michigan, as it pauses production in factories in Canada and Mexico after the President's new 25% tariffs on foreign-made cars. And for Americans watching their 401 (k) s shrink and bracing now for higher prices from groceries to cars, economists across the board arguing this is not a surprise with tariffs so steep.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZANDI\uc2  (CHIEF ECONOMIST MOODY'S ANALYTICS)\par \par \uc2 Tariffs are a tax on imports, and that tax has to be borne by somebody. And history suggests it's going to be the American consumer. Everything from a banana to your shoes to consumer electronics, you're going to be paying more for them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 NIKE\uc2 , APPLE AMONG U.S. COMPANIES TAKING LOSSES\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Other American companies seeing their stocks plunge, too. \uc2 Nike\uc2 , which produces about half its shoes at factories in Vietnam, down 14.4%. \uc2 Apple\uc2 , which designs its phones in California, but relies on manufacturing in China, India, Vietnam, and Malaysia, down 9.2%. And if \uc2 Apple\uc2  made just 10% of its phones back here in the US, analysts estimate the cost of an iPhone would surge from $1,000 to $3,500, because of the cost of labor here. Home building and furniture hurt, too. \uc2 Restoration Hardware\uc2  stock sinking 40% as the President announced his tariffs. The CEO reacting in real-time. And tonight, on Wall Street, investors telling me the pain is far from over.\par \par \uc2 JAY WOODS (CHIEF GLOBAL STRATEGIST FREEDOM CAPITAL MARKETS)\par \par \uc2 Living through a day like today, not knowing what the next few days are going to bring, or the next quarter, that's where it gets a little troubling.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, David, we are just in day one, just 24 hours since the President's announcement. It's still unclear how company, countries will respond. How they'll react, whether they'll negotiate or retaliate. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  tonight saying that if these tariffs are sustained, it will likely plunge the US and the world into a recession. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rebecca Jarvis leading us off tonight. Rebecca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250407el430002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "JOB GHOSTING" ON THE RISE WHAT YOU CAN DO TO HELP PREVENT "JOB GHOSTING"\b0\par\par\uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE CROSS-COUNTRY STORM\par \par \uc2 CROSS, COUNTRY, STORM, MUDSLIDES, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, SCHUMER, VAN HOLLEN, RICKETTS, TRUMP, TARIFFS, LEBLANC, CANADA, \uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 WITKOFF, PUTIN, UKRAINE, CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 ARSON, \uc2 BAYER\uc2 , THOMPSON, MURDER, HEALTH, CARE LUIGI\par \par \uc2 INFLATION, REPORT, PRICES, CONSUMER, EGGS\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, TECH, MANHOLE, FIRE, POWER, OUTAGES, SPRING, BREAK\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2 , \uc2 SPACEX\uc2 , CREW, WILLIAMS, WILMORE, BLOOD, MOON, LUNAR, ECLIPSE, TIMELINE\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM TUCKERMAN'S RAVINE, NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER MAP\par \par \uc2 MOVIE CLIP FROM "COACH CARTER"\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, BEACH, KONANKI, RIIBE, SCHRIEVER, DUSEK\par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIA, JONES, INSTAGRAM, WOMBAT, MAGILL, WILDLIFE, SELFIES, BEAR, BISON\par \par \uc2 TOLL, SCAM, TEXT, WARNING, SMISHING, TIPS, TIKTOK\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM SOCIAL MEDIA POSTS\par \par \uc2 SHARON, GIGI, FALL, TODDLER, HERO, PHONE\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM MENLO PARK, CALIFORNIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRONG WIND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE CROSS-COUNTRY STORM\par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, TORNADOES, TRACK\par \par \uc2 KOHBERGER, EVIDENCE, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 RINGWALD, HUGHES, MANN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 JOB HUNTING, JOB GHOSTING, FISHER, PILLAI\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to turn now to one of the frustrations of job hunting, ghosting.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "JOB GHOSTING" ON THE RISE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It doesn't just happen on dating apps. It's also happening to job seeker. Rebecca Jarvis back with that story. Hey, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh. Hi, George. Yeah. If this sounds familiar, you're interviewing for a new job. Things seem to be going well, and then suddenly nothing. You're not hearing back from anyone. You can't get information about what's going on. It is happening more and more. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Twenty-five-year-old graphic designer Cijay Pillai was awaiting feedback after submitting a design assessment in the second round of an interview process when the communication went silent, prompting him to follow up.\par \par \uc2 CIJAY PILLAI (GRAPHIC DESIGNER)\par \par \uc2 I said, hi, I just wanted to follow up where we are at in the interview process. Because I had already gotten a job offer, so I wanted to wait a little bit and see where this one went.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And even after another follow-up, still nothing. It's called job ghosting, an issue job seekers are increasingly facing. 40% having experienced it during the hiring process.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (CAREER EXPERT LINKEDIN)\par \par \uc2 It's really frustrating for job seekers. And there are some things you can do to prepare for it and other things you can do to try to make sure that it doesn't happen to you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIP #1, SET CLEAR EXPECTATIONS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) First, set clear expectations during the interview.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT YOU CAN DO TO HELP PREVENT "JOB GHOSTING"\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (CAREER EXPERT LINKEDIN)\par \par \uc2 Just ask, hey, what are the next steps here? Will I be hearing back if you want to move forward or if you're not interested in moving forward, will I hear back as well?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIP #2, OFFER TO GIVE CLARIFICATION\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Then offer to give clarification.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (CAREER EXPERT LINKEDIN)\par \par \uc2 It is absolutely okay to ask things like, is there anything in my experience that you want me to explain more? Is there anything that lends you pause about what I said?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIP #3, SEND A THANK YOU NOTE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And finally, follow-up with a thank you note.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (CAREER EXPERT LINKEDIN)\par \par \uc2 It gives you another opportunity to explain why you're the best candidate for the role. You can follow up a couple of times. But after the third time, it's probably time just to sit back and then start, you know, focusing on other roles.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Which is what Cijay did to land a role at a different agency, and he continues to grow.\par \par \uc2 CIJAY PILLAI (GRAPHIC DESIGNER)\par \par \uc2 I've been really putting a lot of work into my portfolio, into my networking skills. I do the extra work to really tailor everything that I'm doing to get that job.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, if you do get ghosted, you have to do everything in your power not to take this personally. It is a highly competitive jobs market. Hiring managers are spending three to five hours a day going over resumes. And people often ask me, what's your best advice to your younger self? Don't take this stuff personally. Just keep moving forward, even if you get a no. George?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Rebecca, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250411el3d0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FEDERAL WORKERS BRACE FOR MORE LAYOFFS AFTER SECOND DOGE EMAIL SOME FIRED FEDERAL WORKERS WEIGHING NEW JOB OPTIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW ROUND OF FIRINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SLASHING JOBS\par \par \uc2 ACADEMY AWARDS, BEARDAN, O'BRIEN\par \par \uc2 MUSK, TRUMP, LAYOFFS, DIXON, LECKIE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL WORKERS BRACE FOR MORE LAYOFFS AFTER SECOND DOGE EMAIL\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are going to turn now to those mass federal firings and rising levels of uncertainty for government workers. Billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  doubling down on his DOGE drive to slash federal spending. ABC's Selina Wang is in Washington with the details now and what's ahead this week. Good morning, Selina.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Gio. Yeah, federal workers are getting even more anxious and confused. Thousands have already been laid off, and those who still have their jobs are now going to get another email asking them what they did at work last week. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says, if they don't respond, they'll be fired.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, federal workers bracing for more layoffs as they receive a second wave of emails demanding they list five tasks they completed at work last week by Monday night. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  saying, those who fail to respond will be fired. Writing on X, "The President has made it clear that this is mandatory for the executive branch." The email doesn't include Musk's threat, only adding to the confusion. It's the latest push for Musk's Department of Government Efficiency to dramatically slash the federal workforce. The Department of Education giving workers a Monday deadline to accept a payment of up to $25,000 to voluntarily resign or retire, according to a memo obtained by ABC News. And the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2 , which provides key benefits to older Americans, saying it plans to cut 7,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (HEAD OF DEPARTMENT OF GOVERNMENT EFFICIENCY)\par \par \uc2 Only the federal government can get away with this level of waste.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Secretary of Defense Hegseth telling workers to comply with Musk's order as the Pentagon braces for thousands of layoffs any day now. This 30-year veteran currently working for the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 , tells me he started the hunt for a new job.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME FIRED FEDERAL WORKERS WEIGHING NEW JOB OPTIONS\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM DIXON (US ARMY CORPS OF ENGINEERS WORKER)\par \par \uc2 We just a fear and put the sacrifice out. It's almost like it's nothing, like, we don't even matter. We don't count.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you worried that these changes could impact our country's military readiness?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM DIXON (US ARMY CORPS OF ENGINEERS WORKER)\par \par \uc2 Definitely.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This federal worker who says he was recently fired from the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2 , rebuking Musk.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LECKIE (FEDERAL WORKER)\par \par \uc2 Now just incredibly terrified and scrambling to find new jobs to keep the roof over our head and, you know, feed our little one.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A federal judge is ruling that these mass firings of probationary workers is illegal, but said he doesn't have the power to reinstate the workers or stop future firings. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Selina Wang, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250310el320002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DOGE ISSUES NEW WARNING FEDERAL WORKERS BRACE FOR "LARGE SCALE" LAYOFFS\b0\par\par\uc2 688 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, EMERGENCY, RESIDENTS, WINDS, SMOKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAROLINA WILDFIRE DOUBLES IN SIZE\par \par \uc2 STORM, WINDS, ALERTS, SNOW\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, UK, FRANCE, STARMER, PEACE, \uc2 NATO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UK & FRANCE DEVELOP UKRAINE PEACE DEAL\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, GAZA, AID, CEASEFIRE, HOSTAGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL HALTS ALL AID TO GAZA\par \par \uc2 DOGE, LAYOFFS, EMPLOYEES, MUSK, COHEN\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ines, thank you. And back here at home, more than two million federal workers have until tomorrow to reply to another email instructing them to list five things they accomplished in the last week. And tonight, we're learning the Department of Education is offering employees thousands of dollars to leave their jobs now ahead of layoffs. ABC News senior White House correspondent Selena Wang has the latest.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOGE ISSUES NEW ULTIMATUM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL WORKERS BRACE FOR "LARGE SCALE" LAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, another looming deadline for federal workers, as agencies prepare plans for large-scale layoffs. Billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  threatening to fire government employees who don't respond to a second email, demanding they justify their jobs by Monday night. Musk writing on X, "The President has made it clear that this is mandatory for the executive branch."\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The email from the Office of Personnel Management saying it's now a weekly requirement to list five tasks they completed in the last week. Secretary of Defense Hegseth telling civilian workers to comply.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (SECRETARY OF DEFENSE)\par \par \uc2 It's a simple task, really as Elon said, as the President recognized in our first cabinet meeting, just a pulse check, are you there?\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But other federal agencies getting different and conflicting guidance to their staffers about whether and how to respond, only adding to the chaos, confusion, and panic. Musk claiming he's just cutting waste.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (DOGE)\par \par \uc2 I thought it would be bad, but I did not think it would as bad as this. I mean, look, the good news is that it's a totally rich environment for certain money.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This veteran currently working the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  tells me, he's already on the hunt for a new job.\par \par \uc2 US VETERAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's almost like a slap in the face, like we don't even matter. We don't count.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you worried that all these changes could impact our country's military readiness?\par \par \uc2 US VETERAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Definitely.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOGE ISSUES NEW ULTIMATUM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL FIRINGS RAISE SECURITY CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And tonight, a real concern within the intelligence community, that foreign adversaries, including Russia and China, could target the growing ranks of recently fired federal employees for recruitment.\par \par \uc2 JOHN COHEN (FMR ACTING UNDERSECRETARY COUNTERTERRORISM COOR DHS)\par \par \uc2 It's not difficult for a foreign spy service to find a target for recruitment. They just simply have to go online and look at the postings of people who recently have been fired, who are angry, and who are in need of money.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And let's get right to Selena Wang, she joins us now live from the White House. Selina, it's another busy week ahead. The President will be addressing a joint session of Congress. What more can we expect?\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Rachel, something to watch for is how President Trump addresses the economy. He had campaigned on lowering prices for Americans, but right now grocery prices are high, and economists warn that Trump's tariff plan will raise costs for Americans. In fact, also on Tuesday, Trump's tariffs on imports from China, Canada and Mexico are excited to go into effect. Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Selina, thank you. And ABC News will have live special coverage of the President's joint address to Congress and the Democratic response. I'll be joining David, Linsey and the rest of our powerhouse political team live from Washington Tuesday night starting at 9:00 PM right here on ABC.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020250305el3200006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOVERNMENT JOB CUTS COULD IMPACT NATIONAL PARKS WHAT TO KNOW FOR UPCOMING TRAVEL\b0\par\par\uc2 772 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WHITE HOUSE, MEETING, TRUMP, ZELENSKYY, VANCE\par \par \uc2 WORLD, REACTION, OVAL OFFICE, TRUMP, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS, PNEUMONIA\par \par \uc2 HACKMAN, WIFE, DEATH\par \par \uc2 QUINTERO, CAMARENA\par \par \uc2 MEASLES, OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 OSCARS, NOMINEES\par \par \uc2 OHIO, STADIUM, NHL\par \par \uc2 OSCAR, PREDICTIONS\par \par \uc2 POLITICS, TRUMP, ZELENSKYY, CONGRESS, BUDGET, MEDICAID\par \par \uc2 MOON, LANDING, BLUE GHOST, FIREFLY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ZELENSKYY, MEETING, TENSION, DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXPLOSIVE WHITE HOUSE MEETING WITH ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 VATICAN, POPE, HEALTH, CONDITION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VATICAN: POPE RESTING WITH RESPIRATORY CONDITION\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE, DEATH, CEO, MIRANDA, CONSTITUTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUIGI MANGIONE DEFENSE SEEKS TO THROW OUT EVIDENCE\par \par \uc2 CDC, MEASLES, HEALTH, VACCINATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC: 164 MEASLES CASES ACROSS NINE STATES\par \par \uc2 DEATH, MEMORIAL, HONOR, POLICE, DUARTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC: 164 MEASLES CASES ACROSS NINE STATES\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL PARKS, CUTS, GOVERNMENT, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNMENT JOB CUTS COULD IMPACT NATIONAL PARKS\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are back with our "GMA" cover story, the growing concerns at America's national parks after federal job cuts and how they could impact upcoming vacations, especially during the busy spring break and summer seasons. Erielle Reshef has more.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, as millions gear up to spend spring break at some of the nation's most treasured parks, from Yosemite to the Grand Canyon, a warning from park officials that the deep government job cuts could have a noticeable impact on your experience.\par \par \uc2 BETH PRATT (\uc2 NATIONAL WILDLIFE FEDERATION\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 These parks are not going to be able to operate. The visitors are not going to be able to do the things they want. You know, if you want to bring your Yosemite and can't camp, or it takes four hours to get into the park, what kind of experience is that?\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nearly a thousand park employees were laid off on Valentine's Day, and some ripple effects are already being felt, from longer lines to cut hours to closed visitor centers and canceled tours.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR CARRASCO (VISITING FROM LOS ANGELES WITH HIS KIDS)\par \par \uc2 We had an experience for 10 options. We only did, like, four. You can still have a good time, especially that bridge, but other than that, you're cutting it short.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  says more than 325 million people visited these sites in 2023, up four percent from the year prior. But now, officials say the staffing shortages will make keeping the areas pristine and visits running smoothly more challenging.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGDALE MAYOR BABARA BRUNO (UTAH)\par \par \uc2 In my mind, it's a crisis. If it's the employees that are gone, I'm not sure how the park could stay open.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some travelers and local businesses are bracing for a spring shakeup.\par \par \uc2 LIZ WOOLSEY (BRISTLECONE GENERAL STORE & STARAZER INN)\par \par \uc2 Businesses that are here in Baker certainly rely on tourism and travels. So, we are unsure what that's going to look like this season.\par \par \uc2 BEN SHREVES (FORMER NATIONAL PARK RANGER)\par \par \uc2 I was on lunch on February 14 and got a call from my supervisor, and he said to be out and done by 5:00 pm, and so it was a mad dash.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ben Shreves is one of the park employees who recently lost his job.\par \par \uc2 BEN SHREVES (FORMER NATIONAL PARK RANGER)\par \par \uc2 These parks are already running on a bare bones crew. People are, again, it's a labor of love. People are working overtime to provide the best experience, and they're already struggling to do that. And part of that was a lack of resources, and so now, it is just, like, they have even less of that.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts say if you're planning to traverse the parks this spring to be patient and over-prepare.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BRENGEL (NATIONAL PARKS CONSERVATION ASSOCIATION)\par \par \uc2 There's going to be a big imbalance between the number of people who are going to the parks and the amount of staff that can help them. It's really important for park visitors to go prepared and to make sure that they are dressed properly, have enough food and water on them.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For "Good Morning America," Erielle Reshef, ABC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) All right, Erielle, thank you for that. And the Trump administration did backtrack on some of the firings at national parks. Park advocates say that it's definitely a win and a testament to the rangers and others who have been vocal about what it can mean for travelers.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, well, some good news there that we'll continue watching.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250404el310000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FEDERAL JUDGE RULES ON TRUMP ADMINISTRATION'S MASS LAYOFFS TRUMP DOUBLES DOWN ON TARIFFS\b0\par\par\uc2 843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GENE HACKMAN DEATH "SUSPICIOUS"\par \par \uc2 HACKMAN, ARAKAWA, DEATHS, SUSPICIOUS, MENDOZA, TRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 JUDGE, RULING, TRUMP, LAYOFFS, EMPLOYEES, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really a devastating loss and truly one of the greats. And he didn't even get started really until his late 30s. An incredible story there. All right, Trevor, thank you. We turn now to breaking news overnight. A federal judge reportedly says the Trump administration's mass firings likely break the law. Our senior political correspondent Rachel Scott is at the White House with what this could mean for the thousands of workers who have already been let go. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL JUDGE RULES ON TRUMP ADMINISTRATION'S MASS LAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rebecca, good morning to you. And there is no doubt about it this is a blow for the Trump administration, but it is not total relief for federal workers out there. News that this federal judge says that the firing of thousands of probationary workers was illegal, but only suggesting that it should be stopped.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, reports of a federal judge ruling the Trump administration's mass firings of probationary workers is illegal and should be stopped and rescinded. According to the "AP," the judge saying that the office of personnel management did not have any authority whatsoever to order the firings of probationary employees. But the judge acknowledging he could not stop agencies from independently firing workers in the future.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're cutting down the size of government. We have to. We're bloated. We're sloppy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And this was the scene at \uc2 USAID\uc2 , the humanitarian agency that has provided food and medicine to millions of children around the world. Workers who were fired returning to retrieve their belongings.\par \par \uc2 USAID\uc2  EMPLOYEE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's heart breaking to see this whole sector, this whole field kind of collapse. It's really heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The impacts of the cuts at the agency reportedly leading to the termination of projects, including HIV treatment, malnutrition, malaria control programs and polio vaccination. The Pentagon preparing for layoffs any day now. The Department of Veterans Affairs dismissing more than 1,400 probationary workers earlier this week. Veterans make up 30% of the federal work force.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, fired workers showing up at this town hall in Virginia telling their Democratic representatives they're concerned about the cuts. One veteran fired from his job at the Department of Agriculture, speaking with our Jay O'Brien.\par \par \uc2 LEO KUPPER (VETERAN FORMER DEPT OF AGRICULTURE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 Go back to the veterans of our country and tell them that they were not performing well in a job when we defensed our country for many, many years. Was directly, for lack of a better word, a slap in the face.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President was asked if he's tracking how many veterans have been fired so far.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're watching that very carefully and we hope it's going to be as small a number as possible, but we are having great success in slimming down our government.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The latest firings hitting \uc2 NOAA\uc2 , the nation's top weather and climate agency. Hundreds let go.\par \par \uc2 REP RO KHANNA (DEM) (CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 These are the scientists who help figure out where the hurricanes are coming, how severe they're going to be, whether we're going to have an earthquake, whether we're going to have a wild fire.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So the unions that were suing the Trump administration celebrating this move, saying that it is illegal full stop. But the judge here not really providing so much clarity on what this means for workers that have already been let go. Will they be rehired? And, again, only suggesting that these firings should stop not ordering it, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, and George has a question, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, the President saying that more tariffs are coming?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP DOUBLES DOWN ON TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, so you may remember the President threatened a 25% tariff on Mexico and Canada. The President announcing that those tariffs are now moving forward next week on March 4th. He's saying that those countries have not done enough to stop drugs flowing into the United States.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEXICO DRUG CARTEL OPERATIVES SENT TO US\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is notable, though, because Mexico has in fact extradited members of drug cartels trying to show this good faith effort to stop this from actually going into effect. The President also noting that there will be a 10% tariff also slapped on China. Economists say this could drive up the prices on everything, from cars to beer to avocados. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250320el2s000b7\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HOW GOVERNMENT JOB CUTS COULD IMPACT NATIONAL PARKS EXPERTS WARN VISITORS TO BE MORE PREPARED\b0\par\par\uc2 795 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOSE TO CATASTROPHE\par \par \uc2 PLANES, RUNWAY, PILOTS, LANDING, ABORT, PASSENGERS\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, BUDGET, CHAOS, MASSEY, DEMOCRATS, JEFFRIES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, CABINET, MUSK EMPLOYEES, BACKLASH, SMITH, GOLD CARD\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, US, MINERALS, DEAL, MEETING\par \par \uc2 TOURISTS, VACATION, DEAD, HOTEL, WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 EGGS, PRICES, BIRD FLU, \uc2 USDA\uc2 , BUSINESSES\par \par \uc2 DONCIC, LAKERS, MAVERICKS, TRADE, FACEOFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPRING LIKE TEMPERATURE'S\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 KOHBERGER, PROSECUTORS, MURDERS, EVIDENCE, DNA, ALIBI\par \par \uc2 CRUISE LINES, RULES, AGE, GUARDIAN, PENALTIES\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN, ACADEMY AWARDS, HOSTING, WRITERS SURPRISES\par \par \uc2 DOLPHINS, POD, MONTEREY BAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD FEBRUARY HEAT\par \par \uc2 SOUTHWEST, PLANE, NEAR MISS\par \par \uc2 ROMANCE, SCAM, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 MEDITERRANEAN DIET, HEALTH, CANCER\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL, PARKS, FEDERAL, JOB, CUTS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...Times Square. We're back now with our "GMA" cover story. Growing concerns at America's national parks after federal job cuts and how they could impact upcoming vacations, especially during the busy spring break and summer seasons. Erielle Reshef back with the story. Hey, Erielle.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW GOVERNMENT JOB CUTS COULD IMPACT NATIONAL PARKS\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey there, George. Hundreds of millions of tourists visit our national parks this year, and spring break is really one of the most popular times to take in their majestic beauty. But with more than 1,000 park employees terminated in a recent wave of government job cuts, experts say you may be in for a rockier vacation.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, as millions gear up to spend spring break at some of the nation's most treasured parks, from Yosemite to the Grand Canyon, a warning from park officials that the deep government job cuts could have a noticeable impact on your experience.\par \par \uc2 BETH PRATT (REGIONAL EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR FOR NWF)\par \par \uc2 The visitors are not going to be able to do the things they want. You know, if you want to bring your family to Yosemite and can't camp, or it takes four hours to get into the park, what kind of experience is that?\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nearly 1,000 park employees were laid off on Valentine's Day, and some ripple effects are already being felt, from longer lines, to cut hours, to closed visitor centers, and cancelled tours.\par \par \uc2 PARK GOER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We had an experience for ten options. We only did, like, four.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  says more than 325 million people visited these sites in 2023, up 4 percent from the year prior. But now officials say the staffing shortages will make keeping the areas pristine and visits running smoothly more challenging.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BARBARA BRUNO (SPRINGDALE UTAH)\par \par \uc2 In my mind, it's a crisis. If it's the employees that are gone, I'm not sure how the park could stay open.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some travelers and local businesses are bracing for a spring shakeup.\par \par \uc2 LIZ WOOLSEY (BRISTLECONE GENERAL STORE & STARGAZER INN)\par \par \uc2 Businesses that are here in Baker certainly rely on tourism and travelers. So we are unsure what that's gonna look like this season.\par \par \uc2 BEN SHREVES (NATIVE PLANT BIOLOGIST FOR NATIONAL PARK SERVICE)\par \par \uc2 I was on lunch on February 14th and got a call from my supervisor, and it said to be out and done by 5:00 pm. And so it was a mad dash.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ben Shreves is one of the park employees who recently lost his job.\par \par \uc2 BEN SHREVES (NATIVE PLANT BIOLOGIST FOR NATIONAL PARK SERVICE)\par \par \uc2 We were working overtime to provide the best experience. And they were already struggling to do that, and part of that was the lack of resources. And so now it's just, like, they have even less than that.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts say if you're planning to traverse the parks the spring to be patient and over prepared.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BRENGEL (SVP OF GOVT AFFAIRS AT NATL PARKS CONSERVATION ASSOC)\par \par \uc2 There's gonna be a big imbalance between the number of people who are going to the parks and the amount of staff that can help them.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the park service has about 20,000 employees. The Trump administration has rolled back some of those firings and says it will hire 3,000 seasonal workers. In the meantime, if you're planning a vacation to a national park, prepare for a slightly different experience. You want to make sure you have your maps. Make sure you have everything you need because there may not be a park ranger or someone to help you out when you're out there.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, big difference for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250320el2q0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2  TO LAY OFF 1,100 EMPLOYEES, SHRINK MENU WILL STARBUCKS RAISE COFFEE PRICES?\b0\par\par\uc2 736 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, MUSK, EMAIL, JEFFRIES, ALFORD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK DOUBLES DOWN ON FEDERAL WORKER ULTIMATUM AND CONFUSION\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, ZELENSKYY, TRUMP, MACRON, RUSSIA, DEAL, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP ON RUSSIA & ENDING WAR IN UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 DELTA AIRLINES, CHAPMAN, SMORE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DELTA FLIGHT MAKES EMERGENCY LANDING AFTER SMOKE FILLS CABIN\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VATICAN: POPE SHOWING "SLIGHT IMPROVEMENT", BUT STILL CRITICAL\par \par \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2 , LAYOFFS, MENU, PRICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2  TO LAY OFF 1,100 EMPLOYEES, SHRINK MENU\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  laying off more than a thousand employees and getting rid of more than a dozen drinks. Chief business correspondent Rebecca Jarvis is here with whether your favorite drink, is it going to still be on the menu?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure, a lot of people are wondering about this, Robin. Good morning. Nice to see all of you. It's 1,100 workers that are going to be laid off. People in corporate roles mostly as the company restructures and shrinks its menu.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The move is not going to affect baristas, those people in you caf\u233\'00\'E9s. And the changes are part of larger overhaul at the coffee giant. Remember, we shared this you when I sat down with the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  CEO in December to address slumping sales and customer complaints about everything from wait times to the cost of drinks. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is cutting about 30% of its menu.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2  TRIMS MENU\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, for those 13 drinks they are getting rid of by March 4th. They include the iced matcha lemonade, nine Frappuccino drinks, including caf\u233\'00\'E9 vanilla java chip, the caramel ribbon crunch creme, and the double chocolatey chip creme.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Say it ain't so.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yeah. And the white hot chocolate.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is also out with a list of substitution, so you can substitute a lot of your drinks. The drinks are pretty similar here. That's something to keep in mind. There's a lot of drinks on this list that you can find something else on the menu.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But they're very popular drinks as well.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They can be. But actually what the CEO told me is, a lot of these drinks only get ordered once or twice in their locations.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which is why they're doing away with them and they believe that you'll have something else there.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But they'll hear the one or twice that's getting it.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You'll hear it. They'll hear from them. And he actually also said he was aware of that and he hopes they'll find one of these substitutes.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do they get by cutting off the things off the menu?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) People who work at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  have a little more peace of mind because when you have so many things on the menu and you have to go make all of these different drinks, that's one of the issues, and the other is time.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure, sure.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You, as a customer, get your drink faster if they are making fewer of them. And they want to make us faster drinks They within 4 minutes and your coffee is within 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL STARBUCKS RAISE COFFEE PRICES?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That makes sense. But how about these coffee bean prices? So, does that mean the prices are going to, they're going to have to go up?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Go up at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . So, the CEO told me at least not until September. That's the end of their fiscal year. He said they're hitting the pause button on any kinds of price hikes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, less choices but better service, better quality?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is the goal.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250404el2p0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MUSK'S NEW ULTIMATUM DOUBLES DOWN ON FEDERAL WORKER ULTIMATUM AMID CONFUSION\b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, MUSK, EMAIL, JEFFRIES, ALFORD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK DOUBLES DOWN ON FEDERAL WORKER ULTIMATUM AND CONFUSION\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, ZELENSKYY, TRUMP, MACRON, RUSSIA, DEAL, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP ON RUSSIA & ENDING WAR IN UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 DELTA AIRLINES\uc2 , CHAPMAN, SMORE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DELTA FLIGHT MAKES EMERGENCY LANDING AFTER SMOKE FILLS CABIN\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VATICAN: POPE SHOWING "SLIGHT IMPROVEMENT", BUT STILL CRITICAL\par \par \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2 , LAYOFFS, MENU, PRICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2  TO LAY OFF 1,100 EMPLOYEES, SHRINK MENU\par \par \uc2 AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 "TUSH PUSH", NFL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NFL TO GIVE "TUSH PUSH" THE SHOVE?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 FRANKE, LAMBERT, "DEVIL IN THE FAMILY: THE FALL OF RUBY FRANKE"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUBY FRANKE'S ESTRANGED HUSBAND & OLDEST SON SPEAK OUT\par \par \uc2 FLACK, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 CURRO DURBANVILLE HIGH SCHOOL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOLD GRIFTERS SCAM WARNING, 8:12\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MUSK, ULTIMATUM, WORKERS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) First, a look at the top stories Breaking at 8, we start with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . He's doubling down on his ultimatum to federal workers to explain what they did last week or face termination. But top officials in the administration are resisting this. Mixed messages being sent to employees. Want to go back to our senior political correspondent Rachel Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning. Yes, mixed messages and whiplash for federal workers this morning. The deadline has now passed for employees to respond to that email with a list of their accomplishments. The President and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says that those who didn't respond could be fired or semi-fired. Then the President's own administration providing conflicting and contradiction responses, saying that it is voluntary to respond and that employees do not have to do it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AGENCIES REQUIRING A RESPONSE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Federal agencies also providing their own guidance, which is also conflicting. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the Justice Department saying employees do not have to respond. Other agencies directing their employees to respond. And this morning, federal employees are now waking up with new guidance from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , (inaudible) on his social media, saying that federal workers can expect to receive another email, saying failure to respond to the second email will result in termination. Meanwhile, this morning, sources are telling us that the acting commissioner of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is expected to step down in the middle of a very busy tax season, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, a lot going on, all confusing there, Rachel. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250404el2p00037\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MUSK DOUBLES DOWN ON FEDERAL WORKER ULTIMATUM AND CONFUSION MORE CUTS AMID TRUMP & MUSK'S GOVERNMENT OVERHAUL\b0\par\par\uc2 814 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, MUSK, EMAIL, JEFFRIES, ALFORD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK DOUBLES DOWN ON FEDERAL WORKER ULTIMATUM AND CONFUSION\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we begin with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  doubling down on his ultimatum to federal workers to explain what they did last week or face termination. Our senior political correspondent Rachel Scott has the very latest on the mixed messages sent to some employees. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Michael, good morning mixed messages to say the least. This is becoming whiplash for federal workers. The President and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  saying that those workers who did not respond to that email listing their accomplishments would be fired or semi-fired. But then the President's own administration directly contradicted him. And so, all of this really raising questions about which guidance federal employees should be listening to.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, federal workers in limbo after confusing and contradictory guidance about the ultimatum and midnight deadline from billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  saying, employees must respond to an email with a list of what they did last week or lose their job. The President called that idea genius, saying those who didn't answer semi-fired or fired.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 By asking the question, tell us what you did this week, what he's doing is saying, are you actually working? And then, if you don't answer, like, you are sort of semi-fired or fired.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But just as the President weighed in, his own administration gave conflicting information. The Office of Personnel Management told agencies the request was voluntary and that workers don't have to respond. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the Pentagon, and the Justice Department had already told workers to hold off. The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  sent competing messages.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEPT. OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES GUIDELINES\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At first, telling employees to respond to the email, then six hours later telling them not to respond. And finally, telling workers they can respond if they want to but warning they should assume that what you write will be read by maligned foreign actors and tailor your response accordingly. But at least six other agencies, including Treasury Department, telling employees it is required.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY (SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION)\par \par \uc2 I have no problem with the employees here saying what did he do last week. And if you can't come up with five things that you did, you know, maybe you shouldn't be employed here.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And overnight, Musk is not backing down, posting on social media that workers can expect that email to go out again. Writing, "Failure to respond a second time will result in termination."\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (DEM-NY) (HOUSE MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and Donald Trump, they're flooding the zone in part to create the stress, to create a sense of invincibility and inevitability.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Trump administration pushing forward with cuts to the federal workforce. The Department of Veterans Affairs letting go of more than 1,400 probationary employees overnight. Some of the efforts from Musk and the \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency\uc2  sparking backlash.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE CUTS AMID TRUMP & MUSK'S GOVERNMENT OVERHAUL\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Monitors at the \uc2 Department of Housing and Urban Development\uc2  briefly displayed a fake image of Trump and Musk with the words, "Long live the real king."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BACKLASH OVER MUSK'S INFLUENCE IN TRUMP ADMINISTRATION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) HUD calling it abuse of taxpayer resources, saying they plan to investigate the incident. In other parts of the country, voters showing up at town halls. This was a line at one Belton, Missouri, for Republican Congressman Mark Alford. Inside, tensions high.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MARK ALFORD (REP) (MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has contracts with the federal government. But he is also, I think doing a better job in weeding out waste...\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Conflict of interest.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Show me.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MARK ALFORD (REP) (MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 Better than a lot of people...\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Show me.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, adding to all of this confusion, the administration put out a new memo overnight saying it will be up to the agencies to decide the appropriate action for those federal workers who did not respond to that email. But you heard there from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  saying that they could expect to receive a second email soon. Meanwhile, George, there are reports this morning that the acting commissioner of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  could be stepping down as early as today right in the middle of a busy tax season, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And most of these issues going to the courts. Okay, Rachel, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250404el2p0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MUSK'S NEW ULTIMATUM TO FEDERAL WORKERS SPARKS FEAR & CONFUSION TOP TRUMP OFFICALS PUSH BACK ON MUSK'S NEW ULTIMATUM\b0\par\par\uc2 863 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHAOS & CONFUSION\par \par \uc2 MUSK, WORKERS, CONFUSION, VOTERS, REPUBLICANS, BONGINO\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Gio is going to explain all of it, all of it to us. The not crowded vacations aren't bad at all. But we're gonna begin with the standoff in Washington over \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  alternating, demanding that two million federal employees explain what they accomplished last week. Senior political correspondent Rachel Scott is at the White House. And, Rachel, Musk is also sparking fear that if employees don't respond, they're going to lose their jobs?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK'S NEW ULTIMATUM TO FEDERAL WORKERS SPARKS FEAR & CONFUSION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Michael, good morning. That's right. This is a new ultimatum from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  telling all federal employees they must respond to this email listing their accomplishments or resign. It is now prompting confusion and uncertainty, and this morning some federal agencies are pushing back.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, top Trump officials dismissing a new ultimatum from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . The billionaire posting on X this weekend that some two million federal employees would have to explain what they got done last week, writing "Failure to respond will be taken as a resignation." Musk insisting he's following instructions from President Trump who encouraged him to get more aggressive.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And Elon is doing a great job. He's doing a great job.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Within hours, emails going out reaching employees across the federal government from \uc2 NASA\uc2  to the State Department, even sitting federal judges. The subject line: What did you do last week? Demanding workers reply with five bullet points by Midnight tonight. But it made no mention of that threat from Musk that they could be at risk at losing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP TRUMP OFFICIALS PUSH BACK ON MUSK'S NEW ULTIMATUM\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Top officials scramble to provide guidance. The President's own cabinet pushing back. Newly confirmed \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director, Kash Patel, telling employees to pause on responding. And the Director of National Intelligence, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , writing, "Given the inherently sensitive and classified nature of our work, employees should not respond."\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The State Department and Pentagon telling employees to hold off too. Some Republicans on Capitol Hill expressing concern.\par \par \uc2 SEN JOHN CURTIS (REP) (UTAH)\par \par \uc2 If I can say one thing to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  it's like please put a dose of compassion in this. These are real people, these are real lives.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME REPUBLICANS EXPRESS CONCERN ABOUT MUSK'S TACTICS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Republican Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  writing on social media, "If \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  truly wants to understand what federal workers accomplished over the past week, he should get to know each department and agency and learn about the jobs he's trying to cut." Voters showing up at town halls in some parts of the country over the last week to confront GOP lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid, and I would really like to know what you are going to do to protect those programs for your constituents?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Over the weekend, the President posting this meme on social media mocking concerns. The image with a list of what got done last week followed by bullet points including, cried about Trump, cried about Elon.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The largest federal workers union blasting this latest move as unlawful and calling it an attempt to "Bully the hardworking federal employees that serve our country." For weeks Musk has boasted about slashing the federal workforce, appearing at a conservative conference with a chainsaw in hand.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (DEPARTMENT OF EFFICIENCY)\par \par \uc2 The chainsaw for bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats pouncing.\par \par \uc2 SEN CHRIS VAN HOLLEN (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 There is no article for in the constitution that gives \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that authority.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So just moments ago, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  posted on social media saying that those who do not take this email seriously will for be furthering their careers elsewhere. But all of this really raising questions here about who is calling the shots. Is it \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  sending out these threats on social media or is it these federal agencies, some of which that are now telling their employees to hold off on responding to that email, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nothing but confusion right there. Meantime, Rachel, new Deputy Director designee for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Dan Bongino, right wing podcaster election denier.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONSERVATIVE MEDIA PERSONALITY NAMED DEPUTY FBI DIRECTOR\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep, he has pushed false claims about the 2020 election, conspiracy theories about January 6th. And the President announcing that Dan Bongino will now serve as second command in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . This really sends a strong message and a signal about the way that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  will now be reshaped. Kash Patel, Dan Bongino, two of the harshest critics, they will be leading the department. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250402el2o00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GROWING CONFUSION OVER MUSK ORDER FEDERAL WORKERS UNDER DEADLINE TO JUSTIFY JOBS\b0\par\par\uc2 856 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MUSK, LAYOFFS, DUFFY, KELLEY\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. We begin tonight with this midnight deadline. Millions of federal workers have just hours left to answer an email.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  telling the federal workers to list five things you did in the last week at work or risk losing your job.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING CONFUSION OVER MUSK ORDER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, confusion over whether this is mandatory, but \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  on X saying, failure to do so, quote, "will be taken as a resignation." And today, President Trump calling the proposal ingenious, saying, "If people don't respond, they would be semi-fired or fired." Mary Bruce is leading us off.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL WORKERS UNDER DEADLINE TO JUSTIFY JOBS\par \par \uc2 CONGRESSMAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 (inaudible) I'll come back to you, okay? I promise.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight growing backlash after \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  gave more than 2 million federal workers until midnight tonight to send an email listing what they've done in the last week or lose their jobs. In Belton, Missouri, long lines of people waiting to get into a town meeting for Republican Congressman Mark Alford.\par \par \uc2 CONGRESSMAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has contracts with the federal government. But he is also, I think, doing an effective job...\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Conflict of interest.\par \par \uc2 CONGRESSMAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I know a lot of people who don't want to see waste, abuse and fraud, weed it out. Some of them are here today to prove that.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I don't want him in charge of it.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Over the weekend, Musk posting on X, "All federal employees will shortly receive an email requesting to understand what they got done last week. Failure to respond will be taken as a resignation." Soon after, the Office of Personnel Management emailing workers, "Please reply to this email with approximately five bullets of what you accomplished last week. Deadline is this Monday at 11:59 PM."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP: IF NO RESPONSE, WORKERS "SEMI-FIRE" OR "FIRED"\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Then the confusion began. Newly installed \uc2 FBI\uc2  director Kash Patel quickly telling his team, "The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , through the office of director, is in charge of all of our review processes. For now, please pause any responses." At least three agencies, including the Justice Department, the State Department, and the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  also instructing their employees not to respond to Musk's directive. But other agencies sending the opposite message. The Treasury Department telling its employees, "You are required to respond before the deadline." Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy sending similar signals.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY (SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION)\par \par \uc2 I have no problem with the employees here saying, what did you do last week? And if you can't come up with five things that you did, you know, maybe you shouldn't be employed here.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  sending competing messages to its own workers. First, telling them to respond to the email, then, six hours later, telling them not to respond. And tonight, telling workers they can respond if they want to, but instructing assume what you write will be read by maligned foreign actors and tailor your response accordingly. Still, other agencies insisting the email was optional. The Department of Agriculture telling workers, "There is no penalty for not responding." Today, in the Oval Office, the President himself saying, if you don't respond, you're semi-fired or fired.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We have people that don't show up to work and nobody even knows if they work for the government. So, by asking the question, tell us what you did this week, what he's doing is saying, are you actually working? And then, if you don't answer, like, you're sort of semi-fired or you're fired.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President calling Musk's idea ingenious. But tonight, the head of the largest union of federal employees says the workforce is on edge and very frustrated, adding the administration's goal is clear.\par \par \uc2 EVERETT KELLEY\uc2  (PRESIDENT AMERICAN FEDERATION OF GOVERNMENT EMPLOYEES)\par \par \uc2 They want to confuse the workforce. They want to put fear in the minds of the workforce that they'll just quit.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now, sources tell us the Office of Personnel Management has told agencies this directive is voluntary, but we all heard what the President said, that if you don't respond, you will be fired or semi-fired. The bottom line here, there is still a lot of confusion around all of this tonight. And in the meantime, the President is announcing the far-right podcaster Dan Bongino to be second in command at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , a loyal Trump supporter who stoked conspiracy theories about January 6th and has been critical of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Mary Bruce leading us off at the White House. Mary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250227el2o0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FEAR & CONFUSION AMONGST FEDERAL WORKERS AFTER MUSK'S ULTIMATUM PRESIDENT TRUMP TOUTS MASS LAYOFFS OF FEDERAL WORKERS\b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PONTIFF IN CRITICAL CONDITION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRAYERS FOR POPE FRANCIS\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VATICAN: POPE FRANCIS IN CRITICAL CONDITION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MUSK, EMAIL, LAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEAR & CONFUSION AMONGST FEDERAL WORKERS AFTER MUSK'S ULTIMATUM\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the new ultimatum to federal workers from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , federal employees to detail their accomplishments last week. And it comes with a warning if the email is ignored. ABC's senior White House correspondent Selina Wang is in Washington tracking this. Good morning, Selina.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Gio. And that ultimatum from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , it is making federal workers even more anxious and confused. Musk is taking his effort to slash the size of the federal government to a whole new level by posting that federal workers need to explain what they've accomplished in the last week or get fired.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, chaos and confusion across the federal government. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  now downplaying the ultimatum he issued to federal workers. Explain what you've done or resign. Musk initially posting on X, "All federal employees will shortly receive an email requesting to understand what they got done last week. Failure to respond will be taken as a resignation." Those emails already going out. Sources tell ABC, they ask employees to list five bullet points of what they've accomplished in the last week. The deadline to respond is tomorrow night. Several hours later, Musk posting, "The bar is very low here. An email with some bullet points that make any sense at all is acceptable."\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sources tell ABC top officials at the White House were caught off guard. And many federal agencies, from the State Department to \uc2 NASA\uc2 , are urging employees to hold off responding. Newly installed \uc2 FBI\uc2  director Kash Patel telling employees to pause on responding to the email. When pressed, a White House official tells ABC, it's up to each employee's manager to determine if the answers are satisfactory. And overnight, President Trump praising the latest moves from Musk.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP TOUTS MASS LAYOFFS OF FEDERAL WORKERS\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Elon is doing a great job. He's doing a great job.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Taking a victory lap at the Conservative Political Action Conference, touting his mass layoffs of federal workers.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I've ended all of the so-called diversity, equity, and inclusion programs across the entire federal government and the private sector, and notified every single government DEI officer that their job has been deleted. They're gone. They're fired. You're fired. Get out.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the fallout continues after Trump fired the nation's top military leadership, ousting general CQ Brown as chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff alongside several other senior leaders.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FALLOUT FROM FIRINGS OF TOP MILITARY OFFICIALS\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Brown, a four-star fighter pilot, has come under fire as Trump aims to rid the military of leaders who support diversity, equity, and inclusion efforts as Brown and some of the others who were fired has done. Trump nominating as his replacement, Dan Caine, a retired three-star Air Force general, who has never served in a four-star position. Senator \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2 , the top Democrat on the Armed Services Committee, slamming the move as part of a premeditated campaign by President Trump and Secretary Hegseth to purge talented officers for politically charged reasons, which would undermine the professionalism of our military and send a chilling message through the ranks.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, guys, it's been a stressful time to be a federal worker to say the least, and these mass firings are just beginning. Starting next week, is Pentagon is expected to lay off 4,500 civilian employees. And that is just the first wave of firings in what could total tens of thousands of layoffs. Rachel?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the White House has not said how long these firings will last or just how many people are going to be impacted. Selina Wang tracking it all for us. Selina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250305el2n00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FORMER FEDERAL WORKERS SPEAK OUT AFTER ABRUPT FIRINGS WH, DOGE TAKE RAPID ACTION AIMED AT SHRINKING GOVERNMENT\b0\par\par\uc2 1,719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2025 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PENTAGON, DEI, FIRINGS, BROWN, FRANCHETTI, SLIFE, HEGSETH, CAINE, CASEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S 1ST MONTH\par \par \uc2 WAR, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, TRUMP, BIDEN, VANCE, HEGSETH, RUBIO, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 REED, TRUMP, FIRINGS, BROWN, CAINE, UKRAINE, INVASION, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 LAWLER, DOGE, DEI, EMPLOYEES, MILITARY, TRUMP, ZELENSKYY, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK, GOVT, FIRINGS, BRANDT, DECIUS, PLACE, DOGE, EMAIL, MUSK, \uc2 USAID\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 GEORGIA VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We are all freaking pissed off about this. You're going to hear it and feel it.\par \par \uc2 REP. RICH MCCORMICK (REP) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 But by and large, the President has great purview over where a lot of this money goes. Now, some of this is going to be litigated.\par \par \uc2 GEORGIA VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I would like to know, rather the people would like to know what you, Congressman, and your fellow congressmen are going to go to rein in the megalomaniac in the White House.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Constituents in Georgia grilling Republican Congressman Rick McCormick Thursday night over President Trump, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , and the Department of Government Efficiency's slashing of the federal workforce. The Trump administration has abruptly fired thousands of workers across more than a dozen agencies in the last few weeks. We sat down with three of them this week.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER FEDERAL WORKERS SPEAK OUT AFTER ABRUPT FIRINGS\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BRANDT (FORMER ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION AGENCY EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I'm Elizabeth Brandt and I worked on climate adaptation at the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARIE DECIUS (FORMER HEALTH RESOURCES & SERVICES ADMIN. EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I'm Marie Decius. I was a management analyst as a liaison between Division of Business Operations and the NPDB, the National Practitioner Data Bank.\par \par \uc2 EMMA PLACE (FORMER FEDERAL AVIATION ADMIN. EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 And my name is Emma, and I was an economist at the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Twenty-seven-year-old Emma Place had long dreamed of working for the \uc2 FAA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMMA PLACE (FORMER FEDERAL AVIATION ADMIN. EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 Ever since I was a child, I loved the idea of airports and aviation. And I was in graduate school for public policy and I concentrated in transportation policy, and so, the only place I looked at was the \uc2 FAA\uc2 . So, it was truly a dream to get it and I was really hoping to stay there for the rest of my career.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATEMENT FROM SECRETARY DUFFY\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Emma was one of several hundred probationary workers suddenly fired from the agency.\par \par \uc2 EMMA PLACE (FORMER FEDERAL AVIATION ADMIN. EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I never actually got my termination email. My system access was shut off before I got it. So, all weekend, you know, I didn't have the information I needed to apply for unemployment insurance benefits.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Marie Decius is a former Army combat medic and single mother of three. She spent her savings to move her family to DC from Florida in October to start her job at the \uc2 Health Resources and Services Administration\uc2 . Her division served as a watchdog that tracks medical malpractice.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You liked your job?\par \par \uc2 MARIE DECIUS (FORMER HEALTH RESOURCES & SERVICES ADMIN. EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I love my job. I love getting up every day and knowing I will do something, just something that makes a difference in somebody's life.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Elizabeth Brandt, a mother of two, was inspired to work for the \uc2 EPA\uc2  after growing up near a toxic waste site in Washington state, that the agency helped clean up.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BRANDT (FORMER ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION AGENCY EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 That was important to me. It was important to me to be able to give back in this way, to an agency that's meant so much for the health of my family.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) All three let go with a similar email, blaming their firings on inadequate performance.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BRANDT (FORMER ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION AGENCY EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 That is a lie. I have an excellent performance review. And that hurts. You know, I've never been treated that way in, in any job, and I wouldn't want that for any of my fellow Americans.\par \par \uc2 EMMA PLACE (FORMER FEDERAL AVIATION ADMIN. EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I wasn't there long enough to have an official performance review.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A frustrating experience that they wish had been handled differently.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you believe fundamentally that the Federal - Government could be reduced, could be cut, needed to be in some ways?\par \par \uc2 EMMA PLACE (FORMER FEDERAL AVIATION ADMIN. EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I think it's fair to look into that. But if you do decide to look into that, I don't think arbitrary firings is the way to do that.\par \par \uc2 MARIE DECIUS (FORMER HEALTH RESOURCES & SERVICES ADMIN. EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's okay to make changes. It's the way the changes are being made.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is it painful for you to hear or see on X or \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BRANDT (FORMER ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION AGENCY EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...what people are saying?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BRANDT (FORMER ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION AGENCY EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 You know, I've had a lot of jobs and I've certainly never lost a job with this kind of notice, right? I mean, I've even been an office temp and had more notice than this. I serve at the will of the Government. If the, if the people don't want me to be there, that's okay. I understand that. It should be done with more dignity than this. That's all I ask for.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WH, DOGE TAKE RAPID ACTION AIMED AT SHRINKING GOVERNMENT\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And for more on the legal fallout from the Trump administration's federal cutbacks, I'm joined by former New Jersey Governor and US Attorney \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . Good morning, Chris. I, I want to start with what \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  did on Saturday, which is order all federal employees to list their accomplishments or basically be fired. Can he do that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FED. EMPLOYEES GET EMAIL DEMANDING LIST OF WORK ACCOMPLISHMENTS\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, no, he can't. Let's look at it a few ways, Martha. First is from a legal perspective. You have a situation where it is a complete overstep for him to be asking people, what did you do in the last week, and then use that as a basis to evaluate whether someone should be retained or fired. You know, as with everybody's employment, things vary from week to week, and I think that if that went to court with an employee who was fired, if Mr. Musk and his minions didn't find what they did in the last week acceptable, there would be very little, if any, legal basis for them to be able to prevail, meaning the Government.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERAL AGENCIES TELL WORKERS TO HOLD OFF, NOT ANSWER OPM EMAIL\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But secondly, as a former governor, too, from a management perspective, you can see what a clown car this is right now. Because you've got for instance, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel, appropriately emailing his people in response saying, don't answer it. Don't answer the questionnaire. Any review process will be done internally by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Same thing from the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2 . Same thing from the acting US Attorney in the District of Columbia.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you know, you have people, you know, doing this, you know? Pointing in each direction. You do this, you do that. It shows that again, this type of idea of Musk coming in there with the chainsaw that he was famously brandishing at a political event in Washington, DC this week, it's a lot more complicated than that. Running government is a lot more complicated than that, and hopefully they get their act together at some point.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but Chris, the Trump administration has had some wins and in these legal battles. In, in fact, a federal judge agreed that they can now put thousands of \uc2 USAID\uc2  employees on leave. So any surprise there? And, and take a look at all the wins they have had.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE CLEARS WAY FOR ADMIN. TO PUT \uc2 USAID\uc2  WORKERS ON LEAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, Martha, look, I, I think that the, if you follow an organized process, the executive will have a lot of authority to be able to lay people off, reorder priorities within the Government as a whole, and within particular departments as well at \uc2 USAID\uc2  as an agency, but working very closely with the State Department. So, it's not they can't do it. It's just you can't do it in this haphazard way, where you have one guy, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and his DOGE area, which isn't really even a department of the Government, sending emails out, and then you have the people the President has appointed, and in some instances like Kash Patel's instance are, are Senate-confirmed people saying, no, disregard that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EFFORTS TO RESHAPE GOVERNMENT WEATHER LEGAL CHALLENGES\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's where the person at the top has to give appropriate direction, and that has not come from the President of the United States at this point. He's giving different opinions to different folks, giving different direction. It leads to this type of thing, and that's where they're going to get in trouble in court if people sue because there's no process for these folks to be dealt with fairly and appropriately like any employee in the public sector or the private sector needs to be dealt with.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUESTIONS SWIRL ABOUT DOGE, MUSK'S ROLE IN FEDERAL GOVT.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Okay, Chris. We have lots more to discuss. We'll stay on this in the weeks to come. Thanks for joining us. The Roundtable is up next. We're back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The Roundtable is ahead with analysis of President Trump's first month in office. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020250304el2n00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FEDERAL EMPLOYEES TOLD TO JUSTIFY WORK MUSK GIVES 48 HOURS TO DOCUMENT WORK OR RESIGN\b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 FLIGHT, DIVERTED, SECURITY, FIGHTER JETS, PASSENGERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECURITY THREAT ONBOARD PROMPTS JET ESCORT\par \par \uc2 EMPLOYEES, MUSK, RESIGN, BACKLASH, CURTIS, \uc2 USAID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL EMPLOYEES TOLD TO JUSTIFY WORK\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of high anxiety in the skies lately. Morgan, thank you. Now to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's new attempt to slash the federal government. Federal workers were told via email to list their accomplishments or face termination. But some unions and agencies, including the new \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel, have told workers to pause on the order.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And late word tonight that the Trump administration is firing 2,000 \uc2 USAID\uc2  workers and placing thousands of others on leave. Here's ABC's senior White House correspondent Selina Wang.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL EMPLOYEES TOLD TO JUSTIFY WORK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK GIVE 48 HOURS TO DOCUMENT WORK OR RESIGN\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, President Trump's cabinet defying \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's order to federal workers, telling their agency employees to hold off on responding to an email from the office of personal manager asking for five bullet points detailing their previous week's accomplishments. The pushback coming after Musk posted an ultimatum on social media demanding workers justify their jobs before Midnight Monday or face termination.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL EMPLOYEES TOLD TO JUSTIFY WORK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MULTIPLE AGENCIES ADVISE MEMBERS NOT TO RESPOND\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Musk writing on X, "Failure to respond will be taken as a resignation." But several agencies telling workers to wait. Newly installed \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel ordering his workforce to pause any responses. Senior leaders at the State Department saying no employees obligated to report their activities outside of their department chain of command. And tonight, the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  telling employees that the department will respond on their behalf when and if required.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Acting US Attorney in DC Ed Martin, was one of the few senior officials to encourage his employees to comply, calling it a good opportunity to highlight the importance of our work. The largest federal workers union calling the email unlawful and an attempt to bully hard-working Americans.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sources tell ABC that even though Trump publicly urged Musk to get more aggressive on Saturday, top White House officials were caught off guard. The White House refusing to comment publicly. Even some Republican lawmakers questioning Musk's tactics.\par \par \uc2 SEN JOHN CURTIS (REP) (UTAH)\par \par \uc2 If I could say one thing to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , it's like please put a dose of compassion in this. These are real people. These are real lives.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Linsey, more breaking news tonight. \uc2 USAID\uc2  says it's firing 2,000 workers and putting all but a few remaining staffers on leave around the world. This comes after President Trump and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  have vowed to dismantle the agency that provides humanitarian aid around the world. They claim it's wasteful. Linsey.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020250227el2n00003\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb